window.CHEMISTRY_CURRICULUM_11 = [{"id":"basicconcepts11","index":0,"name":"Some Basic Concepts of Chemistry","subtitle":"Chemistry Chapter 1","emoji":"⚗️","palette":{"from":"#60a5fa","to":"#1e3a8a"},"lessons":[{"id":"bc1","title":"The Alchemist's Gate","icon":"🧪","kind":"lesson","story":"You step through a shimmering gate inscribed with the name 'Rasayan Shastra' and find yourself in an ancient Indian workshop full of glazed pottery and mercury vials. An old alchemist hands you a blank notebook: 'Before you may wield chemistry, you must know why it matters, and what matter itself is made of.'","teach":[{"title":"Chemistry is central to science","body":"Chemistry studies the preparation, properties, structure and reactions of material substances. It touches diverse areas like weather, the brain, computers, fertilisers, drugs, polymers, and alloys, and contributes heavily to national economy and quality of life.","visual":"⚗️"},{"title":"Chemistry saves and sustains life","body":"Chemistry provides methods to isolate and synthesise life-saving drugs such as cisplatin and taxol (used in cancer therapy) and AZT (used for AIDS patients). It also helped design safer refrigerants to replace ozone-depleting CFCs.","visual":"💊"},{"title":"India's ancient chemistry","body":"Ancient India had deep chemical knowledge long before modern science: Harappans made glazed pottery and glass, Charaka Samhita describes acids and metal oxides, and Acharya Kanad proposed an atomic theory ('Paramanu') around 2500 years before John Dalton.","visual":"🏺"},{"title":"Matter: anything with mass that occupies space","body":"Matter can exist in three physical states: solid (definite shape and volume), liquid (definite volume, no definite shape), and gas (neither definite shape nor volume). These states are interconvertible by changing temperature and pressure.","visual":"🧊"}],"questions":[{"kind":"mcq","prompt":"Which drug mentioned in the text is used for helping AIDS patients?","options":["AZT (Azidothymidine)","Cisplatin","Taxol","Insulin"],"answer":0,"explain":"The text names AZT (Azidothymidine) as the drug used for helping AIDS patients."},{"kind":"mcq","prompt":"Who first proposed the concept of indivisible particles called 'Paramanu' in ancient India?","options":["Acharya Kanad","Nagarjuna","Chakrapani","Charaka"],"answer":0,"explain":"Acharya Kanad (born 600 BCE), originally named Kashyap, proposed the theory of 'Paramanu' about 2500 years before Dalton."},{"kind":"mcq","prompt":"In which state of matter are particles far apart with fast, easy movement and neither definite shape nor definite volume?","options":["Gas","Solid","Liquid","Plasma"],"answer":0,"explain":"Gases have particles far apart with easy, fast movement, so they have neither definite shape nor definite volume."},{"kind":"mcq","prompt":"Which safer alternative did chemistry help develop to replace ozone-depleting CFCs (chlorofluorocarbons)?","options":["Environmentally safer refrigerants","Leaded petrol","Asbestos insulation","DDT pesticide"],"answer":0,"explain":"The text states that safer alternatives to environmentally hazardous CFC refrigerants have been successfully synthesised."}]},{"id":"bc2","title":"The Sorting Chamber","icon":"🧫","kind":"lesson","story":"A door labelled 'Pure vs Mixed' seals behind you. Piles of samples lie scattered: sugar solution, salt-and-sand, gold dust, glucose crystals. A voice echoes: 'Sort truly, traveller, or the chamber will not open.'","teach":[{"title":"Mixtures vs pure substances","body":"At the bulk level, matter is classified as a mixture or a pure substance. A pure substance has all constituent particles the same in chemical nature; a mixture contains particles of two or more pure substances in variable ratio.","visual":"⚖️"},{"title":"Homogeneous vs heterogeneous mixtures","body":"In a homogeneous mixture (like sugar solution or air), components mix completely and composition is uniform throughout. In a heterogeneous mixture (like salt and sugar, or grains with stone pieces), the composition is not uniform and components may be visible.","visual":"🌫️"},{"title":"Elements and compounds","body":"Pure substances split into elements (only one type of atom, e.g. sodium, copper, oxygen) and compounds (atoms of different elements combined in a fixed ratio, e.g. water H2O, carbon dioxide CO2). Compound constituents can only be separated by chemical methods, unlike mixtures which separate by physical methods like filtration, distillation, or crystallisation.","visual":"🔗"}],"questions":[{"kind":"mcq","prompt":"A water molecule (H2O) is an example of which class of pure substance?","options":["Compound","Element","Homogeneous mixture","Heterogeneous mixture"],"answer":0,"explain":"Water is a compound: two hydrogen atoms combine with one oxygen atom in a fixed, definite ratio."},{"kind":"mcq","prompt":"Which of these is described in the text as a homogeneous mixture?","options":["Sugar solution in water","Salt and sugar mixed dry","Grains and pulses with dirt","Iron filings and sulphur powder"],"answer":0,"explain":"Sugar solution and air are given as examples of homogeneous mixtures where composition is uniform throughout."},{"kind":"mcq","prompt":"Which physical method is NOT used to separate the components of a mixture, according to the text?","options":["Chemical reaction","Filtration","Distillation","Crystallisation"],"answer":0,"explain":"Mixture components are separated by physical methods (hand-picking, filtration, crystallisation, distillation); compounds require chemical methods."},{"kind":"mcq","prompt":"Why can sodium and copper be called elements?","options":["Their constituent particles are all one type of atom","They are liquids at room temperature","They contain two different atoms combined","They can be separated by filtration"],"answer":0,"explain":"Particles of an element consist of only one type of atom; sodium and copper's atoms are all of one type."}]},{"id":"bc3","title":"The Measurer's Vault","icon":"📏","kind":"lesson","story":"Inside a vault of gleaming balances and graduated cylinders, an inscription reads: 'A number without a unit is a lie.' Ancient scales for mass, volume, and temperature line the walls, each demanding precise readings.","teach":[{"title":"Physical vs chemical properties","body":"Physical properties (colour, odour, melting point, boiling point, density) can be measured without changing a substance's identity. Chemical properties (combustibility, reactivity with acids/bases) require a chemical change to observe.","visual":"🔥"},{"title":"The SI system has seven base units","body":"The International System of Units (SI) has seven base units for length (metre, m), mass (kilogram, kg), time (second, s), electric current (ampere, A), thermodynamic temperature (kelvin, K), amount of substance (mole, mol), and luminous intensity (candela, cd).","visual":"📐"},{"title":"Mass, volume, and density","body":"Mass is the amount of matter (constant everywhere) while weight is the force of gravity on it (varies with location). Volume is space occupied, with SI unit m3 (labs use cm3 or dm3, plus the non-SI litre: 1 L = 1000 mL = 1000 cm3 = 1 dm3). Density = Mass/Volume, with SI unit kg m-3 (labs use g cm-3).","visual":"⚖️"},{"title":"Temperature scales","body":"Temperature is measured in Celsius (°C), Fahrenheit (°F), or Kelvin (K, the SI unit). The relations are F = (9/5)C + 32 and K = °C + 273.15. Unlike Celsius, Kelvin has no negative values.","visual":"🌡️"}],"questions":[{"kind":"mcq","prompt":"What is the SI base unit of amount of substance?","options":["mole (mol)","kilogram (kg)","candela (cd)","ampere (A)"],"answer":0,"explain":"The mole (symbol mol) is the SI base unit for the amount of substance."},{"kind":"mcq","prompt":"Why does an object's weight change from place to place while its mass stays constant?","options":["Weight is the force of gravity on the object, which varies by location","Mass depends on temperature","Weight is measured only in kilograms","Mass is not a physical property"],"answer":0,"explain":"The text states weight is the force exerted by gravity on an object, and gravity varies from place to place, while mass (amount of matter) is constant."},{"kind":"numeric","prompt":"Using K = °C + 273.15, what is 0°C in Kelvin?","answer":273.15,"explain":"K = 0 + 273.15 = 273.15 K, the freezing point of water on the Kelvin scale."},{"kind":"numeric","prompt":"How many mL are there in 1 litre?","answer":1000,"explain":"The text states 1 L = 1000 mL."}]},{"id":"bc4","title":"Digits of Uncertainty","icon":"🔢","kind":"lesson","story":"A puzzle-door demands you tame numbers as vast as 602,200,000,000,000,000,000,000 and as tiny as 0.00000000000000000000000166. The only way through is scientific notation and the discipline of significant figures.","teach":[{"title":"Scientific notation","body":"Any number can be written as N x 10^n, where N (the digit term) is between 1.000 and 9.999, and n is a positive or negative exponent. For example, 232.508 = 2.32508 x 10^2, and 0.00016 = 1.6 x 10^-4.","visual":"🧮"},{"title":"Rules for significant figures","body":"All non-zero digits are significant. Zeros before the first non-zero digit are not significant (0.0052 has 2 sig figs). Zeros between non-zero digits are significant (2.005 has 4 sig figs). Trailing zeros after a decimal point are significant (0.200 g has 3 sig figs), but trailing zeros with no decimal point are not (100 has 1 sig fig, but 100. has 3).","visual":"📊"},{"title":"Precision vs accuracy","body":"Precision is how close repeated measurements are to each other; accuracy is how close a value is to the true value. Student C's readings of 2.01 g and 1.99 g (true value 2.00 g) are both precise and accurate, while Student A's 1.95 g and 1.93 g are precise but not accurate.","visual":"🎯"},{"title":"Rounding off numbers","body":"If the digit to be removed is more than 5, round up (1.386 -> 1.39). If less than 5, round down (4.334 -> 4.33). If exactly 5, round to the nearest even digit (6.35 -> 6.4, but 6.25 -> 6.2).","visual":"➗"}],"questions":[{"kind":"numeric","prompt":"How many significant figures are in the number 0.0052?","answer":2,"explain":"Zeros preceding the first non-zero digit are not significant, so 0.0052 has 2 significant figures (5 and 2)."},{"kind":"numeric","prompt":"How many significant figures are in the number 2.005?","answer":4,"explain":"Zeros between two non-zero digits are significant, so 2.005 has 4 significant figures."},{"kind":"mcq","prompt":"Student A measures a true 2.00 g value as 1.95 g and 1.93 g. What best describes these results?","options":["Precise but not accurate","Accurate but not precise","Both precise and accurate","Neither precise nor accurate"],"answer":0,"explain":"The two values are close to each other (precise) but not close to the true value of 2.00 g (not accurate)."},{"kind":"numeric","prompt":"Round 6.35 to one decimal place using the 'round to even' rule for a trailing 5.","answer":6.4,"explain":"Since the digit before 5 (i.e. 3) is odd, it is increased by one, giving 6.4."},{"kind":"numeric","prompt":"Add 6.65 x 10^4 and 8.95 x 10^3 (express your answer as a plain number, e.g. 75450).","answer":75450,"explain":"Converting to the same exponent: (6.65 + 0.895) x 10^4 = 7.545 x 10^4 = 75450."}]},{"id":"bc5","title":"The Unit Converter's Bridge","icon":"🌉","kind":"lesson","story":"A rope bridge only holds if every plank's units cancel correctly. The bridge-keeper mutters about 'unit factors' -- multiply by 1, and nothing changes but the label.","teach":[{"title":"The factor label method","body":"Dimensional analysis (factor label / unit factor method) converts units by multiplying with a 'unit factor' -- a ratio equal to 1, like (2.54 cm / 1 in), since 1 in = 2.54 cm. Multiplying a quantity by this ratio changes its units without changing its value.","visual":"🔁"},{"title":"Worked example: inches to cm","body":"A 3 inch piece of metal converts to cm as: 3 in x (2.54 cm / 1 in) = 3 x 2.54 cm = 7.62 cm. The chosen unit factor's numerator must carry the units wanted in the final answer.","visual":"📏"},{"title":"Chaining unit factors","body":"Multiple unit factors can be chained in one step. To convert 2 days to seconds: 2 day x (24 h / 1 day) x (60 min / 1 h) x (60 s / 1 min) = 2 x 24 x 60 x 60 s = 172800 s.","visual":"⛓️"}],"questions":[{"kind":"numeric","prompt":"Convert 3 inches to cm using 1 in = 2.54 cm.","answer":7.62,"explain":"3 in x 2.54 cm/in = 7.62 cm."},{"kind":"numeric","prompt":"How many seconds are there in 2 days?","answer":172800,"explain":"2 days x 24 h/day x 60 min/h x 60 s/min = 172800 s."},{"kind":"mcq","prompt":"In the factor label method, which unit factor should you multiply by to get the desired final units?","options":["The one whose numerator carries the units required in the result","Any unit factor, since they are all equal to 1","The one with the smallest numerical value","The one with units in the denominator matching the desired result"],"answer":0,"explain":"The text notes the numerator should have that part which is required in the desired result."},{"kind":"numeric","prompt":"A jug contains 2 L of milk (1 L = 1000 cm3, 1 m = 100 cm). What is its volume in m3?","answer":0.002,"explain":"2 L = 2000 cm3; using (1 m / 100 cm)^3 = 1 m3 / 10^6 cm3, we get 2000 / 10^6 = 0.002 m3."}]},{"id":"bc6","title":"The Five Ancient Laws","icon":"⚖️","kind":"lesson","story":"Five stone tablets guard the inner sanctum, each carved with a law by a different scientist -- Lavoisier, Proust, Dalton, Gay Lussac, Avogadro. Only by understanding all five can you awaken Dalton's atomic theory that binds them together.","teach":[{"title":"Law of Conservation of Mass (Lavoisier, 1789)","body":"Antoine Lavoisier's careful combustion experiments showed there is no net change in mass during any physical or chemical change -- matter can neither be created nor destroyed.","visual":"⚗️"},{"title":"Law of Definite Proportions (Proust)","body":"Joseph Proust showed that a given compound always contains exactly the same proportion of elements by mass, regardless of source. His cupric carbonate samples (natural and synthetic) both had 51.35% copper, 9.74% carbon, and 38.91% oxygen.","visual":"🔬"},{"title":"Law of Multiple Proportions (Dalton, 1803)","body":"If two elements form more than one compound, the masses of one element that combine with a fixed mass of the other are in a ratio of small whole numbers. Hydrogen (2g) combines with 16g oxygen to form water, or 32g oxygen to form hydrogen peroxide -- the oxygen masses are in ratio 16:32 = 1:2.","visual":"🔢"},{"title":"Gay Lussac's Law & Avogadro's Law","body":"Gay Lussac (1808) found gases combine in simple volume ratios at the same temperature and pressure -- 100 mL hydrogen + 50 mL oxygen give 100 mL water vapour (ratio 2:1). Avogadro (1811) proposed equal volumes of gases at the same temperature and pressure contain equal numbers of molecules, which explained Gay Lussac's law by recognising hydrogen and oxygen as diatomic.","visual":"🎈"},{"title":"Dalton's Atomic Theory (1808)","body":"Dalton proposed: (1) matter consists of indivisible atoms; (2) all atoms of a given element are identical, including in mass, while atoms of different elements differ; (3) compounds form when atoms of different elements combine in a fixed ratio; (4) chemical reactions only reorganise atoms -- they are neither created nor destroyed. It explained the laws of chemical combination but not the laws of gaseous volumes.","visual":"⚛️"}],"questions":[{"kind":"mcq","prompt":"Which law states that matter can neither be created nor destroyed in a physical or chemical change?","options":["Law of Conservation of Mass","Law of Definite Proportions","Law of Multiple Proportions","Avogadro's Law"],"answer":0,"explain":"Lavoisier's Law of Conservation of Mass states there is no net change in mass during any physical or chemical process."},{"kind":"mcq","prompt":"Hydrogen forms water (2g H : 16g O) and hydrogen peroxide (2g H : 32g O). This 16:32 (1:2) ratio illustrates which law?","options":["Law of Multiple Proportions","Law of Definite Proportions","Law of Conservation of Mass","Gay Lussac's Law"],"answer":0,"explain":"When one element forms more than one compound with a fixed mass of another, the masses are in a small whole number ratio -- the Law of Multiple Proportions."},{"kind":"mcq","prompt":"Proust found both natural and synthetic cupric carbonate had 51.35% copper, 9.74% carbon and 38.91% oxygen. This supports which law?","options":["Law of Definite Proportions","Law of Multiple Proportions","Gay Lussac's Law of Gaseous Volumes","Avogadro's Law"],"answer":0,"explain":"A compound always contains the same elements in the same proportion by mass, regardless of source -- the Law of Definite Proportions."},{"kind":"mcq","prompt":"According to Dalton's Atomic Theory, what happens to atoms during a chemical reaction?","options":["They are reorganised, but neither created nor destroyed","New atoms are created from energy","Atoms are destroyed and replaced","Atoms split into smaller indivisible units"],"answer":0,"explain":"Dalton's fourth postulate: chemical reactions involve reorganisation of atoms, which are neither created nor destroyed."},{"kind":"numeric","prompt":"100 mL of hydrogen combines with 50 mL of oxygen to give 100 mL of water vapour. What is the simple ratio of hydrogen to oxygen volumes (as hydrogen/oxygen, e.g. answer 2 means 2:1)?","answer":2,"explain":"100 mL hydrogen to 50 mL oxygen simplifies to a ratio of 2:1."}]},{"id":"bc7","title":"Scales of the Infinitesimal","icon":"⚛️","kind":"lesson","story":"A cosmic balance floats before you, calibrated against a single carbon-12 atom split into twelve equal parts. To pass, you must weigh atoms and molecules using this impossibly tiny standard.","teach":[{"title":"Atomic mass unit (amu / u)","body":"The present system of atomic masses is based on carbon-12 (12C), assigned a mass of exactly 12 atomic mass units. One atomic mass unit (now called 'u', unified mass) equals one-twelfth the mass of one carbon-12 atom, and 1 amu = 1.66056 x 10^-24 g.","visual":"🎚️"},{"title":"Average atomic mass","body":"Many elements exist as multiple isotopes. The average atomic mass accounts for each isotope's relative abundance. For carbon: (0.98892)(12u) + (0.01108)(13.00335u) + (2x10^-12)(14.00317u) = 12.011 u.","visual":"📈"},{"title":"Molecular mass and formula mass","body":"Molecular mass is the sum of atomic masses of all atoms in a molecule, e.g. methane CH4 = 12.011 + 4(1.008) = 16.043 u. For ionic solids like NaCl that don't exist as discrete molecules, we use formula mass instead: 23.0 u (Na) + 35.5 u (Cl) = 58.5 u.","visual":"🧮"}],"questions":[{"kind":"numeric","prompt":"What is the molecular mass (in u) of methane, CH4? (Atomic masses: C = 12.011, H = 1.008)","answer":16.043,"explain":"Molecular mass of CH4 = 12.011 + 4(1.008) = 12.011 + 4.032 = 16.043 u."},{"kind":"numeric","prompt":"What is the formula mass (in u) of sodium chloride, NaCl? (Na = 23.0 u, Cl = 35.5 u)","answer":58.5,"explain":"Formula mass of NaCl = atomic mass of Na + atomic mass of Cl = 23.0 + 35.5 = 58.5 u."},{"kind":"numeric","prompt":"What is the molecular mass (in u) of glucose, C6H12O6? (C = 12.011, H = 1.008, O = 16.00)","answer":180.162,"explain":"6(12.011) + 12(1.008) + 6(16.00) = 72.066 + 12.096 + 96.00 = 180.162 u."},{"kind":"mcq","prompt":"Why is 'formula mass' used for sodium chloride instead of 'molecular mass'?","options":["NaCl does not exist as discrete molecules; it forms a 3D ionic lattice","NaCl is a gas at room temperature","Sodium and chlorine are isotopes of each other","NaCl has no measurable mass"],"answer":0,"explain":"In solid NaCl, Na+ and Cl- ions are arranged in a 3D structure, not as single discrete molecules, so 'formula mass' is used."},{"kind":"mcq","prompt":"One atomic mass unit (u) is defined as what fraction of the mass of one carbon-12 atom?","options":["One-twelfth","One-sixth","One-half","One-hundredth"],"answer":0,"explain":"1 u is defined as exactly one-twelfth of the mass of one carbon-12 atom."}]},{"id":"bc8","title":"The Avogadro Convergence","icon":"🌀","kind":"lesson","story":"A swirling portal counts particles faster than the eye can see. The guardian challenges: 'Just as a dozen means 12 and a gross means 144, tell me what a mole means -- and you may pass with an army of 6.022 x 10^23 entities at your back.'","teach":[{"title":"The mole and Avogadro constant","body":"The mole (mol) is the SI base unit for amount of substance. One mole contains exactly 6.02214076 x 10^23 elementary entities (atoms, molecules, ions, or particles) -- this fixed number is the Avogadro constant, NA.","visual":"🔮"},{"title":"Molar mass","body":"Molar mass is the mass of one mole of a substance in grams, and is numerically equal to the atomic/molecular/formula mass in u. For example, molar mass of water = 18.02 g/mol, and molar mass of sodium chloride = 58.5 g/mol.","visual":"⚖️"},{"title":"Counting atoms via molar mass","body":"Since 1 mole of carbon-12 weighs exactly 12 g, and one 12C atom has mass 1.992648 x 10^-23 g, dividing gives the Avogadro number: 12 g/mol divided by 1.992648x10^-23 g/atom = 6.0221367 x 10^23 atoms/mol.","visual":"🧮"}],"questions":[{"kind":"numeric","prompt":"How many entities (atoms/molecules) are in exactly 1 mole of any substance? Give your answer as a number in units of 10^23 (e.g. 6.022 means 6.022 x 10^23).","answer":6.022,"explain":"One mole contains 6.02214076 x 10^23 elementary entities -- the Avogadro number, approximately 6.022 x 10^23."},{"kind":"numeric","prompt":"What is the molar mass of water, H2O, in g/mol? (H = 1.008, O = 16.00)","answer":18.02,"explain":"Molar mass of water = 2(1.008) + 16.00 = 18.02 g/mol, matching the text's stated value."},{"kind":"numeric","prompt":"What is the molar mass of sodium chloride, NaCl, in g/mol?","answer":58.5,"explain":"The molar mass in grams is numerically equal to the formula mass in u: 58.5 g/mol."},{"kind":"mcq","prompt":"The mole is analogous to which everyday counting unit, according to the text?","options":["A dozen (12 items) or a gross (144 items)","A kilogram","A litre","A percentage"],"answer":0,"explain":"Just as a dozen means 12 items and a gross means 144 items, a mole is a convenient counting unit for extremely large numbers of entities."}]},{"id":"bc9","title":"The Formula Forge","icon":"🔥","kind":"lesson","story":"An unidentified compound sits on the forge's anvil, its percentage composition scrawled beside it: 4.07% hydrogen, 24.27% carbon, 71.65% chlorine. The forge-master demands you hammer out its true empirical and molecular formula before the fire dies.","teach":[{"title":"Mass percent formula","body":"Mass % of an element = (mass of that element in the compound / molar mass of the compound) x 100. For water (molar mass 18.02 g), mass % of hydrogen = 11.18% and mass % of oxygen = 88.79%.","visual":"📐"},{"title":"Percentage composition of ethanol","body":"For ethanol (C2H5OH, molar mass 46.068 g): mass % carbon = 24.02/46.068 x 100 = 52.14%, mass % hydrogen = 6.048/46.068 x 100 = 13.13%, and mass % oxygen = 16.00/46.068 x 100 = 34.73%.","visual":"🍶"},{"title":"Empirical formula from percent composition","body":"Steps: (1) convert mass % to grams using 100 g sample; (2) divide each mass by its atomic mass to get moles; (3) divide all mole values by the smallest one to get simplest ratio; (4) write the empirical formula. For 4.07% H, 24.27% C, 71.65% Cl: moles are H=4.04, C=2.021, Cl=2.021, giving ratio 2:1:1, so empirical formula = CH2Cl.","visual":"🔎"},{"title":"Molecular formula from empirical formula","body":"Divide the compound's molar mass by the empirical formula mass to get n, then multiply the empirical formula by n. For CH2Cl (empirical formula mass 49.48 g) with molar mass 98.96 g: n = 98.96/49.48 = 2, so molecular formula = C2H4Cl2.","visual":"🧬"}],"questions":[{"kind":"numeric","prompt":"Molar mass of water is 18.02 g. What is the mass percent of oxygen in water (O = 16.00 g)? Round to two decimal places.","answer":88.79,"explain":"Mass % oxygen = (16.00/18.02) x 100 = 88.79%, as computed in the text."},{"kind":"numeric","prompt":"In ethanol (C2H5OH, molar mass 46.068 g), what is the mass percent of carbon? Round to two decimal places.","answer":52.14,"explain":"Mass % carbon = (24.02/46.068) x 100 = 52.14%, as computed in the text."},{"kind":"mcq","prompt":"A compound has 4.07% H, 24.27% C, and 71.65% Cl, giving mole ratio H:C:Cl = 2:1:1. What is its empirical formula?","options":["CH2Cl","CHCl2","C2H4Cl","CH4Cl2"],"answer":0,"explain":"The mole ratio 2:1:1 for H:C:Cl directly gives the empirical formula CH2Cl."},{"kind":"numeric","prompt":"Empirical formula CH2Cl has an empirical formula mass of 49.48 g. If the compound's molar mass is 98.96 g, what is the value of n (molar mass / empirical formula mass)?","answer":2,"explain":"n = 98.96 / 49.48 = 2."},{"kind":"mcq","prompt":"Using n = 2 with empirical formula CH2Cl, what is the molecular formula?","options":["C2H4Cl2","CH2Cl","C4H8Cl2","C2H2Cl2"],"answer":0,"explain":"Multiplying the empirical formula CH2Cl by n = 2 gives the molecular formula C2H4Cl2."}]},{"id":"bc10","title":"The Reactant's Reckoning","icon":"⚖️","kind":"lesson","story":"Two reactant statues face off across a chamber -- one dinitrogen, one dihydrogen -- but their amounts are unequal. Only the reactant that runs out first (the limiting reagent) determines how much product the chamber will yield. Balance the equation, and claim your ammonia.","teach":[{"title":"Stoichiometry and balanced equations","body":"Stoichiometry deals with calculating masses (or volumes) of reactants and products in a chemical reaction. In CH4(g) + 2O2(g) -> CO2(g) + 2H2O(g), the coefficients (stoichiometric coefficients) show that 1 mol CH4 reacts with 2 mol O2 to give 1 mol CO2 and 2 mol H2O, i.e. 16 g CH4 reacts with 2x32 g O2 to give 44 g CO2 and 2x18 g H2O.","visual":"🧾"},{"title":"Limiting reagent","body":"When reactants are not present in the exact ratio required by the balanced equation, the reactant present in the least required amount is consumed first and stops the reaction -- it is called the limiting reagent, and it determines the maximum amount of product formed.","visual":"🛑"},{"title":"Worked example: ammonia synthesis","body":"For N2 + 3H2 -> 2NH3, mixing 50.0 kg N2 (1786 mol) with 10.0 kg H2 (4960 mol): 1786 mol N2 would need 5360 mol H2, but only 4960 mol H2 is available, so H2 is the limiting reagent. This yields 3.30 x 10^3 mol NH3, i.e. 56.1 kg NH3.","visual":"🏭"},{"title":"Molarity of a solution","body":"Molarity (M) = moles of solute / volume of solution in litres. It is the most widely used concentration unit. To prepare 0.2 M NaOH from a 1 M stock, you use M1V1 = M2V2, taking 200 mL of the 1 M stock and diluting to 1 L.","visual":"🧪"}],"questions":[{"kind":"numeric","prompt":"Using CH4(g) + 2O2(g) -> CO2(g) + 2H2O(g), how many grams of water are produced from the combustion of 16 g of methane (1 mole)?","answer":36,"explain":"1 mol CH4 gives 2 mol H2O = 2 x 18 g = 36 g H2O, as shown in Problem 1.3 of the text."},{"kind":"numeric","prompt":"Using CH4(g) + 2O2(g) -> CO2(g) + 2H2O(g), how many moles of methane are required to produce 22 g of CO2?","answer":0.5,"explain":"44 g CO2 (1 mol) comes from 1 mol CH4, so 22 g CO2 (0.5 mol) needs 0.5 mol CH4, as in Problem 1.4."},{"kind":"mcq","prompt":"In the reaction N2 + 3H2 -> 2NH3, mixing 1786 mol N2 with only 4960 mol H2 (when 5360 mol H2 would be needed), which is the limiting reagent?","options":["H2 (dihydrogen)","N2 (dinitrogen)","NH3 (ammonia)","Neither -- both are used completely"],"answer":0,"explain":"Since only 4960 mol H2 is available but 5360 mol is required to react with all the N2, dihydrogen runs out first and is the limiting reagent."},{"kind":"numeric","prompt":"A solution is prepared by adding 2 g of substance A to 18 g of water (total 20 g solution). What is the mass percent of solute A?","answer":10,"explain":"Mass % = (mass of solute / total mass of solution) x 100 = (2/20) x 100 = 10%."},{"kind":"numeric","prompt":"What volume (in mL) of a 1 M NaOH stock solution is needed to prepare 1000 mL of 0.2 M NaOH solution?","answer":200,"explain":"Using M1V1 = M2V2: 1 M x V1 = 0.2 M x 1000 mL, so V1 = 200 mL, as computed in the text."}]},{"id":"bc-boss","title":"Duel with the Mole Master","icon":"👹","kind":"boss","story":"At the heart of the temple stands the Mole Master, guardian of every law, unit, and formula you have learned. Its body shimmers as 6.022 x 10^23 tiny motes, and it will only fall if you answer with the precision of a true chemist.","boss":{"name":"The Mole Master","taunt":"You think a handful of atoms can face me? I AM the Avogadro constant given form -- prove your mastery of matter, measurement, and moles, or be forever uncertain!"},"teach":[{"title":"Review: laws and theory","body":"The Law of Conservation of Mass, Law of Definite Proportions, Law of Multiple Proportions, Gay Lussac's Law, and Avogadro's Law together led to Dalton's Atomic Theory -- atoms are the indivisible building blocks of matter.","visual":"📜"},{"title":"Review: mole and molar mass","body":"One mole equals 6.022 x 10^23 entities (Avogadro constant). Molar mass in grams equals the atomic/molecular/formula mass in u -- e.g. water is 18.02 g/mol, NaCl is 58.5 g/mol.","visual":"🌀"},{"title":"Review: stoichiometry","body":"A balanced chemical equation's coefficients give molar ratios of reactants and products. The limiting reagent -- the one present in the least required amount -- determines how much product can form.","visual":"⚖️"}],"questions":[{"kind":"mcq","prompt":"Which law explains why hydrogen (2g) combines with oxygen in the ratio 16g (water) or 32g (hydrogen peroxide), giving a simple whole-number ratio of 1:2?","options":["Law of Multiple Proportions","Law of Conservation of Mass","Law of Definite Proportions","Avogadro's Law"],"answer":0,"explain":"The Law of Multiple Proportions states that masses of one element combining with a fixed mass of another are in a ratio of small whole numbers."},{"kind":"numeric","prompt":"What is the molecular mass (in u) of glucose, C6H12O6? (C = 12.011, H = 1.008, O = 16.00)","answer":180.162,"explain":"6(12.011) + 12(1.008) + 6(16.00) = 72.066 + 12.096 + 96.00 = 180.162 u, as in Problem 1.1."},{"kind":"numeric","prompt":"How many entities are in one mole, expressed as a multiple of 10^23 (e.g. 6.022 for 6.022x10^23)?","answer":6.022,"explain":"The Avogadro constant is 6.02214076 x 10^23, approximately 6.022 x 10^23."},{"kind":"numeric","prompt":"What is the molar mass (g/mol) of sodium chloride, NaCl? (Na = 23.0 u, Cl = 35.5 u)","answer":58.5,"explain":"Formula mass = 23.0 + 35.5 = 58.5 u, so molar mass = 58.5 g/mol."},{"kind":"mcq","prompt":"Dalton's Atomic Theory could explain the laws of chemical combination but failed to explain which set of observations?","options":["The laws of gaseous volumes","The law of conservation of mass","The law of definite proportions","The concept of isotopes"],"answer":0,"explain":"The text states Dalton's theory could not explain the laws of gaseous volumes -- this was resolved later by Avogadro's hypothesis."},{"kind":"numeric","prompt":"In N2 + 3H2 -> 2NH3, if H2 is the limiting reagent providing 4.96 x 10^3 mol H2, how many mol of NH3 form? (3 mol H2 gives 2 mol NH3). Answer in units of 10^3 mol (e.g. 3.30 means 3.30x10^3).","answer":3.3,"explain":"4.96x10^3 mol H2 x (2 mol NH3 / 3 mol H2) = 3.30x10^3 mol NH3, matching Problem 1.5."},{"kind":"numeric","prompt":"Using CH4(g) + 2O2(g) -> CO2(g) + 2H2O(g), how many grams of water form from burning 16 g of methane completely?","answer":36,"explain":"1 mol CH4 (16 g) gives 2 mol H2O = 36 g, as shown in the worked example."},{"kind":"mcq","prompt":"What number of significant figures should the answer of 2.5 x 1.25 = 3.125 be reported to?","options":["2 (giving 3.1)","4 (giving 3.125)","1 (giving 3)","3 (giving 3.13)"],"answer":0,"explain":"The result must not have more significant figures than the least precise measurement (2.5 has 2 sig figs), so the answer is rounded to 3.1."}]}]},{"id":"structureatom11","index":1,"name":"Structure of Atom","subtitle":"Chemistry Chapter 2","emoji":"⚛️","palette":{"from":"#34d399","to":"#065f46"},"lessons":[{"id":"sa1","title":"Discovery of the Electron","icon":"⚡","kind":"lesson","story":"The Study Atlas flickers and drags you into a humming glass tube crackling with pale green light. A beam races silently from the dark cathode toward a glowing screen — to unlock the door out, you must prove exactly what that mysterious beam is made of.","teach":[{"title":"Cathode Ray Discharge Tubes","body":"In partially evacuated glass tubes at very low pressure and high voltage, a stream of particles flows from the negative electrode (cathode) toward the positive electrode (anode) — these are cathode rays. They travel in straight lines and make a zinc sulphide screen glow.","visual":"🔦"},{"title":"Bent by Fields, So Negatively Charged","body":"In electric or magnetic fields, cathode rays deflect like negatively charged particles, and their behaviour does not depend on the electrode material or the gas used — so electrons are a basic constituent of all atoms.","visual":"⚡"},{"title":"Thomson's Charge-to-Mass Ratio","body":"In 1897, J.J. Thomson measured the electron's charge-to-mass ratio using crossed electric and magnetic fields, finding e/me = 1.758820 × 10^11 C per kg.","visual":"🧩"},{"title":"Millikan's Oil Drop Experiment","body":"R.A. Millikan's oil-drop experiment (1906-14) found the electron's charge to be -1.602176 × 10^-19 C. Combined with Thomson's ratio, this gives the electron's mass: 9.1094 × 10^-31 kg.","visual":"🛢️"}],"questions":[{"kind":"mcq","prompt":"Cathode rays travelling inside a discharge tube move...","options":["from the cathode toward the anode","from the anode toward the cathode","in random directions","only when the tube is heated"],"answer":0,"explain":"Cathode rays stream from the negative cathode to the positive anode."},{"kind":"mcq","prompt":"What did Thomson conclude about cathode rays from their behaviour in electric and magnetic fields?","options":["They are streams of negatively charged particles called electrons","They are neutral light waves","They are positively charged protons","They are made of X-rays"],"answer":0,"explain":"Cathode rays deflected like negatively charged particles, revealing the electron."},{"kind":"numeric","prompt":"Millikan found the charge on an electron to be about 1.6 × 10^-19 C. What is this value in units of 10^-19 C (one decimal place)?","answer":1.6,"explain":"Millikan measured the electron's charge as -1.6 × 10^-19 C (accepted value -1.602176 × 10^-19 C)."},{"kind":"numeric","prompt":"Millikan showed the charge q on an oil drop is always an integral multiple of e (q = ne). If a drop carries a charge of -8.01 × 10^-19 C and e = 1.602 × 10^-19 C, how many excess electrons (n) does it carry?","answer":5,"explain":"n = q/e = 8.01×10^-19 / 1.602×10^-19 = 5."}]},{"id":"sa2","title":"Protons, Neutrons and the Atomic Zoo","icon":"🧪","kind":"lesson","story":"Beyond the cathode ray chamber lies a vault guarded by a swirling cloud of charged particles. To open it, you must sort the positive canal rays from the neutral intruders Chadwick found hiding inside a beryllium target — and count every proton, neutron and electron correctly.","teach":[{"title":"Canal Rays and the Proton","body":"Modified cathode ray tubes revealed canal rays of positively charged particles whose mass depends on the gas used. The lightest of these, from hydrogen, was named the proton and characterised in 1919.","visual":"➕"},{"title":"Chadwick's Neutron","body":"In 1932, James Chadwick bombarded a thin sheet of beryllium with alpha particles and found electrically neutral particles slightly heavier than protons, which he named neutrons.","visual":"⚪"},{"title":"The Fundamental Particles","body":"Electron: charge -1.602176×10^-19 C, mass 9.109382×10^-31 kg (~0.00054 u). Proton: charge +1.602176×10^-19 C, mass 1.6726216×10^-27 kg (1.00727 u). Neutron: neutral, mass 1.674927×10^-27 kg (1.00867 u).","visual":"⚛️"},{"title":"Atomic Number, Mass Number, Isotopes","body":"Atomic number Z = number of protons = number of electrons in a neutral atom. Mass number A = protons + neutrons. Isotopes share Z but differ in A; isobars share A but differ in Z, e.g. carbon-14 and nitrogen-14.","visual":"🔢"}],"questions":[{"kind":"mcq","prompt":"Neutrons were discovered by bombarding a thin sheet of which element with alpha particles?","options":["Beryllium","Gold","Hydrogen","Helium"],"answer":0,"explain":"Chadwick (1932) bombarded beryllium with alpha particles to discover the neutron."},{"kind":"numeric","prompt":"For the neutral atom 80/35 Br (Z = 35, A = 80), how many neutrons does it contain?","answer":45,"explain":"Neutrons = A - Z = 80 - 35 = 45."},{"kind":"numeric","prompt":"A species has 18 electrons, 16 protons and 16 neutrons. What is its mass number?","answer":32,"explain":"Mass number = protons + neutrons = 16 + 16 = 32 (this is the sulphide ion, S2-)."},{"kind":"mcq","prompt":"Carbon-12, carbon-13 and carbon-14 (6, 7 and 8 neutrons, all with 6 protons) are examples of...","options":["isotopes","isobars","isoelectronic species","different elements"],"answer":0,"explain":"Isotopes share the same atomic number but differ in neutron count and mass number."},{"kind":"numeric","prompt":"Protium (ordinary hydrogen) has mass number 1. Deuterium has one proton and one neutron. What is deuterium's mass number?","answer":2,"explain":"Deuterium has 1 proton + 1 neutron, giving mass number 2."}]},{"id":"sa3","title":"Thomson's Pudding and Rutherford's Gold Foil","icon":"🎯","kind":"lesson","story":"A shimmering gold foil blocks your path, sprayed with alpha particles from an unseen source. Most sail straight through — but a rare few ricochet backward, and only by reading that pattern correctly can you shatter the plum-pudding illusion and find the atom's true, tiny heart.","teach":[{"title":"Thomson's Model (1898)","body":"Thomson proposed the atom as a uniformly charged sphere of positive electricity (radius ~10^-10 m) with electrons embedded in it like seeds in a watermelon — nicknamed the 'plum pudding' model. It explained overall neutrality but not later experiments.","visual":"🍉"},{"title":"Rutherford's Gold Foil Experiment","body":"Rutherford, Geiger and Marsden fired high-energy alpha particles at a thin (~100 nm) gold foil. Most passed straight through, a small fraction deflected slightly, and about 1 in 20,000 bounced back by nearly 180 degrees.","visual":"☄️"},{"title":"The Nuclear Model","body":"Rutherford concluded that an atom's positive charge and most of its mass are concentrated in a tiny central nucleus (radius ~10^-15 m, versus the atom's ~10^-10 m), with electrons orbiting it like planets around the sun.","visual":"⚛️"},{"title":"A Fatal Flaw","body":"Classical theory says an accelerating (orbiting) charge must radiate energy, so an orbiting electron should spiral into the nucleus within about 10^-8 s — but atoms are stable. Rutherford's model could not explain this.","visual":"💫"}],"questions":[{"kind":"mcq","prompt":"In Rutherford's gold foil experiment, most alpha particles...","options":["passed straight through the foil undeflected","bounced straight back","were absorbed by the foil","turned into electrons"],"answer":0,"explain":"Most alpha particles passed through undeflected, showing atoms are mostly empty space."},{"kind":"numeric","prompt":"About how many alpha particles out of 20,000 bounced back by nearly 180 degrees in Rutherford's experiment?","answer":1,"explain":"Roughly 1 in 20,000 alpha particles were deflected by nearly 180 degrees."},{"kind":"mcq","prompt":"What discovery from the scattering experiment doomed Thomson's plum-pudding model?","options":["Positive charge is concentrated in a tiny nucleus, not spread evenly","Electrons have no charge","Atoms are completely indivisible","Gold atoms have no nucleus at all"],"answer":0,"explain":"The sharp deflections showed positive charge is concentrated, contradicting Thomson's uniform sphere."},{"kind":"mcq","prompt":"What key stability problem could Rutherford's nuclear model NOT explain?","options":["Why orbiting electrons don't spiral into the nucleus, radiating away all their energy","Why atoms have mass","Why atoms are electrically neutral","Why protons repel each other"],"answer":0,"explain":"Classical theory predicts an orbiting electron should radiate energy and collapse into the nucleus in about 10^-8 s."},{"kind":"numeric","prompt":"If the radius of an atom is about 10^-10 m and the nucleus is about 10^-15 m, how many powers of ten smaller is the nucleus than the atom?","answer":5,"explain":"10^-10 / 10^-15 = 10^5, so the nucleus is about 5 orders of magnitude smaller."}]},{"id":"sa4","title":"Light's Hidden Code","icon":"🌈","kind":"lesson","story":"A furnace of glowing metal blocks the corridor, shifting from dull red to blazing blue as its temperature climbs. To pass, you must decode the electromagnetic wave crackling from it and prove that light, like coins, comes only in discrete quanta.","teach":[{"title":"Electromagnetic Waves","body":"Maxwell showed that an accelerating charged particle produces oscillating electric and magnetic fields, perpendicular to each other and to the direction of travel, that propagate as electromagnetic waves needing no medium — all travelling at c = 3.0×10^8 m/s in vacuum.","visual":"📶"},{"title":"Frequency, Wavelength, Wavenumber","body":"c = νλ relates frequency (Hz) and wavelength (m). The visible spectrum spans roughly 4.0×10^14 Hz (red, 750 nm) to 7.5×10^14 Hz (violet, 400 nm).","visual":"🌈"},{"title":"Black-Body Radiation","body":"A black body absorbs and emits radiation of all frequencies; its intensity and wavelength of maximum emission depend only on temperature. Classical wave theory could not explain the observed intensity-wavelength curve.","visual":"🔥"},{"title":"Planck's Quantum Theory","body":"In 1900, Max Planck proposed that atoms emit or absorb energy only in discrete packets called quanta, with energy E = hν, where Planck's constant h = 6.626×10^-34 J s.","visual":"🎲"}],"questions":[{"kind":"mcq","prompt":"All electromagnetic radiation travels through vacuum at...","options":["the same speed, c = 3.0×10^8 m/s","different speeds depending on wavelength","the speed of sound","zero speed"],"answer":0,"explain":"In vacuum, all electromagnetic radiation travels at the same speed c, regardless of wavelength."},{"kind":"numeric","prompt":"Using c = νλ, what is the frequency (in units of 10^14 Hz, one decimal place) of violet light with wavelength 400 nm?","answer":7.5,"explain":"ν = c/λ = 3×10^8 / 400×10^-9 = 7.5×10^14 Hz."},{"kind":"numeric","prompt":"What is the frequency (in units of 10^14 Hz) of red light with wavelength 750 nm?","answer":4,"explain":"ν = c/λ = 3×10^8 / 750×10^-9 = 4.00×10^14 Hz."},{"kind":"mcq","prompt":"According to Planck, an atom can only emit or absorb energy in...","options":["discrete packets called quanta, E = hν","any continuous amount","negative amounts only","amounts unrelated to frequency"],"answer":0,"explain":"Planck's quantum theory states energy is emitted/absorbed only in discrete quanta, E = hν."},{"kind":"numeric","prompt":"What is Planck's constant, in units of 10^-34 J s (three significant figures)?","answer":6.626,"explain":"Planck's constant h = 6.626 × 10^-34 J s."}]},{"id":"sa5","title":"The Photoelectric Effect","icon":"💡","kind":"lesson","story":"A detector chamber challenges you: shine light on a slab of metal and make electrons fly out instantly. Red light fails no matter how bright it burns — only a dim yellow beam above the metal's secret threshold frequency will do the job.","teach":[{"title":"Hertz's Puzzle","body":"In 1887, Hertz found that light striking certain metals (potassium, rubidium, caesium) instantly ejects electrons — with no time lag — but only above a metal-specific minimum 'threshold frequency'. Below it, no electrons are ejected no matter how bright the light.","visual":"🔌"},{"title":"Einstein's Explanation (1905)","body":"Einstein used Planck's quantum idea: each photon of energy hν can eject one electron if hν exceeds the work function W0 = hν0. The excess energy becomes the photoelectron's kinetic energy: KE = hν - hν0.","visual":"🤵"},{"title":"Work Function Examples","body":"Different metals need different minimum photon energies to eject an electron — sodium needs 2.3 eV while copper needs 4.8 eV. Potassium's threshold frequency is 5.0×10^14 Hz, so weak yellow light (~5.1-5.2×10^14 Hz) ejects electrons but red light (~4.3-4.6×10^14 Hz) never does.","visual":"📊"},{"title":"Wave-Particle Duality of Light","body":"Light shows wave behaviour (interference, diffraction) but also particle behaviour (photoelectric effect, black-body radiation). Radiation has a dual character, behaving as a wave or as a stream of photons depending on the experiment.","visual":"🌀"}],"questions":[{"kind":"mcq","prompt":"Why doesn't bright red light eject electrons from potassium, while even weak yellow light does?","options":["Red light's frequency is below potassium's threshold frequency, regardless of brightness","Red light has too much energy","Yellow light is brighter than red light","Potassium only responds to red light"],"answer":0,"explain":"Photoelectric emission depends on frequency exceeding the threshold, not on brightness."},{"kind":"mcq","prompt":"In the photoelectric effect, increasing the intensity (brightness) of light above the threshold frequency increases...","options":["the number of electrons ejected, not their kinetic energy","only the kinetic energy of each electron","nothing at all","the threshold frequency"],"answer":0,"explain":"More photons (higher intensity) eject more electrons; kinetic energy depends only on frequency."},{"kind":"numeric","prompt":"Potassium's threshold frequency is 5.0×10^14 Hz. Radiation of frequency 7.0×10^14 Hz strikes it. Using KE = h(ν - ν0), what is (ν - ν0) in units of 10^14 Hz?","answer":2,"explain":"7.0×10^14 - 5.0×10^14 = 2×10^14 Hz."},{"kind":"mcq","prompt":"The photoelectric effect and black-body radiation are best explained by treating light as...","options":["a stream of particles (photons) each carrying energy hν","a purely continuous wave","having no energy at all","a stream of electrons"],"answer":0,"explain":"Einstein explained the photoelectric effect using Planck's quantum (photon) picture of light."},{"kind":"numeric","prompt":"Sodium's work function is 2.3 eV. If a photon carries exactly 2.3 eV and strikes sodium, what is the kinetic energy (in eV) of the ejected photoelectron?","answer":0,"explain":"When hν equals the work function hν0 exactly, KE = hν - hν0 = 0."}]},{"id":"sa6","title":"Atomic Spectra: Fingerprints of Elements","icon":"🔭","kind":"lesson","story":"An unlabeled glass tube glows with a strange, ghostly colour. A prism splits its light into a handful of bright lines instead of a rainbow — and matching that unique pattern to hydrogen's fingerprint is the only way to identify the gas trapped inside.","teach":[{"title":"Continuous vs Line Spectra","body":"White light passed through a prism spreads into a continuous rainbow, but excited gaseous atoms emit light only at specific wavelengths, producing bright line spectra with dark gaps between them — a unique fingerprint for each element.","visual":"🌈"},{"title":"Emission and Absorption Spectra","body":"An emission spectrum records wavelengths radiated by excited atoms; an absorption spectrum is its photographic negative, showing dark lines where a continuum of light is absorbed by unexcited atoms of the same element.","visual":"▫️"},{"title":"The Hydrogen Spectrum and Rydberg's Formula","body":"Balmer found hydrogen's visible lines fit a formula; Rydberg generalised it to wavenumber = RH(1/n1² - 1/n2²), with the Rydberg constant RH = 109,677 cm^-1, n1 = 1,2,3... and n2 > n1.","visual":"📐"},{"title":"The Named Series","body":"Transitions ending at n1 = 1, 2, 3, 4, 5 are the Lyman (ultraviolet), Balmer (visible), Paschen, Brackett and Pfund (all infrared) series respectively.","visual":"🏷️"}],"questions":[{"kind":"mcq","prompt":"Why can line spectra be used to identify elements, like fingerprints identify people?","options":["Each element's excited atoms emit light only at its own unique set of wavelengths","All elements emit identical spectra","Line spectra only exist for hydrogen","Spectra depend only on temperature, not the element"],"answer":0,"explain":"Each element has a unique, characteristic line emission spectrum."},{"kind":"mcq","prompt":"Which series of hydrogen's spectral lines falls in the visible region?","options":["Balmer series","Lyman series","Paschen series","Pfund series"],"answer":0,"explain":"The Balmer series (n1 = 2) is the only hydrogen series that appears in visible light."},{"kind":"numeric","prompt":"In the Rydberg formula, which n1 value corresponds to the Lyman series (ultraviolet)?","answer":1,"explain":"The Lyman series corresponds to transitions ending at n1 = 1."},{"kind":"numeric","prompt":"Which n1 value corresponds to the Paschen series (infrared)?","answer":3,"explain":"The Paschen series corresponds to transitions ending at n1 = 3."},{"kind":"mcq","prompt":"An absorption spectrum is best described as...","options":["a photographic negative of the emission spectrum, showing dark lines where radiation was absorbed","identical to a continuous spectrum","only observable for solids","impossible to observe experimentally"],"answer":0,"explain":"The absorption spectrum shows dark lines at the same wavelengths the emission spectrum shows bright lines."}]},{"id":"sa7","title":"Bohr's Model of the Hydrogen Atom","icon":"🪐","kind":"lesson","story":"An electron spirals toward the nucleus, seconds from collapse. Niels Bohr's blueprint offers a way to freeze it into a fixed, quantized orbit — but only if you can calculate exactly which orbit, and with how much energy, it belongs in.","teach":[{"title":"Bohr's Postulates","body":"Niels Bohr (1913) proposed that an electron moves around the nucleus only in certain fixed circular orbits ('stationary states') of definite energy, without radiating energy while in them; energy is absorbed or emitted only when the electron jumps between orbits.","visual":"🎯"},{"title":"Quantized Angular Momentum","body":"Bohr postulated that an electron's angular momentum is quantized: mevr = nh/2π, where n = 1,2,3... is the principal quantum number — so only certain orbits are allowed.","visual":"🔄"},{"title":"Orbit Radii","body":"The radius of the nth stationary state is rn = n²a0, where a0 = 52.9 pm is the radius of the first Bohr orbit (n=1), where the electron normally resides in a hydrogen atom.","visual":"📏"},{"title":"Orbit Energies","body":"The energy of the nth stationary state is En = -RH/n², with RH = 2.18×10^-18 J. The ground state (n=1) has the most negative energy, E1 = -2.18×10^-18 J, and is the most stable. The same formula (with a Z² factor) applies to hydrogen-like ions such as He+ and Li2+.","visual":"⚖️"}],"questions":[{"kind":"mcq","prompt":"According to Bohr, an electron in a stationary orbit...","options":["moves without radiating any energy, as long as it stays in that orbit","constantly loses energy and spirals inward","radiates continuously","has zero angular momentum"],"answer":0,"explain":"Bohr's stationary states do not radiate energy while the electron remains in them."},{"kind":"numeric","prompt":"The radius of the first Bohr orbit (n=1) in hydrogen is a0 = 52.9 pm. Using rn = n²a0, what is the radius, in pm, of the orbit with n=2?","answer":211.6,"explain":"r2 = 2² × 52.9 pm = 4 × 52.9 = 211.6 pm."},{"kind":"numeric","prompt":"The ground state (n=1) energy of hydrogen is -2.18×10^-18 J. Using En = -2.18×10^-18/n² J, what is the energy (in units of 10^-18 J, as a negative number) of the n=2 state?","answer":-0.545,"explain":"E2 = -2.18×10^-18 / 4 = -0.545×10^-18 J."},{"kind":"mcq","prompt":"For hydrogen-like ions (He+, Li2+...), as the atomic number Z increases, the orbit energies become...","options":["more negative, and radii become smaller — the electron is held more tightly","less negative, and radii increase","unaffected by Z","positive"],"answer":0,"explain":"Higher Z pulls the electron in more strongly: energy becomes more negative and radius shrinks."},{"kind":"numeric","prompt":"For He+ (Z=2), the orbit radius is rn = 52.9(n²/Z) pm. With n=1, what is this radius in pm (two decimal places)?","answer":26.45,"explain":"r1 = 52.9 × (1/2) = 26.45 pm."}]},{"id":"sa8","title":"Bohr's Triumph and Fall","icon":"✨","kind":"lesson","story":"Your Bohr-orbit trick works perfectly on hydrogen's glowing lines — until a helium atom drifts into the chamber and the model falls apart. Face the cracks in Bohr's theory before they swallow your progress.","teach":[{"title":"Explaining Hydrogen's Spectral Lines","body":"Bohr's model explained hydrogen's line spectrum as photons emitted or absorbed when an electron jumps between orbits: ΔE = Ef - Ei, with photon frequency ν = ΔE/h — successfully reproducing the Rydberg formula.","visual":"📉"},{"title":"A Worked Transition","body":"For an electron falling from n=5 to n=2 (a Balmer-series, visible-light transition), the energy released is 4.58×10^-19 J, corresponding to a photon frequency of 6.91×10^14 Hz.","visual":"🎨"},{"title":"Where Bohr's Model Broke Down","body":"Bohr's theory could not explain the finer doublet structure of hydrogen's spectrum, could not account for the spectra of multi-electron atoms like helium, and failed to explain spectral line splitting in magnetic (Zeeman effect) or electric (Stark effect) fields.","visual":"🪨"},{"title":"Missing the Point Entirely","body":"Bohr's model also could not explain how atoms form chemical bonds with each other — a fundamental gap that a better, more general theory of atomic structure would need to fill.","visual":"❓"}],"questions":[{"kind":"mcq","prompt":"In Bohr's model, radiation is emitted when an electron...","options":["moves from a higher orbit to a lower orbit","moves from a lower orbit to a higher orbit","stays in the same orbit","leaves the atom entirely"],"answer":0,"explain":"Emission occurs when the electron falls from a higher-energy orbit to a lower one."},{"kind":"numeric","prompt":"For a transition from n=5 to n=2 in hydrogen, the emitted photon's energy is 4.58×10^-19 J. What is its frequency in units of 10^14 Hz (two decimal places)?","answer":6.91,"explain":"ν = ΔE/h = 4.58×10^-19 / 6.626×10^-34 ≈ 6.91×10^14 Hz."},{"kind":"mcq","prompt":"Which of these could Bohr's model NOT explain?","options":["The spectrum of helium, an atom with more than one electron","The existence of hydrogen's Balmer series","The radius of hydrogen's first orbit","The ground state energy of hydrogen"],"answer":0,"explain":"Bohr's theory was unable to explain the spectrum of multi-electron atoms like helium."},{"kind":"mcq","prompt":"The splitting of spectral lines in a magnetic field, which Bohr's model failed to explain, is called the...","options":["Zeeman effect","Stark effect","Compton effect","Doppler effect"],"answer":0,"explain":"The Zeeman effect is the splitting of spectral lines in a magnetic field."},{"kind":"mcq","prompt":"In an absorption transition, the electron moves...","options":["from a lower orbit to a higher orbit, absorbing energy","from a higher orbit to a lower orbit, absorbing energy","nowhere — absorption transitions don't exist","from the nucleus outward, ejecting the nucleus"],"answer":0,"explain":"Absorption occurs when nf > ni: the electron absorbs energy to move to a higher orbit."}]},{"id":"sa9","title":"Wave-Particle Duality and Uncertainty","icon":"🌊","kind":"lesson","story":"A shard of the Atlas asks you to pinpoint a speeding electron with a beam of light — but the sharper you focus on its position, the wilder its velocity becomes. Only de Broglie's wave idea and Heisenberg's warning can get you through without breaking the laws of physics.","teach":[{"title":"De Broglie's Bold Idea","body":"In 1924, Louis de Broglie proposed that matter, like radiation, has dual wave-particle behaviour: λ = h/mv, relating a particle's wavelength to its momentum (mass × velocity). Electron diffraction later confirmed this experimentally.","visual":"🌊"},{"title":"Only Small Things Show It","body":"Every moving object has a wavelength, but for everyday massive objects the wavelength is immeasurably tiny (a 0.1 kg ball at 10 m/s has λ = 6.626×10^-34 m). Only tiny masses like electrons have measurable wavelengths — exploited in electron microscopes, which magnify about 15 million times.","visual":"🔬"},{"title":"Heisenberg's Uncertainty Principle","body":"In 1927, Heisenberg showed it is impossible to simultaneously know an electron's exact position and exact momentum: Δx·Δp ≥ h/4π. Pinning down position precisely blurs the velocity, and vice versa.","visual":"🤷"},{"title":"Why This Killed Bohr's Orbits","body":"A well-defined orbit requires knowing both exact position and exact velocity at once, which the uncertainty principle forbids for something as small as an electron — so only probabilities of finding the electron can be discussed, not fixed paths.","visual":"🌫️"}],"questions":[{"kind":"mcq","prompt":"De Broglie's relation λ = h/mv means that...","options":["every moving particle, including electrons, has an associated wavelength","only light has a wavelength","only electrons have mass","wavelength does not depend on velocity"],"answer":0,"explain":"De Broglie proposed that all matter in motion has an associated wavelength."},{"kind":"numeric","prompt":"A 0.1 kg ball moves at 10 m/s. Its momentum mv (in kg·m/s) is used in λ = h/mv. What is mv?","answer":1,"explain":"mv = 0.1 kg × 10 m/s = 1 kg·m/s."},{"kind":"mcq","prompt":"Why don't we notice the wave nature of everyday objects like balls or cars?","options":["Their mass is so large that the associated wavelength is immeasurably tiny","They don't obey de Broglie's relation","Only charged particles have wavelengths","Wave nature only applies at very high speed"],"answer":0,"explain":"Large mass makes λ = h/mv extremely small and undetectable for macroscopic objects."},{"kind":"mcq","prompt":"Heisenberg's uncertainty principle states that it is impossible to simultaneously know an electron's exact...","options":["position and momentum","charge and mass","spin and charge","energy and mass"],"answer":0,"explain":"The uncertainty principle relates uncertainty in position (Δx) and momentum (Δp)."},{"kind":"mcq","prompt":"Why is the uncertainty principle significant for electrons but negligible for a milligram-sized object?","options":["The Δx·Δv product is significant only for very small masses like electrons, not heavier objects","It applies only to charged particles","Heavier objects don't have momentum","Electrons don't obey physical laws"],"answer":0,"explain":"Because Δv·Δx scales inversely with mass, the effect is negligible for macroscopic objects but significant for electrons."}]},{"id":"sa10","title":"Quantum Numbers and Orbital Shapes","icon":"🧩","kind":"lesson","story":"The final chamber is a maze of glowing clouds — spheres, dumbbells, and cloverleaf shapes drifting around a nucleus. Each cloud is an orbital, and only a correct four-number ID card (n, l, ml, ms) will let you claim the electron living inside.","teach":[{"title":"Schrodinger's Equation and Orbitals","body":"In 1926, Schrodinger developed a wave equation that, solved for the hydrogen atom, gives allowed energy levels and wave functions (ψ) called atomic orbitals, each described by three quantum numbers: n, l and ml. The probability of finding the electron at a point is proportional to |ψ|².","visual":"🌀"},{"title":"Principal and Azimuthal Quantum Numbers","body":"n (1,2,3...) fixes the shell (K,L,M,N...) and mainly the orbital's size and energy. l (0 to n-1) is the azimuthal quantum number fixing the subshell's shape: l = 0,1,2,3 are s, p, d, f.","visual":"🔢"},{"title":"Magnetic and Spin Quantum Numbers","body":"ml (from -l to +l, giving 2l+1 values) fixes an orbital's spatial orientation, so a subshell has 1 s-orbital, 3 p-orbitals, or 5 d-orbitals. ms (+1/2 or -1/2), proposed by Uhlenbeck and Goudsmit in 1925, describes electron spin; no orbital holds more than two electrons, and they must have opposite spins.","visual":"🔄"},{"title":"Shapes of Orbitals","body":"s-orbitals are spherical around the nucleus; p-orbitals have two lobes on either side of the nucleus (2px, 2py, 2pz); the five d-orbitals (dxy, dyz, dxz, dx2-y2, dz2) have more complex shapes and first appear at n=3.","visual":"🍀"}],"questions":[{"kind":"numeric","prompt":"For the third shell (n=3), l can be 0, 1 or 2, giving 1 s-orbital, 3 p-orbitals and 5 d-orbitals. What is the total number of orbitals in this shell?","answer":9,"explain":"1 + 3 + 5 = 9, which also equals n² = 3² = 9."},{"kind":"mcq","prompt":"An orbital with n=4, l=0 is designated...","options":["4s","4p","4d","4f"],"answer":0,"explain":"l = 0 corresponds to the s subshell, so n=4, l=0 is the 4s orbital."},{"kind":"mcq","prompt":"An orbital with n=5, l=3 is designated...","options":["5f","5s","5p","5d"],"answer":0,"explain":"l = 3 corresponds to the f subshell, so n=5, l=3 is the 5f orbital."},{"kind":"numeric","prompt":"For l=2 (a d subshell), how many values can ml take, using 2l+1?","answer":5,"explain":"2l+1 = 2(2)+1 = 5, giving five d orbitals."},{"kind":"mcq","prompt":"What shape do all s-orbitals have?","options":["Spherical, centred on the nucleus","Dumbbell-shaped with two lobes","Four-lobed clover shape","Cubic"],"answer":0,"explain":"s-orbitals are spherically symmetric around the nucleus."}]},{"id":"sa-boss","title":"Building the Periodic Table","icon":"👑","kind":"boss","story":"The Periodic Sentinel rises, built from every orbital you've ever filled. It will only fall if you can fill its shells the right way — following Aufbau, obeying Pauli, respecting Hund — and explain why chromium and copper refuse to play by the ordinary rules.","boss":{"name":"The Periodic Sentinel","taunt":"Fill me correctly, or your electrons scatter into chaos! Aufbau, Pauli, Hund — prove you command them all."},"teach":[{"title":"Aufbau Principle","body":"Electrons fill orbitals in order of increasing energy: 1s, 2s, 2p, 3s, 3p, 4s, 3d, 4p, 5s, 4d, 5p, 6s, 4f, 5d, 6p, 7s... In multi-electron atoms, the lower the value of (n+l) for an orbital, the lower its energy; if two orbitals share (n+l), the one with lower n has lower energy.","visual":"🏗️"},{"title":"Pauli Exclusion Principle","body":"No two electrons in an atom can share all four quantum numbers; equivalently, an orbital holds at most two electrons, which must have opposite spins. This limits a shell with principal quantum number n to a maximum of 2n² electrons.","visual":"🚫"},{"title":"Hund's Rule of Maximum Multiplicity","body":"Electrons entering degenerate orbitals of the same subshell (p, d or f) occupy each orbital singly, with parallel spins, before any pairing occurs — pairing starts only with the 4th electron in p, 6th in d, or 8th in f.","visual":"↑↑↑"},{"title":"The Chromium and Copper Surprise","body":"Chromium's expected configuration [Ar]3d4 4s2 actually appears as [Ar]3d5 4s1, and copper's expected [Ar]3d9 4s2 appears as [Ar]3d10 4s1 — an electron shifts from 4s to 3d because a half-filled (d5) or fully filled (d10) subshell is extra stable.","visual":"🔀"},{"title":"Why Half-Filled and Full Subshells Are Extra Stable","body":"Symmetrical electron distribution reduces mutual shielding and increases nuclear attraction, and the exchange energy released by swapping same-spin electrons between degenerate orbitals is maximum when a subshell is exactly half-filled or completely filled — both effects lower the energy and raise stability.","visual":"⚖️"}],"questions":[{"kind":"mcq","prompt":"Which electron configuration does chromium (Z=24) actually adopt, and why?","options":["[Ar]3d5 4s1, because a half-filled d subshell is extra stable","[Ar]3d4 4s2, following the normal filling order exactly","[Ar]3d6, ignoring the 4s orbital entirely","[Ar]4s2 4p4, skipping the d subshell"],"answer":0,"explain":"Chromium adopts 3d5 4s1 instead of 3d4 4s2 because a half-filled d subshell is extra stable."},{"kind":"mcq","prompt":"Copper (Z=29) adopts [Ar]3d10 4s1 instead of [Ar]3d9 4s2 because...","options":["a completely filled d subshell (d10) is extra stable","copper has no 4s electrons at all","the 3d subshell cannot hold 10 electrons","Hund's rule forbids [Ar]3d9 4s2"],"answer":0,"explain":"A completely filled d10 subshell is extra stable, so one 4s electron shifts into 3d."},{"kind":"numeric","prompt":"What is the maximum number of electrons that the shell with n=4 can hold, using the 2n² rule?","answer":32,"explain":"2n² = 2(4)² = 32."},{"kind":"mcq","prompt":"Hund's rule says that electrons filling a set of degenerate p-orbitals will...","options":["occupy each of the three p orbitals singly (parallel spins) before any pairing occurs","pair up in one orbital first before spreading out","never occupy more than one p orbital","always have opposite spins even when unpaired"],"answer":0,"explain":"Hund's rule requires single occupancy of each degenerate orbital before pairing begins."},{"kind":"mcq","prompt":"Which of these configurations represents a half-filled subshell (extra stable)?","options":["p3","p2","p4","p5"],"answer":0,"explain":"p3 has 3 electrons in the 3 p-orbitals, one each — a half-filled, extra-stable subshell."},{"kind":"mcq","prompt":"Two electrons occupying the same orbital must have...","options":["opposite spin quantum numbers (one +1/2, one -1/2)","the same spin quantum number","different principal quantum numbers","different azimuthal quantum numbers"],"answer":0,"explain":"The Pauli exclusion principle requires electrons sharing an orbital to have opposite spins."},{"kind":"numeric","prompt":"Nitrogen (Z=7) has configuration 1s2 2s2 2p3. Following Hund's rule, how many unpaired electrons are in its 2p subshell?","answer":3,"explain":"Each of the three 2p orbitals holds one electron with parallel spin, giving 3 unpaired electrons."},{"kind":"mcq","prompt":"What is the main reason extra stability arises for half-filled and fully-filled subshells?","options":["Symmetrical electron distribution and maximum exchange energy lower the overall energy","Electrons in these subshells have less mass","These subshells are closer to the nucleus","These subshells contain more protons"],"answer":0,"explain":"Symmetry reduces shielding and repulsion, and exchange energy is maximised, both lowering energy."}]}]},{"id":"classificationperiodicity11","index":2,"name":"Classification of Elements and Periodicity","subtitle":"Chemistry Chapter 3","emoji":"🧪","palette":{"from":"#f472b6","to":"#831843"},"lessons":[{"id":"cp1","title":"The Fractured Element Archive","icon":"🔺","kind":"lesson","story":"An old chemist's ledger lies in pieces — 63 elements known by 1865, scattered with no order. Before you can rebuild the archive into a working Periodic Table, you must recover the first three clues chemists ever found: triads, octaves, and a cylinder of atomic weights.","teach":[{"title":"Dobereiner's Triads","body":"In 1829 Johann Dobereiner noticed groups of three elements (triads) whose middle element had an atomic weight roughly halfway between the other two, and properties in between as well. Example: Li (7), Na (23), K (39) — Na's weight is about the average of Li and K. The same pattern held for Ca, Sr, Ba and for Cl, Br, I.","visual":"⚖️"},{"title":"Newlands' Law of Octaves","body":"In 1865 John Newlands arranged elements in increasing order of atomic weight and found that every eighth element resembled the first, like musical octaves. The pattern worked neatly only up to calcium. Newlands was awarded the Davy Medal by the Royal Society in 1887 for this work.","visual":"🎵"},{"title":"A cylinder of elements","body":"In 1862, French geologist A.E.B. de Chancourtois arranged the known elements in order of increasing atomic weight on a cylindrical table, an early attempt to show that properties recur periodically.","visual":"🌀"}],"questions":[{"kind":"mcq","prompt":"Johann Dobereiner's observation that groups of three elements shared related properties is known as the:","options":["Law of Triads","Law of Octaves","Modern Periodic Law","Law of Multiple Proportions"],"answer":0,"explain":"Dobereiner noted that in each triad the middle element's atomic weight was roughly the average of the other two."},{"kind":"numeric","prompt":"In Dobereiner's triad Li (atomic weight 7), Na, K (atomic weight 39), what atomic weight does the triad rule predict for the middle element, Na?","answer":23,"explain":"The average of 7 and 39 is 23, very close to sodium's actual atomic weight."},{"kind":"mcq","prompt":"Newlands' Law of Octaves compared the recurrence of element properties to:","options":["every eighth note in a musical octave","every eighth planet from the sun","the eight electrons in a noble gas shell","the eight groups in Mendeleev's table"],"answer":0,"explain":"Newlands found every eighth element (by increasing atomic weight) had properties similar to the first, just like musical octaves."},{"kind":"mcq","prompt":"Newlands' Law of Octaves worked reasonably well only for elements up to:","options":["calcium","chlorine","potassium","argon"],"answer":0,"explain":"Beyond calcium the eight-element repeating pattern broke down, and the law lost predictive power."}]},{"id":"cp2","title":"Mendeleev's Gambit","icon":"♟️","kind":"lesson","story":"Two gaps sit open in Mendeleev's table like missing teeth. He dares to leave them blank and predict the exact weight and density of elements no one has ever seen. Years later, gallium and germanium walk out of the lab and match his numbers almost exactly — you're about to see how bold a periodic table can be.","teach":[{"title":"Mendeleev's Periodic Law","body":"Working independently around 1869, Dmitri Mendeleev and Lothar Meyer proposed that arranging elements in increasing order of atomic weight makes similar physical and chemical properties reappear at regular intervals. Mendeleev's Periodic Law states: 'The properties of the elements are a periodic function of their atomic weights.'","visual":"📜"},{"title":"Properties over strict weight order","body":"Mendeleev sometimes ignored strict atomic-weight order to keep elements with similar properties together. Iodine has a lower atomic weight than tellurium, yet he placed iodine in Group VII with fluorine, chlorine and bromine because their chemical behaviour matched.","visual":"🔄"},{"title":"Predicting Eka-aluminium and Eka-silicon","body":"Mendeleev left gaps under aluminium and silicon, naming the missing elements Eka-aluminium and Eka-silicon, and predicted their properties. Eka-aluminium: atomic weight 68, density 5.9 g/cm3, oxide E2O3. When gallium was discovered its atomic weight was 70 and density 5.94 g/cm3 — a striking match. Eka-silicon (predicted atomic weight 72, density 5.5) matched germanium (72.6, density 5.36).","visual":"🔮"}],"questions":[{"kind":"mcq","prompt":"Mendeleev's Periodic Law says the properties of elements are a periodic function of their:","options":["atomic weights","atomic numbers","atomic volumes","nuclear charges"],"answer":0,"explain":"Mendeleev classified by atomic weight; atomic number as the true basis came later with Moseley."},{"kind":"numeric","prompt":"What density (in g/cm3) did Mendeleev predict for Eka-aluminium?","answer":5.9,"explain":"Gallium, discovered later, was found to have a density of 5.94 g/cm3 — very close to the prediction."},{"kind":"mcq","prompt":"Why did Mendeleev place iodine (lower atomic weight) in the same group as fluorine, chlorine and bromine, ahead of tellurium?","options":["Because iodine's chemical properties matched that group better than strict atomic-weight order suggested","Because iodine's atomic weight was miscalculated by everyone else","Because tellurium had not been discovered yet","Because IUPAC rules required alphabetical grouping"],"answer":0,"explain":"Mendeleev prioritised similarity of properties over strict atomic-weight ordering."},{"kind":"numeric","prompt":"What atomic weight did Mendeleev predict for Eka-silicon?","answer":72,"explain":"Germanium, discovered later, had an atomic weight of 72.6 — confirming the prediction almost exactly."}]},{"id":"cp3","title":"Moseley's X-ray Ladder","icon":"📡","kind":"lesson","story":"A physicist named Moseley aims X-rays at row after row of elements. The frequencies climb in a perfect straight line — but only when plotted against one particular number. The archive's true organizing principle is about to be revealed, and it isn't atomic weight.","teach":[{"title":"Moseley and the Modern Periodic Law","body":"In 1913 Henry Moseley studied the characteristic X-ray spectra of elements and found that a plot of X-ray frequency against atomic number (Z) gave a straight line, unlike a plot against atomic mass. This showed atomic number, not atomic weight, is the more fundamental property. The Modern Periodic Law states: 'The physical and chemical properties of the elements are periodic functions of their atomic numbers.'","visual":"📈"},{"title":"The long form periodic table","body":"The modern long-form table has 7 horizontal periods and 18 vertical groups (numbered 1–18 by IUPAC, replacing the old IA…VIIA, VIII, IB…VIIB, 0 notation). The period number equals the highest principal quantum number (n) of elements in that period.","visual":"🗂️"},{"title":"How many elements per period","body":"Period 1 has 2 elements (filling 1s). Periods 2 and 3 have 8 each (filling ns, np). Periods 4 and 5 have 18 each, because filling a d subshell becomes energetically favourable partway through. Periods 6 and 7 have 32 each, with 14 f-block elements from each placed in separate panels below the main table.","visual":"🔢"}],"questions":[{"kind":"mcq","prompt":"Henry Moseley showed that a straight-line plot of X-ray frequency is obtained against:","options":["atomic number","atomic mass","atomic radius","electron gain enthalpy"],"answer":0,"explain":"This established atomic number, not atomic weight, as the fundamental ordering property of elements."},{"kind":"numeric","prompt":"How many elements are in Period 4 of the modern periodic table?","answer":18,"explain":"Period 4 runs from potassium to krypton and includes the 3d transition series, giving 18 elements."},{"kind":"mcq","prompt":"The Modern Periodic Law states that the properties of elements are a periodic function of their:","options":["atomic numbers","atomic weights","ionization enthalpies","group numbers"],"answer":0,"explain":"This modified Mendeleev's original law, which was based on atomic weight."},{"kind":"numeric","prompt":"How many elements are in Period 1 of the periodic table?","answer":2,"explain":"Period 1 contains only hydrogen and helium, as the 1s orbital holds a maximum of 2 electrons."}]},{"id":"cp4","title":"Naming the Unnamed","icon":"🏷️","kind":"lesson","story":"A brand-new, furiously unstable element flickers into existence for a fraction of a second in a lab, and two rival teams both claim to have found it first. Until the dispute is settled, IUPAC needs a name nobody can fight over — built directly from the atomic number itself.","teach":[{"title":"Why a systematic name is needed","body":"Naming new elements was traditionally the discoverer's privilege, ratified by IUPAC. This caused disputes — element 104 was named Rutherfordium by Americans and Kurchatovium by Soviets. To avoid such conflicts, IUPAC recommends a temporary systematic name derived directly from the atomic number until discovery is confirmed.","visual":"⚔️"},{"title":"Building the systematic name","body":"Numerical roots stand for each digit (0=nil, 1=un, 2=bi, 3=tri, 4=quad, 5=pent, 6=hex, 7=sept, 8=oct, 9=enn). The roots for the digits of the atomic number are strung together in order, and 'ium' is added at the end. For example, element 104 becomes Un-nil-quad-ium = Unnilquadium, symbol Unq.","visual":"🧩"},{"title":"Worked example: element 120","body":"For atomic number 120, the digits 1, 2, 0 give roots un, bi, nil. Joined together with 'ium': unbinilium, symbol Ubn. This is exactly how Problem 3.1 in the textbook is solved.","visual":"🧮"},{"title":"Later, a permanent name","body":"Once confirmed, an element receives a permanent name and symbol by vote of IUPAC representatives, often honouring a country or scientist. Element 104's official name became Rutherfordium (Rf); element 106 was named Seaborgium (Sg) in honour of Glenn T. Seaborg, whose work led to the modern placement of the actinoids.","visual":"🏅"}],"questions":[{"kind":"mcq","prompt":"Using IUPAC's systematic nomenclature, what is the temporary name for the element with atomic number 104?","options":["Unnilquadium","Unnilpentium","Unniltrium","Ununquadium"],"answer":0,"explain":"1-0-4 gives roots un-nil-quad, plus the 'ium' ending: Unnilquadium."},{"kind":"mcq","prompt":"What is the IUPAC systematic name for the element with atomic number 120 (Problem 3.1 in the text)?","options":["unbinilium","binilunium","nilbiunium","unnilbium"],"answer":0,"explain":"Digits 1, 2, 0 give roots un, bi, nil, joined with 'ium': unbinilium, symbol Ubn."},{"kind":"mcq","prompt":"Which permanent element name honours Glenn T. Seaborg?","options":["Seaborgium (Sg), element 106","Rutherfordium (Rf), element 104","Dubnium (Db), element 105","Bohrium (Bh), element 107"],"answer":0,"explain":"Element 106 was named Seaborgium in honour of Seaborg's work on transuranium elements."},{"kind":"numeric","prompt":"What is the atomic number of the element whose official IUPAC name is Rutherfordium?","answer":104,"explain":"Element 104 was claimed by both American and Soviet scientists before being officially named Rutherfordium."}]},{"id":"cp5","title":"Building the Periods, Shell by Shell","icon":"🧱","kind":"lesson","story":"The archive floor is laid out in rows, and each row only unlocks once the previous row's electron shell is fully bricked in. Walk the aufbau path from hydrogen to krypton and see exactly where each row of the table begins, bends into a d-block detour, and ends.","teach":[{"title":"Periods 1-3: filling s and p","body":"Period 1 (n=1) has just 2 elements: H (1s1) and He (1s2). Period 2 (n=2) starts at lithium, fills 2s then 2p, and ends at neon (2s2 2p6) — 8 elements. Period 3 works the same way with 3s and 3p, from sodium to argon — 8 elements.","visual":"🧯"},{"title":"Period 4: the first d-block detour","body":"Period 4 starts at potassium (4s fills first), but before 4p fills, the 3d orbitals become energetically favourable, producing the 3d transition series. It starts at scandium (Z=21, configuration 3d1 4s2) and the 3d subshell is completely filled by zinc (Z=30, 3d10 4s2). The period ends at krypton after 4p fills, giving 18 elements total.","visual":"🔀"},{"title":"Period 5, and why it has 18 elements","body":"Period 5 mirrors period 4, with the 4d series starting at yttrium (Z=39) and ending at xenon. For n=5, the available orbitals in order of filling are 5s, 4d, 5p — 9 orbitals in total, holding a maximum of 18 electrons, which is exactly why the period has 18 elements (Problem 3.2).","visual":"🧮"},{"title":"Periods 6-7: the f-block appears","body":"Period 6 has 32 elements: electrons fill 6s, then 4f (starting at cerium, Z=58, and ending at lutetium, Z=71 — the lanthanoid series), then 5d, then 6p. Period 7 follows the same pattern with 7s, 5f (the actinoid series, starting after actinium, Z=89), 6d and 7p, ending at element 118, a noble gas.","visual":"🏔️"}],"questions":[{"kind":"numeric","prompt":"How many elements are in Period 2 of the periodic table?","answer":8,"explain":"Period 2 runs from lithium to neon, filling the 2s and 2p subshells (8 electrons total)."},{"kind":"mcq","prompt":"The 3d transition series in Period 4 begins with which element?","options":["Scandium (Z=21)","Potassium (Z=19)","Titanium (Z=22)","Zinc (Z=30)"],"answer":0,"explain":"Scandium has the configuration 3d1 4s2, marking the start of 3d filling."},{"kind":"numeric","prompt":"At which atomic number is the 3d subshell completely filled (electronic configuration 3d10 4s2)?","answer":30,"explain":"This is zinc, Z=30, where the 3d subshell holds its full 10 electrons."},{"kind":"numeric","prompt":"Using Problem 3.2's logic (n=5 gives 9 available orbitals: 5s, 4d, 5p), how many elements does Period 5 contain?","answer":18,"explain":"9 orbitals hold a maximum of 18 electrons, so Period 5 has 18 elements."},{"kind":"mcq","prompt":"The lanthanoid (4f) series begins at which element?","options":["Cerium (Z=58)","Lanthanum (Z=57)","Lutetium (Z=71)","Actinium (Z=89)"],"answer":0,"explain":"The text states the 4f orbitals begin filling at cerium (Z=58) and end at lutetium (Z=71)."}]},{"id":"cp6","title":"Four Tribes of Elements","icon":"🏛️","kind":"lesson","story":"The archive splits into four wings — s, p, d, and f — each guarded by elements with a shared electronic signature. Hydrogen and helium refuse to sit neatly in any one wing. Sort the tribes correctly before the whole structure collapses.","teach":[{"title":"s-Block Elements","body":"Group 1 (alkali metals, ns1) and Group 2 (alkaline earth metals, ns2) make up the s-block. These are reactive metals with low ionization enthalpies that readily lose their outer electron(s) to form 1+ or 2+ ions. Because of their high reactivity, they are never found pure in nature.","visual":"🥇"},{"title":"p-Block Elements","body":"Groups 13 to 18 form the p-block, with configurations from ns2np1 to ns2np6. Each period ends with a noble gas (ns2np6) of very low reactivity. Just before the noble gases sit the halogens (Group 17) and chalcogens (Group 16), which have highly negative electron gain enthalpies and readily gain electrons.","visual":"🥈"},{"title":"d-Block (Transition) Elements","body":"Groups 3 to 12 make up the d-block, with the general configuration (n-1)d1-10 ns0-2. They are all metals, often form coloured ions, show variable oxidation states, and are frequently used as catalysts. Zn, Cd and Hg, with configuration (n-1)d10ns2, don't show most typical transition-metal properties.","visual":"🥉"},{"title":"f-Block Elements and the H/He exceptions","body":"The lanthanoids (Ce–Lu) and actinoids (Th–Lr) form the f-block, configuration (n-2)f1-14(n-1)d0-1ns2 — all metals, with actinoids being radioactive. Helium technically belongs to the s-block (1s2) but is placed with the p-block noble gases because its filled shell gives it their properties. Hydrogen (1 electron, ns1) is placed separately at the top since it can behave like both an alkali metal and a halogen.","visual":"🎖️"}],"questions":[{"kind":"mcq","prompt":"The outer electronic configuration of Group 1 (alkali metal) elements is:","options":["ns1","ns2","ns2np1","(n-1)d1ns2"],"answer":0,"explain":"A single electron in the outermost s orbital defines the alkali metals."},{"kind":"mcq","prompt":"Although helium has the s-block configuration 1s2, it is positioned with the noble gases because:","options":["its completely filled valence shell gives it noble-gas-like properties","it was discovered alongside neon and argon","IUPAC ran out of space in the s-block","it has 8 valence electrons like other noble gases"],"answer":0,"explain":"A filled 1s2 shell makes helium behave chemically like the other noble gases, so it sits in Group 18 despite its s-block configuration."},{"kind":"mcq","prompt":"The general outer electronic configuration of d-block elements is:","options":["(n-1)d1-10 ns0-2","ns2np1-6","(n-2)f1-14(n-1)d0-1ns2","ns1-2"],"answer":0,"explain":"d-block (transition) elements are defined by electrons filling the (n-1)d subshell."},{"kind":"numeric","prompt":"At what atomic number does the lanthanoid (f-block) series end?","answer":71,"explain":"The lanthanoid series runs from cerium (Z=58) to lutetium (Z=71)."},{"kind":"mcq","prompt":"Why is hydrogen placed separately at the top of the periodic table rather than firmly inside Group 1 or Group 17?","options":["It has one s-electron like alkali metals but can also gain an electron like a halogen","It is not actually an element","It has no electrons at all","It belongs to the d-block"],"answer":0,"explain":"Hydrogen's single electron lets it behave like both an alkali metal (losing an electron) and a halogen (gaining one)."}]},{"id":"cp7","title":"Measuring the Immeasurable Atom","icon":"📏","kind":"lesson","story":"You can't put a ruler on an atom — its electron cloud has no sharp edge. Chemists worked around this by measuring the distance between bonded atoms instead. Use their trick to map how atomic and ionic size shift across the archive.","teach":[{"title":"Covalent and metallic radius","body":"Since an atom has no sharp boundary, atomic radius is estimated from the distance between bonded atoms. The Cl-Cl bond in Cl2 is 198 pm, so chlorine's covalent radius is taken as half of that, 99 pm. For metals, the metallic radius is half the distance between adjacent atoms in the crystal — copper atoms are 256 pm apart, giving a metallic radius of 128 pm.","visual":"📐"},{"title":"Atomic radius trends","body":"Across a period, atomic radius decreases (e.g. Period 2: Li 152 pm down to F 64 pm) because effective nuclear charge increases while electrons stay in the same shell. Down a group, atomic radius increases (e.g. Group 1: Li 152 pm up to Cs 262 pm) because a new, farther shell is added and inner electrons shield the nucleus's pull.","visual":"📊"},{"title":"Ionic radius and isoelectronic species","body":"A cation is smaller than its parent atom (Na atomic radius 186 pm vs Na+ ionic radius 95 pm) because it has fewer electrons pulling against the same nuclear charge. An anion is larger than its parent atom (F atomic radius 64 pm vs F- ionic radius 136 pm) because extra electrons increase repulsion and reduce effective nuclear pull. Isoelectronic species (same electron count, e.g. O2-, F-, Na+, Mg2+ all have 10 electrons) shrink as nuclear charge increases.","visual":"⚛️"}],"questions":[{"kind":"numeric","prompt":"What is the atomic radius of lithium, in pm, at the start of Period 2?","answer":152,"explain":"Table 3.6(a) in the text lists lithium's atomic radius as 152 pm, the largest in Period 2."},{"kind":"numeric","prompt":"What is the atomic radius of fluorine, in pm, at the end of Period 2?","answer":64,"explain":"Atomic radius shrinks across a period; fluorine at 64 pm is much smaller than lithium's 152 pm."},{"kind":"mcq","prompt":"Why is the ionic radius of F- (136 pm) larger than the atomic radius of neutral F (64 pm)?","options":["The extra electron increases electron-electron repulsion and lowers the effective nuclear pull","Fluoride has more protons than fluorine","Ionic radius is always measured differently regardless of charge","F- has a different principal quantum number shell than F"],"answer":0,"explain":"Adding an electron increases repulsion among electrons, so the ion expands relative to the neutral atom."},{"kind":"mcq","prompt":"Among Mg, Mg2+, Al, Al3+ (Problem 3.5), which is the largest species?","options":["Mg","Mg2+","Al","Al3+"],"answer":0,"explain":"Atomic radii decrease across a period and cations are smaller than their parent atoms, so neutral Mg is the largest of the four."},{"kind":"numeric","prompt":"What is the atomic radius of caesium (Cs), in pm, at the bottom of Group 1?","answer":262,"explain":"Atomic radius increases down a group; caesium at 262 pm is the largest alkali metal listed."}]},{"id":"cp8","title":"The Cost of Tearing Away an Electron","icon":"⚡","kind":"lesson","story":"Every electron guards its atom fiercely, and prying one loose always costs energy. Some atoms surrender an electron with barely a fight; others clamp down hard. Measure that cost across the archive and you'll predict exactly which elements react — and which refuse to.","teach":[{"title":"Defining ionization enthalpy","body":"Ionization enthalpy is the energy required to remove an electron from an isolated gaseous atom in its ground state: X(g) -> X+(g) + e-. It is always positive and expressed in kJ/mol. The second ionization enthalpy (removing a second electron from X+) is always higher than the first, since removing an electron from a positively charged ion is harder.","visual":"🔋"},{"title":"Trends across periods and down groups","body":"Ionization enthalpy generally increases across a period, because increasing nuclear charge pulls harder on electrons in the same shell while shielding barely changes. It generally decreases down a group, because the outer electron is farther away and more shielded by inner shells, so it comes off more easily.","visual":"📉"},{"title":"Two famous anomalies","body":"Boron's first ionization enthalpy is slightly lower than beryllium's, even though boron has a greater nuclear charge, because boron's electron comes from a more-shielded 2p orbital versus beryllium's more-penetrating 2s orbital. Oxygen's first ionization enthalpy is lower than nitrogen's because oxygen must pair two electrons in one 2p orbital, and the resulting electron-electron repulsion makes that electron easier to remove.","visual":"🧨"}],"questions":[{"kind":"numeric","prompt":"The first ionization enthalpy of sodium is 496 kJ/mol (Problem 3.6). What is this value approximately, in kJ/mol?","answer":496,"explain":"Sodium, at the start of Period 3, has one of the lowest ionization enthalpies in its period."},{"kind":"mcq","prompt":"Why does boron have a slightly lower first ionization enthalpy than beryllium, despite boron's greater nuclear charge?","options":["Boron's electron is removed from a more-shielded 2p orbital, while beryllium's comes from a more-penetrating 2s orbital","Boron has fewer protons than beryllium","Beryllium is a noble gas","Boron's electrons are all paired"],"answer":0,"explain":"2s electrons penetrate closer to the nucleus than 2p electrons, so the 2p electron in boron is easier to remove."},{"kind":"mcq","prompt":"Why does oxygen have a lower first ionization enthalpy than nitrogen?","options":["In oxygen, two electrons must pair in one 2p orbital, and their mutual repulsion makes removal easier","Oxygen has fewer protons than nitrogen","Nitrogen's electrons are all in the 2s orbital","Oxygen has a larger atomic radius than nitrogen"],"answer":0,"explain":"Nitrogen's three 2p electrons sit unpaired in separate orbitals (Hund's rule), so no extra electron pairing repulsion needs overcoming."},{"kind":"numeric","prompt":"In Problem 3.6, given IE1 of Na=496, Mg=737 and Si=786 kJ/mol, is the IE1 of Al closer to 575 or 760 kJ/mol? Answer with the correct number.","answer":575,"explain":"Al's 3p electron is effectively shielded by the 3s2 pair, so its IE1 (about 577 kJ/mol) is lower than Mg's, close to 575."}]},{"id":"cp9","title":"Magnets for Electrons","icon":"🧲","kind":"lesson","story":"Some atoms don't just resist losing electrons — they actively pull new ones in, releasing energy as they do. Others practically repel an incoming electron. Map which corners of the archive attract electrons most fiercely, and you'll understand why halogens are so reactive.","teach":[{"title":"Electron gain enthalpy","body":"Electron gain enthalpy is the enthalpy change when an electron is added to a neutral gaseous atom: X(g) + e- -> X-(g). Halogens have very high negative electron gain enthalpies because gaining one electron gives them a stable noble-gas configuration; noble gases have positive electron gain enthalpies because the extra electron would have to enter an unstable, higher-energy shell.","visual":"🧲"},{"title":"The Cl > F anomaly","body":"Electron gain enthalpy generally becomes more negative across a period and less negative down a group. But chlorine (-349 kJ/mol) is actually more negative than fluorine (-328 kJ/mol), because the electron added to tiny fluorine suffers strong repulsion in the crowded 2p shell, while chlorine's larger 3p shell has room to spare.","visual":"🌀"},{"title":"Electronegativity","body":"Electronegativity is a qualitative measure of an atom's tendency to attract shared electrons in a bond, scaled by Linus Pauling in 1922 with fluorine fixed at 4.0, the highest value. Electronegativity increases across a period (Li 1.0 to F 4.0) and decreases down a group (F 4.0 to At 2.2), tracking the same trend as ionization enthalpy, and correlates directly with non-metallic character.","visual":"🎯"}],"questions":[{"kind":"numeric","prompt":"What is the electron gain enthalpy of chlorine, in kJ/mol (from Table 3.7)?","answer":-349,"explain":"Chlorine's electron gain enthalpy of -349 kJ/mol is actually more negative than fluorine's -328 kJ/mol."},{"kind":"mcq","prompt":"Among P, S, Cl and F (Problem 3.7), which element has the most negative electron gain enthalpy?","options":["Chlorine","Fluorine","Sulphur","Phosphorus"],"answer":0,"explain":"Chlorine's larger 3p subshell accommodates the incoming electron with less repulsion than fluorine's cramped 2p subshell."},{"kind":"mcq","prompt":"Why is fluorine's electron gain enthalpy less negative than chlorine's, even though fluorine is smaller and more electronegative?","options":["The added electron enters fluorine's small, crowded 2p subshell and suffers strong electron-electron repulsion","Fluorine already has a full octet and cannot accept an electron","Fluorine has fewer protons than chlorine","Chlorine is a noble gas"],"answer":0,"explain":"The compact n=2 shell in fluorine causes greater repulsion for the incoming electron than chlorine's larger n=3 shell."},{"kind":"numeric","prompt":"What is fluorine's electronegativity value on the Pauling scale?","answer":4.0,"explain":"Pauling arbitrarily assigned fluorine, the most electronegative element, a value of 4.0."},{"kind":"mcq","prompt":"Electronegativity generally shows which trend across the periodic table?","options":["Increases across a period, decreases down a group","Decreases across a period, increases down a group","Increases across a period and down a group","Stays constant across periods and groups"],"answer":0,"explain":"This mirrors the trend in ionization enthalpy and atomic radius shrinking across a period."}]},{"id":"cp10","title":"Reactivity at the Edges","icon":"💥","kind":"lesson","story":"Walk the archive's shelves from left to right: the leftmost elements practically throw their electrons away, the rightmost ones grab electrons hungrily, and the middle shelves sit strangely calm. Figure out why reactivity peaks at the edges — and dies out in the centre.","teach":[{"title":"Valence and oxidation state","body":"The valence of representative elements usually equals the number of electrons in the outermost shell, or eight minus that number. Group 1 has valence 1, Group 16 has valence 2 (or 6), and Group 17 has valence 1 (8 minus 7). For example, silicon (valence 4) and bromine (valence 1) combine to form SiBr4.","visual":"🔗"},{"title":"Reactivity peaks at the extremes","body":"Across a period, ionization enthalpy is lowest on the extreme left and electron gain enthalpy is most negative on the extreme right, so chemical reactivity is highest at both extremes (alkali metals losing electrons; halogens gaining them) and lowest in the middle. Reactivity of alkali metals increases down the group (Li < Na < K < Rb < Cs), while reactivity of halogens decreases down the group (F > Cl > Br > I).","visual":"↔️"},{"title":"Acidic, basic and amphoteric oxides","body":"Oxides formed by elements on the extreme left of a period are the most basic (e.g. Na2O, which reacts with water to form the strong base NaOH). Oxides formed by elements on the extreme right are the most acidic (e.g. Cl2O7, which forms the strong acid HClO4 with water). Oxides of elements in the middle are amphoteric (e.g. Al2O3) or neutral (e.g. CO, NO).","visual":"🧪"}],"questions":[{"kind":"mcq","prompt":"Na2O reacts with water to form:","options":["NaOH, a strong base","HClO4, a strong acid","Na2CO3, a salt","H2, a gas"],"answer":0,"explain":"Na2O + H2O -> 2NaOH, confirming its strongly basic character as an oxide from the extreme left of the period."},{"kind":"mcq","prompt":"Which oxide, formed by an element at the extreme right of a period, is strongly acidic?","options":["Cl2O7","Na2O","Al2O3","MgO"],"answer":0,"explain":"Cl2O7 reacts with water to form HClO4, a strong acid — typical of oxides from the far right of a period."},{"kind":"mcq","prompt":"How does the reactivity of Group 17 halogens change down the group?","options":["It decreases (F > Cl > Br > I)","It increases (F < Cl < Br < I)","It stays constant","It becomes unpredictable"],"answer":0,"explain":"Halogens become less reactive down the group as atoms get larger and hold added electrons less tightly."},{"kind":"mcq","prompt":"How does the reactivity of Group 1 alkali metals change down the group?","options":["It increases (Li < Na < K < Rb < Cs)","It decreases (Li > Na > K > Rb > Cs)","It stays constant","It becomes unpredictable"],"answer":0,"explain":"Alkali metals become more reactive down the group as the outer electron is held more loosely and lost more easily."},{"kind":"numeric","prompt":"Using the '8 minus outer electrons' rule, what is the valence of a Group 16 element (6 valence electrons) when forming a hydride like H2O?","answer":2,"explain":"8 minus 6 outer electrons gives a valence of 2, matching compounds like H2O and H2S."}]},{"id":"cp-boss","title":"The Reactivity Reaper","icon":"👹","kind":"boss","story":"The archive's final guardian has absorbed every scrap of the chapter — triads, octaves, Mendeleev's gaps, Moseley's straight line, the s/p/d/f wings, and every trend in radius, ionization enthalpy, electron gain enthalpy and electronegativity. Prove you've mastered the whole Periodic Table to seal the archive for good.","boss":{"name":"The Reactivity Reaper","taunt":"I sit at the extremes of every period, where electrons are won or lost in an instant. Show me you understand the whole table, corner to corner, or be filed away as an anomaly!"},"teach":[{"title":"The whole chapter, one map","body":"From Dobereiner's triads and Newlands' octaves, to Mendeleev's atomic-weight law, to Moseley's atomic-number law, the periodic table became an increasingly precise map of electronic configuration — and every physical and chemical trend you've studied flows from where an element sits on that map.","visual":"🗺️"}],"questions":[{"kind":"mcq","prompt":"Dobereiner's observation about groups of three related elements is called the:","options":["Law of Triads","Law of Octaves","Modern Periodic Law","Aufbau Principle"],"answer":0,"explain":"The middle element's atomic weight sat roughly halfway between the other two in each triad."},{"kind":"mcq","prompt":"Newlands' Law of Octaves held true reasonably well only up to which element?","options":["Calcium","Argon","Iron","Zinc"],"answer":0,"explain":"Beyond calcium, the every-eighth-element pattern broke down."},{"kind":"mcq","prompt":"Mendeleev's original Periodic Law was based on elements being a periodic function of their:","options":["atomic weights","atomic numbers","electron gain enthalpies","electronegativities"],"answer":0,"explain":"It was Moseley who later showed atomic number, not atomic weight, was the fundamental property."},{"kind":"numeric","prompt":"At which atomic number does the 3d transition series begin (scandium)?","answer":21,"explain":"Scandium, Z=21, has the configuration 3d1 4s2, starting the 3d transition series."},{"kind":"mcq","prompt":"The Modern Periodic Law (based on Moseley's X-ray work) states properties are a periodic function of:","options":["atomic numbers","atomic weights","atomic radii","ionic charges"],"answer":0,"explain":"Moseley's straight-line plot of X-ray frequency vs atomic number established this modern form of the law."},{"kind":"mcq","prompt":"Before official naming, what would the IUPAC systematic name for element 104 have been?","options":["Unnilquadium","Unnilpentium","Ununquadium","Unniltrium"],"answer":0,"explain":"1-0-4 maps to roots un-nil-quad, plus the 'ium' suffix."},{"kind":"numeric","prompt":"What is the ionic radius of Na+, in pm?","answer":95,"explain":"Na+ (95 pm) is much smaller than neutral sodium's atomic radius (186 pm), since it has lost its outer electron."},{"kind":"mcq","prompt":"Chemical reactivity across a period is generally:","options":["highest at the two extremes (alkali metals and halogens) and lowest in the centre","highest in the centre and lowest at the extremes","constant across the whole period","highest only among the noble gases"],"answer":0,"explain":"Elements at the extreme left lose electrons easily and elements at the extreme right gain electrons easily, making both ends highly reactive."}]}]},{"id":"chemicalbonding11","index":3,"name":"Chemical Bonding and Molecular Structure","subtitle":"Chemistry Chapter 4","emoji":"🔗","palette":{"from":"#fb923c","to":"#7c2d12"},"lessons":[{"id":"cb1","title":"The Kossel-Lewis Codex","icon":"⚛️","kind":"lesson","story":"The ancient Bond Forge lies dormant, its atoms scattered and lonely, each one incomplete. Legend says Kossel and Lewis once decoded the secret of the noble gases' stability in 1916 — relearn their codex, and the Forge's first bonds will awaken for you.","teach":[{"title":"The Kossel-Lewis Idea","body":"In 1916, Kossel and Lewis independently gave the first satisfactory explanation of valence, based on the chemical inertness of noble gases. Lewis pictured an atom as a positively charged 'kernel' (nucleus plus inner electrons) surrounded by an outer shell that could hold a maximum of eight electrons — the octet.","visual":"⚛️"},{"title":"Lewis Symbols","body":"Only the outer shell (valence) electrons take part in chemical combination; inner shell electrons are well protected and generally uninvolved. Lewis symbols show these valence electrons as dots around an element's symbol, and the number of dots helps calculate the element's common group valence.","visual":"•"},{"title":"The Octet Rule","body":"Kossel and Lewis's electronic theory of chemical bonding states that atoms combine either by transferring valence electrons (gaining or losing) or by sharing valence electrons, so as to have eight electrons — an octet — in their valence shells, like a noble gas (ns2 np6).","visual":"8️⃣"},{"title":"Forming NaCl by Electron Transfer","body":"Sodium, [Ne]3s1, loses its lone valence electron to become Na+ with the stable [Ne] configuration. Chlorine, [Ne]3s2 3p5, gains that electron to become Cl- with the stable [Ne]3s2 3p6 (argon-like) configuration. Electrostatic attraction between Na+ and Cl- forms the electrovalent (ionic) bond, and calcium similarly forms Ca2+ and F- ions to give CaF2.","visual":"🧂"}],"questions":[{"kind":"mcq","prompt":"What is the maximum number of electrons Lewis proposed could occupy an atom's outer shell for a particularly stable arrangement?","options":["8","2","6","18"],"answer":0,"explain":"Lewis's octet rule holds that eight electrons (an octet) in the outer shell give a particularly stable electronic arrangement, like the noble gases."},{"kind":"mcq","prompt":"When sodium reacts with chlorine to form NaCl, what happens to the sodium atom?","options":["It loses one electron to become Na+","It gains one electron to become Na-","It shares an electron pair with chlorine","It loses two electrons to become Na2+"],"answer":0,"explain":"Sodium ([Ne]3s1) loses its single valence electron to attain the stable [Ne] configuration, forming Na+."},{"kind":"numeric","prompt":"How many valence electrons does a neutral chlorine atom (outer configuration 3s2 3p5) have?","answer":7,"explain":"Chlorine's outer shell configuration 3s2 3p5 gives 2 + 5 = 7 valence electrons, one short of an octet."},{"kind":"mcq","prompt":"The bond formed by electrostatic attraction between oppositely charged ions, as in NaCl, is called:","options":["Electrovalent (ionic) bond","Covalent bond","Coordinate bond","Hydrogen bond"],"answer":0,"explain":"Kossel termed this the electrovalent bond; its electrovalence equals the number of unit charges on the ion."}]},{"id":"cb2","title":"Sharing Secrets and the Octet's Limits","icon":"🔗","kind":"lesson","story":"Not every atom in the Forge wants to give away electrons forever — some prefer to share. To pass the Sharing Gate, draw correct Lewis dot structures for the molecules guarding it, calculate a hidden formal charge, and learn exactly where the octet rule itself breaks down.","teach":[{"title":"Covalent Bond (Langmuir, 1919)","body":"When atoms share a pair of electrons rather than transferring them, they form a covalent bond. In Cl2, each chlorine atom contributes one electron to a shared pair, and both attain an argon-like outer shell octet.","visual":"🤝"},{"title":"Single, Double and Triple Bonds","body":"Sharing one electron pair gives a single bond (Cl-Cl). Sharing two pairs gives a double bond, as in the two C=O bonds of carbon dioxide. Sharing three pairs gives a triple bond, as in N2 and in ethyne (C2H2).","visual":"≡"},{"title":"Drawing Lewis Structures","body":"Total the valence electrons of all combining atoms (add one per negative charge, subtract one per positive charge). The least electronegative atom usually occupies the central position. Connect atoms with single bonds first, then turn remaining lone pairs into multiple bonds until every atom completes its octet. For CO, carbon (4) and oxygen (6) supply 10 valence electrons; a single bond leaves carbon short of an octet, so the atoms form a C≡O triple bond instead.","visual":"✏️"},{"title":"Formal Charge","body":"Formal charge = (valence electrons in the free atom) - (non-bonding lone pair electrons) - (bonding/shared electrons, counting one electron of each shared pair as belonging to the atom). In one Lewis structure of ozone (O3), the central oxygen carries a formal charge of +1, the double-bonded terminal oxygen is 0, and the single-bonded terminal oxygen is -1. Formal charges are bookkeeping, not real separated charges, and help pick the lowest-energy Lewis structure.","visual":"➖"},{"title":"Limitations of the Octet Rule","body":"The octet rule has three types of exceptions: an incomplete octet on the central atom (elements with fewer than four valence electrons, like Li, Be, B — e.g. LiCl, BeH2, BCl3); odd-electron molecules that can never fully satisfy the rule (NO, NO2); and an expanded octet in third-period-and-beyond elements that use available d orbitals to hold more than eight electrons (PF5, SF6, H2SO4).","visual":"🌐"}],"questions":[{"kind":"numeric","prompt":"How many total valence electrons must be distributed when drawing the Lewis structure of CO (carbon contributes 4, oxygen contributes 6)?","answer":10,"explain":"4 valence electrons from carbon plus 6 from oxygen gives 10 electrons to place as bonding and lone pairs."},{"kind":"mcq","prompt":"What type of bond exists between the carbon and oxygen atoms in the CO molecule?","options":["Triple bond","Single bond","Double bond","No bond, only lone pairs"],"answer":0,"explain":"A single or double bond leaves carbon without a complete octet, so CO is best represented with a C≡O triple bond."},{"kind":"numeric","prompt":"In one Lewis structure of ozone (O3), the central oxygen is double-bonded to one terminal O and single-bonded (plus one lone pair) to the other. What is the formal charge on that central oxygen?","answer":1,"explain":"Formal charge = 6 (free valence electrons) - 2 (one lone pair) - 3 (three bonds counted as one electron each) = +1."},{"kind":"mcq","prompt":"Which of these is a classic example of the 'expanded octet', where the central atom has more than eight electrons around it?","options":["SF6","CH4","BeCl2","NO"],"answer":0,"explain":"Sulfur in SF6 uses d orbitals to accommodate twelve electrons (six S-F bonds) around the central atom, an expanded octet."},{"kind":"mcq","prompt":"Why can odd-electron molecules like NO and NO2 never fully satisfy the octet rule for every atom?","options":["They have an odd total number of electrons, so one electron cannot be paired into any atom's octet","They contain only single bonds","They are ionic compounds","They have no lone pairs at all"],"answer":0,"explain":"With an odd total electron count, at least one electron is left unpaired, so not every atom can reach a complete octet."}]},{"id":"cb3","title":"The Lattice Vault","icon":"🏰","kind":"lesson","story":"Beyond the Sharing Gate stands the Lattice Vault, where Na+ and Cl- ions have locked themselves into a crystal fortress. To breach it, calculate exactly how much energy holds the fortress of ions together.","teach":[{"title":"What Favours Ionic Bond Formation?","body":"Ionic bonds form most easily between an atom that loses electrons easily (low ionization enthalpy) and an atom that gains electrons readily, releasing a lot of energy in the process (highly negative electron gain enthalpy). Ionization is always endothermic; electron gain may be exothermic or endothermic.","visual":"⚡"},{"title":"Ionization and Electron Gain Enthalpy of Na and Cl","body":"Converting Na(g) to Na+(g) requires 495.8 kJ/mol of ionization enthalpy. Converting Cl(g) + e- to Cl-(g) releases 348.6 kJ/mol as electron gain enthalpy. Together, forming the two gaseous ions from the gaseous atoms still costs a net 147.2 kJ/mol.","visual":"🔋"},{"title":"The Rock-Salt Crystal Lattice","body":"In the solid state, NaCl exists not as isolated ion pairs but as an orderly three-dimensional rock-salt lattice of Na+ and Cl- ions, held together by coulombic interaction energies.","visual":"🧊"},{"title":"Lattice Enthalpy Seals the Deal","body":"Lattice enthalpy is the energy required to completely separate one mole of a solid ionic compound into its gaseous constituent ions. For NaCl this is 788 kJ/mol. The energy released on forming the crystal lattice more than compensates for the 147.2 kJ/mol net cost of forming the gaseous ions, so stability is measured by lattice enthalpy, not simply by achieving a gas-phase octet.","visual":"🔒"}],"questions":[{"kind":"numeric","prompt":"What is the ionization enthalpy (in kJ/mol) needed to convert one mole of Na(g) to Na+(g)?","answer":495.8,"explain":"The ionization enthalpy of sodium is 495.8 kJ/mol; ionization is always an endothermic process."},{"kind":"numeric","prompt":"What is the lattice enthalpy of NaCl, in kJ/mol — the energy required to separate one mole of solid NaCl into gaseous Na+ and Cl- ions?","answer":788,"explain":"The lattice enthalpy of NaCl is 788 kJ/mol; this large energy release on crystal formation is what stabilises the ionic solid."},{"kind":"mcq","prompt":"Ionic bonds form most readily between elements with:","options":["Low ionization enthalpy and highly negative electron gain enthalpy","High ionization enthalpy and low electron gain enthalpy","High ionization enthalpy and highly negative electron gain enthalpy","Low ionization enthalpy and low electron gain enthalpy"],"answer":0,"explain":"Easy removal of an electron from one atom and easy, energy-releasing addition to the other favour ionic bond formation."},{"kind":"mcq","prompt":"Why is solid NaCl stable, even though forming gaseous Na+ and Cl- ions from Na(g) and Cl(g) costs a net 147.2 kJ/mol?","options":["The large lattice enthalpy released on forming the crystal (788 kJ/mol) more than compensates for that cost","Sodium and chlorine share electrons instead of transferring them","The octet rule is not obeyed in NaCl","Electron gain enthalpy of chlorine is positive"],"answer":0,"explain":"The energy released in forming the crystal lattice (788 kJ/mol) far exceeds the 147.2 kJ/mol net cost of forming the gaseous ions."}]},{"id":"cb4","title":"Measuring the Forge: Bond Parameters","icon":"📏","kind":"lesson","story":"The Forge's measuring instruments have gone silent. To recalibrate them, learn to read a bond's vital statistics: how long it is, what angle it holds, how much energy breaks it, and how many bonds truly lie between two atoms.","teach":[{"title":"Bond Length & Covalent Radius","body":"Bond length is the equilibrium distance between the nuclei of two bonded atoms, R = rA + rB. In Cl2, the covalent radius of chlorine is 99 pm (half the Cl-Cl bond length of 198 pm), while its van der Waals radius — half the distance between chlorine atoms of neighbouring, non-bonded molecules — is 180 pm.","visual":"📐"},{"title":"Bond Angle","body":"Bond angle is the angle between orbitals containing bonding electron pairs around the central atom, expressed in degrees and measured spectroscopically — for example the H-O-H angle in water.","visual":"📐"},{"title":"Bond Enthalpy","body":"Bond enthalpy is the energy needed to break one mole of a particular bond in the gaseous state. H-H in H2 needs 435.8 kJ/mol, O=O in O2 needs 498 kJ/mol, HCl needs 431.0 kJ/mol, and N≡N in N2 needs a huge 946.0 kJ/mol — one of the highest bond enthalpies of any diatomic molecule. In water, the two O-H bonds need different amounts of energy (502 and 427 kJ/mol) because the chemical environment changes after the first bond breaks, so an average O-H bond enthalpy of (502+427)/2 = 464.5 kJ/mol is reported.","visual":"💥"},{"title":"Bond Order","body":"Bond order is the number of bonds between two atoms in a Lewis structure: 1 for H2, 2 for O2, 3 for N2 and CO. Isoelectronic species share bond orders: F2 and O2^2- are both bond order 1, while N2, CO and NO+ are all bond order 3. As bond order increases, bond enthalpy increases and bond length decreases.","visual":"🔢"}],"questions":[{"kind":"numeric","prompt":"What is the bond enthalpy, in kJ/mol, of the N≡N triple bond in N2 — one of the highest for any diatomic molecule?","answer":946.0,"explain":"N2(g) → N(g) + N(g) requires 946.0 kJ/mol, reflecting the strength of its triple bond."},{"kind":"numeric","prompt":"Using ΔH1 = 502 kJ/mol to break the first O-H bond in water and ΔH2 = 427 kJ/mol for the second, what is the average O-H bond enthalpy (in kJ/mol)?","answer":464.5,"explain":"Average bond enthalpy = (502 + 427) / 2 = 464.5 kJ/mol."},{"kind":"numeric","prompt":"What is the covalent radius of chlorine (in pm), given that the Cl-Cl bond length in Cl2 is 198 pm?","answer":99,"explain":"The covalent radius is half the bond length between two identical covalently bonded atoms: 198 / 2 = 99 pm."},{"kind":"numeric","prompt":"N2 shares three electron pairs between its two nitrogen atoms. What is its bond order?","answer":3,"explain":"Three shared electron pairs between the atoms give N2 a bond order of 3, matching its very high bond enthalpy."},{"kind":"mcq","prompt":"A larger bond dissociation enthalpy generally indicates:","options":["A stronger bond","A weaker bond","A longer bond","No relationship to bond strength"],"answer":0,"explain":"The larger the bond dissociation enthalpy, the stronger the bond in the molecule."}]},{"id":"cb5","title":"The Ghost of Resonance","icon":"🌀","kind":"lesson","story":"A shimmering, shape-shifting spirit blocks the corridor — it is ozone, unable to settle on a single Lewis structure. Beyond it, molecular compasses strain toward the more electronegative atom. Understand resonance and polarity, and the path reveals itself.","teach":[{"title":"Resonance in Ozone","body":"Ozone's two possible Lewis structures each show one O-O single bond (148 pm) and one O=O double bond (121 pm), but its real, experimentally measured O-O bond length is 128 pm for both bonds — intermediate between single and double. Neither single Lewis structure captures this; the true molecule is a resonance hybrid of both canonical structures, and the hybrid is always lower in energy (more stable) than any single canonical form.","visual":"🌀"},{"title":"What Resonance Is (and Isn't)","body":"Canonical (resonance) structures have no real, independent existence. The molecule does not spend part of its time in one canonical form and part in another — there is no such equilibrium between them, unlike tautomers. The molecule has a single true structure: the resonance hybrid.","visual":"❗"},{"title":"Polar vs Nonpolar Covalent Bonds","body":"When identical atoms share electrons (H2, O2, Cl2), the shared pair sits exactly between the nuclei — a nonpolar covalent bond. When different atoms bond, like H and F in HF, the shared pair shifts toward the more electronegative atom, creating a polar covalent bond and a dipole moment (μ = charge × distance of separation), measured in Debye.","visual":"🧲"},{"title":"Shape Determines Whether Dipoles Cancel","body":"Linear CO2 has a dipole moment of 0 D because its two equal, opposite C=O bond dipoles cancel, while bent H2O has a dipole moment of 1.85 D because its bond dipoles do not cancel. Although fluorine is more electronegative than nitrogen, NH3 (1.47 D) has a larger dipole moment than NF3 (0.24 D): in NH3 the lone pair's dipole reinforces the N-H bond dipoles, while in NF3 it opposes and weakens the net N-F dipole.","visual":"➡️"}],"questions":[{"kind":"numeric","prompt":"What is the experimentally measured oxygen-oxygen bond length (in pm) in the O3 molecule, intermediate between a single (148 pm) and double (121 pm) O-O bond?","answer":128,"explain":"Both O-O bonds in ozone measure 128 pm, showing they are equivalent and intermediate between single and double bonds."},{"kind":"mcq","prompt":"Which statement about resonance canonical structures is TRUE?","options":["The canonical forms have no real, independent existence — only the resonance hybrid is real","The molecule spends part of its time in each canonical form","Canonical forms are in a dynamic equilibrium with each other, like tautomers","Each canonical form can be individually observed by spectroscopy"],"answer":0,"explain":"Canonical structures are bookkeeping devices; the actual molecule is a single resonance hybrid, not a mixture or equilibrium of the drawn forms."},{"kind":"numeric","prompt":"What is the dipole moment of water, H2O, in Debye?","answer":1.85,"explain":"H2O has a bent structure with a dipole moment of 1.85 D, since its two O-H bond dipoles don't cancel."},{"kind":"mcq","prompt":"Why does linear CO2 have a dipole moment of zero, even though each C=O bond is polar?","options":["The two equal and opposite C=O bond dipoles cancel each other out","Carbon and oxygen have equal electronegativity","CO2 has no bonding electron pairs","CO2 is an ionic compound"],"answer":0,"explain":"In the linear geometry, the two C=O bond dipoles point in exactly opposite directions and cancel."},{"kind":"mcq","prompt":"Why is the dipole moment of NH3 (1.47 D) greater than that of NF3 (0.24 D), even though fluorine is more electronegative than nitrogen?","options":["In NH3 the lone pair's dipole adds to the bond dipoles' direction, while in NF3 it opposes them","NH3 has more bonding pairs than NF3","NF3 is linear while NH3 is pyramidal","Nitrogen is more electronegative than fluorine"],"answer":0,"explain":"The lone pair's dipole reinforces the resultant N-H dipole in NH3 but opposes the resultant N-F dipole in NF3, lowering NF3's net moment."}]},{"id":"cb6","title":"The Geometry Council (VSEPR)","icon":"🔺","kind":"lesson","story":"The Geometry Council of electron pairs governs every molecule's shape, repelling each other to keep the peace. Master their repulsion rules, and you'll predict the true shape of any molecule left in the Forge.","teach":[{"title":"VSEPR's Core Idea","body":"Sidgwick and Powell proposed, and Nyholm and Gillespie refined, the Valence Shell Electron Pair Repulsion theory: a molecule's shape depends on the number of bonded and non-bonded (lone) electron pairs around the central atom, which arrange themselves to minimise repulsion and maximise distance between them.","visual":"🔵"},{"title":"Shapes Without Lone Pairs","body":"With no lone pairs on the central atom: AB2 is linear (BeCl2), AB3 is trigonal planar (BF3), AB4 is tetrahedral (CH4), AB5 is trigonal bipyramidal (PCl5), and AB6 is octahedral (SF6).","visual":"⭐"},{"title":"The Repulsion Order","body":"Lone pair-lone pair repulsion is strongest, then lone pair-bond pair, then bond pair-bond pair is weakest — because lone pairs, localised on just one atom, spread out more than bonding pairs shared between two nuclei.","visual":"🥊"},{"title":"Shapes With Lone Pairs","body":"A lone pair distorts the idealised shape. An AB2E molecule (like SO2) is bent, with its angle reduced to 119.5° from the ideal 120° of trigonal planar, because lone pair-bond pair repulsion exceeds bond pair-bond pair repulsion.","visual":"🔻"}],"questions":[{"kind":"mcq","prompt":"According to VSEPR theory, what shape does a molecule of type AB4 with no lone pairs on the central atom adopt, as in CH4?","options":["Tetrahedral","Square planar","Trigonal pyramidal","Linear"],"answer":0,"explain":"With four bonding pairs and no lone pairs, the electron pairs arrange in a tetrahedral geometry to minimise repulsion."},{"kind":"mcq","prompt":"Which correctly ranks the repulsive interaction of electron pairs from strongest to weakest?","options":["Lone pair-lone pair > lone pair-bond pair > bond pair-bond pair","Bond pair-bond pair > lone pair-bond pair > lone pair-lone pair","Lone pair-bond pair > lone pair-lone pair > bond pair-bond pair","All three repulsions are equal"],"answer":0,"explain":"Lone pairs, localised on one atom, occupy more space and repel more strongly than shared bonding pairs."},{"kind":"numeric","prompt":"In an AB2E molecule like SO2, lone pair-bond pair repulsion shrinks the ideal 120° trigonal-planar bond angle to what value, in degrees?","answer":119.5,"explain":"The extra repulsion from the lone pair reduces the bond angle slightly, from 120° to 119.5°."},{"kind":"mcq","prompt":"A molecule of type AB5 with no lone pairs, such as PCl5, adopts which VSEPR geometry?","options":["Trigonal bipyramidal","Octahedral","Tetrahedral","Square pyramidal"],"answer":0,"explain":"Five bonding pairs and no lone pairs arrange themselves in a trigonal bipyramidal geometry."}]},{"id":"cb7","title":"Valence Bond Theory: Overlap, Sigma and Pi","icon":"🌗","kind":"lesson","story":"Two hydrogen atoms drift toward each other in an empty chamber — the very first bond ever forged. Watch how their orbitals overlap, and learn the difference between a head-on sigma bond and a sideways pi bond.","teach":[{"title":"Orbital Overlap Forms the Bond","body":"As two hydrogen atoms approach, new attractive forces (nucleus of one atom to the other's electron) begin to outweigh new repulsive forces (electron-electron, nucleus-nucleus), so potential energy drops until a minimum is reached — the stable H2 molecule, with bond length 74 pm and bond enthalpy 435.8 kJ/mol.","visual":"⚛️"},{"title":"The Overlap Concept","body":"A covalent bond forms when atomic orbitals partially interpenetrate (overlap), pairing electrons of opposite spin present in the valence shell. Greater orbital overlap generally means a stronger covalent bond.","visual":"🔄"},{"title":"Sigma (σ) Bonds","body":"A sigma bond forms by end-to-end, head-on (axial) overlap of orbitals along the internuclear axis, via s-s, s-p, or p-p overlap. Because this overlap is extensive, sigma bonds are strong.","visual":"↔️"},{"title":"Pi (π) Bonds","body":"A pi bond forms by sideways overlap of p orbitals whose axes stay parallel to each other but perpendicular to the internuclear axis, producing electron clouds above and below the bonding plane. Pi bonds are weaker than sigma bonds because their overlap is smaller — and every multiple bond between two atoms consists of exactly one sigma bond plus one or more pi bonds.","visual":"〰️"}],"questions":[{"kind":"numeric","prompt":"What is the bond enthalpy of H2, in kJ/mol, as explained by valence bond theory's overlap of two 1s orbitals?","answer":435.8,"explain":"H2(g) → H(g) + H(g) requires 435.8 kJ/mol, matching the depth of the potential energy minimum where the H2 bond forms."},{"kind":"mcq","prompt":"A sigma (σ) bond is formed by which type of orbital overlap?","options":["Head-on (axial) overlap along the internuclear axis","Sideways overlap perpendicular to the internuclear axis","No overlap at all, just electron transfer","Overlap only between d orbitals"],"answer":0,"explain":"Sigma bonds form via end-to-end, head-on overlap of orbitals (s-s, s-p, or p-p) along the line joining the nuclei."},{"kind":"mcq","prompt":"Why is a sigma bond generally stronger than a pi bond?","options":["Sigma bonds involve a greater extent of orbital overlap than pi bonds","Pi bonds involve more electrons than sigma bonds","Sigma bonds only form between identical atoms","Pi bonds are always broken first in any reaction"],"answer":0,"explain":"The head-on overlap in a sigma bond is more extensive than the sideways overlap in a pi bond, making sigma bonds stronger."},{"kind":"mcq","prompt":"In any double or triple bond between two atoms, how many sigma and pi bonds are always present?","options":["Exactly one sigma bond, plus one or more pi bonds","Only pi bonds, no sigma bonds","Two sigma bonds and no pi bonds","Equal numbers of sigma and pi bonds in every case"],"answer":0,"explain":"Every multiple bond consists of exactly one sigma bond; any additional bonds in a double or triple bond are pi bonds."}]},{"id":"cb8","title":"Hybridisation: Reshaping the Orbitals","icon":"🔷","kind":"lesson","story":"Pure atomic orbitals alone can't explain the Forge's tetrahedral chambers, its trigonal halls, or its octahedral vault. Blend — hybridise — the orbitals yourself to unlock each room's true geometry.","teach":[{"title":"Why Hybridise?","body":"Simple orbital overlap can't explain CH4's 109.5° tetrahedral bond angles. Pauling proposed hybridisation: intermixing atomic orbitals of similar energy to form a new set of equivalent hybrid orbitals, which overlap more effectively and point in preferred directions in space, so the type of hybridisation indicates the molecule's geometry.","visual":"🌪️"},{"title":"sp and sp2 Hybridisation","body":"One s + one p orbital mix to form two sp hybrid orbitals (50% s, 50% p character) at 180°, as in linear BeCl2. One s + two p orbitals mix to form three sp2 hybrid orbitals at 120° in a trigonal plane, as in BCl3, giving a Cl-B-Cl bond angle of 120°.","visual":"📐"},{"title":"sp3 Hybridisation","body":"One s + three p orbitals mix to form four sp3 hybrid orbitals (25% s character each), directed to the corners of a tetrahedron at 109.5°, as in CH4. In NH3, one sp3 orbital holds a lone pair instead of bonding, so extra lone pair-bond pair repulsion squeezes the H-N-H angle down to 107°. In H2O, two sp3 orbitals hold lone pairs, squeezing the H-O-H angle further down to 104.5°.","visual":"🔺"},{"title":"sp3d and sp3d2 with d Orbitals","body":"Third-period-and-beyond elements can mix s, p and d orbitals. PCl5 uses sp3d hybridisation (trigonal bipyramidal: three equatorial P-Cl bonds at 120° to each other, two axial P-Cl bonds at 90° to the equatorial plane; axial bonds are slightly longer and weaker because they suffer more repulsion). SF6 uses sp3d2 hybridisation, giving a regular octahedral geometry with six equivalent S-F bonds.","visual":"🔶"}],"questions":[{"kind":"numeric","prompt":"What is the H-C-H bond angle, in degrees, in tetrahedral, sp3-hybridised CH4?","answer":109.5,"explain":"The four sp3 hybrid orbitals of carbon point to the corners of a tetrahedron, giving a bond angle of 109.5°."},{"kind":"numeric","prompt":"The ideal sp3 tetrahedral angle of 109.5° is reduced to what value, in degrees, for the H-N-H bond angle in pyramidal NH3?","answer":107,"explain":"The lone pair on nitrogen repels the bonding pairs more strongly than they repel each other, squeezing the angle to 107°."},{"kind":"mcq","prompt":"What hybridisation does the central Be atom adopt in linear BeCl2?","options":["sp","sp2","sp3","sp3d"],"answer":0,"explain":"One 2s and one 2p orbital of beryllium hybridise to form two sp hybrid orbitals oriented at 180°, giving BeCl2 its linear shape."},{"kind":"mcq","prompt":"In PCl5 (sp3d hybridisation), why are the two axial P-Cl bonds slightly longer and weaker than the three equatorial P-Cl bonds?","options":["The axial bond pairs experience more repulsive interaction from the equatorial bond pairs","Axial bonds involve d orbitals only, without any s or p character","Equatorial bonds are double bonds while axial bonds are single","Axial chlorine atoms are a different isotope"],"answer":0,"explain":"The axial bonds are at 90° to three equatorial bonds each, so they suffer more repulsion, stretching and weakening them slightly."},{"kind":"mcq","prompt":"Which molecule is the classic example of sp3d2 hybridisation with a regular octahedral geometry?","options":["SF6","PCl5","CH4","BeCl2"],"answer":0,"explain":"Sulfur in SF6 uses one s, three p, and two d orbitals to form six equivalent sp3d2 hybrid orbitals pointing to the corners of an octahedron."}]},{"id":"cb9","title":"Molecular Orbital Theory and the Diatomic Menagerie","icon":"🌌","kind":"lesson","story":"In the deepest chamber, ghostly molecular orbitals hum around pairs of nuclei rather than just one. Some pairs of atoms bond and thrive; others, like helium, cannot bond at all. Calculate their bond orders to see which survive.","teach":[{"title":"LCAO: Building Molecular Orbitals","body":"Molecular orbital (MO) theory, developed by Hund and Mulliken, combines atomic orbitals of comparable energy and matching symmetry via linear combination (LCAO). Adding wave functions (ψA + ψB) gives a lower-energy bonding orbital (σ); subtracting them (ψA - ψB) gives a higher-energy antibonding orbital (σ*).","visual":"➕"},{"title":"Bonding vs Antibonding","body":"A bonding MO concentrates electron density between the nuclei, lowering energy and holding the atoms together. An antibonding MO has a node between the nuclei with most electron density pushed away, raising energy and destabilising the molecule.","visual":"⚡"},{"title":"Bond Order From MO Theory","body":"Bond order = 1/2 (Nb - Na), where Nb and Na are the numbers of electrons in bonding and antibonding orbitals. A molecule is stable only if Nb is greater than Na (positive bond order); if Nb is less than or equal to Na, the molecule does not exist.","visual":"🧮"},{"title":"Homonuclear Diatomics: Who Survives?","body":"H2 (σ1s2) has bond order 1 and is stable and diamagnetic. He2 (σ1s2σ*1s2) has bond order 0 and does not exist. Li2 has bond order 1 (stable, diamagnetic). C2 has bond order 2, and since both pi molecular orbitals are fully paired, it is diamagnetic. O2 also has bond order 2, but two unpaired electrons sit in its π*2px and π*2py orbitals, correctly predicting that O2 is paramagnetic.","visual":"🧲"}],"questions":[{"kind":"numeric","prompt":"H2's electronic configuration is σ1s2 (2 bonding electrons, 0 antibonding). What is its bond order?","answer":1,"explain":"Bond order = 1/2(Nb - Na) = 1/2(2-0) = 1."},{"kind":"numeric","prompt":"He2 has configuration σ1s2σ*1s2 (2 bonding, 2 antibonding electrons). What is its bond order — explaining why He2 does not exist?","answer":0,"explain":"Bond order = 1/2(2-2) = 0, meaning no net bonding, so He2 is not a stable molecule."},{"kind":"mcq","prompt":"Why does molecular orbital theory correctly predict that O2 is paramagnetic?","options":["O2's electronic configuration has two unpaired electrons in the π*2px and π*2py antibonding orbitals","O2 has no antibonding electrons at all","O2's bond order is zero","All of O2's electrons are paired in bonding orbitals only"],"answer":0,"explain":"Hund's rule places one electron each in the degenerate π*2px and π*2py orbitals, leaving two unpaired electrons — a result Lewis theory could not explain."},{"kind":"mcq","prompt":"A molecule is predicted to be stable by molecular orbital theory when:","options":["The number of electrons in bonding orbitals (Nb) is greater than in antibonding orbitals (Na)","Nb equals Na","Nb is less than Na","All molecular orbitals are antibonding"],"answer":0,"explain":"A positive bond order (Nb > Na) means the bonding influence dominates, stabilising the molecule."},{"kind":"mcq","prompt":"C2 has configuration KKσ2s2σ*2s2π2px2π2py2 (8 bonding, 4 antibonding electrons). What is its bond order, and is it diamagnetic or paramagnetic?","options":["Bond order 2, diamagnetic","Bond order 3, paramagnetic","Bond order 1, paramagnetic","Bond order 0, does not exist"],"answer":0,"explain":"Bond order = 1/2(8-4) = 2, and since both pi molecular orbitals are fully paired (no unpaired electrons), C2 is diamagnetic."}]},{"id":"cb10","title":"Hydrogen Bonds: The Bridge Between Molecules","icon":"🌉","kind":"lesson","story":"At the Forge's edge, molecules of HF and water cling to each other across an invisible bridge. This is your final lesson before the Boss Chamber — master the hydrogen bond, weakest link yet mightiest influence.","teach":[{"title":"What Is a Hydrogen Bond?","body":"When hydrogen bonds covalently to a strongly electronegative atom (F, O or N), the shared electron pair shifts away from hydrogen, leaving it partially positive (δ+) while the electronegative atom becomes partially negative (δ-). This partially positive hydrogen then forms a weak attractive bond with an electronegative atom of another (or the same) molecule.","visual":"🌉"},{"title":"HF Chain Example","body":"In HF, the hydrogen atom of one molecule is attracted to the fluorine atom of a neighbouring molecule: ···Hδ+-Fδ- ··· Hδ+-Fδ- ···. The hydrogen bond (dotted line) is weaker than the covalent bond (solid line) but still bridges the molecules together.","visual":"⛓️"},{"title":"Intermolecular vs Intramolecular H-Bonds","body":"An intermolecular hydrogen bond forms between two different molecules of the same or different compounds, as in HF, alcohols, or water. An intramolecular hydrogen bond forms within a single molecule, as between the hydrogen and one oxygen in o-nitrophenol, where the H sits between two electronegative oxygens of the same molecule.","visual":"🔁"},{"title":"How Strong, and When?","body":"Hydrogen bonding is weaker than a covalent bond but still strongly influences structure and properties. Its effect is maximum in the solid state and minimum in the gaseous state, since molecules are held closest together in solids.","visual":"🧊"}],"questions":[{"kind":"mcq","prompt":"A hydrogen bond forms when a hydrogen atom, covalently bonded to a strongly electronegative atom, is also attracted to:","options":["An electronegative atom (F, O or N) of another (or the same) molecule","Any carbon atom nearby","A noble gas atom","Another hydrogen atom only"],"answer":0,"explain":"Hydrogen bonding needs a partially positive hydrogen (bonded to F, O or N) to be attracted to an electronegative F, O or N atom elsewhere."},{"kind":"mcq","prompt":"Which best describes an intramolecular hydrogen bond, as seen in o-nitrophenol?","options":["A hydrogen bond formed within a single molecule, between the H and an electronegative atom of the same molecule","A hydrogen bond formed between two separate molecules of the same compound","A covalent bond between two hydrogen atoms","A bond formed only in the gaseous state"],"answer":0,"explain":"In o-nitrophenol, the hydrogen sits between the two oxygen atoms of the same molecule, forming an intramolecular hydrogen bond."},{"kind":"mcq","prompt":"In which physical state is the influence of hydrogen bonding on a substance's structure at its maximum?","options":["Solid state","Gaseous state","Plasma state","It is the same in every state"],"answer":0,"explain":"Hydrogen bonding is strongest in the solid state and weakest in the gaseous state, where molecules are far apart."},{"kind":"mcq","prompt":"Compared to a covalent bond, a hydrogen bond is:","options":["Weaker","Stronger","Exactly equal in strength","Not comparable, since hydrogen bonds are ionic"],"answer":0,"explain":"The hydrogen bond is weaker than the covalent bond, even though it still significantly affects molecular properties."}]},{"id":"cb-boss","title":"Boss Chamber: The Octet Breaker","icon":"💀","kind":"boss","story":"At the Forge's core waits the Octet Breaker, a chaotic entity woven from every exception you studied — incomplete octets, odd electrons, expanded shells. It challenges you to prove mastery over every kind of bond in the Forge: ionic, covalent, resonant, hybridised, molecular-orbital, and hydrogen-bonded alike.","teach":[{"title":"Final Review","body":"This chamber tests everything: Kossel-Lewis and the octet rule, ionic bonding and lattice enthalpy, bond parameters and resonance, VSEPR shapes, valence bond theory and hybridisation, molecular orbital theory, and hydrogen bonding. Recall the numbers and reasoning from every lesson in the Bond Forge.","visual":"🔗"}],"boss":{"name":"The Octet Breaker","taunt":"Every rule has its exceptions — and I AM the exception! Incomplete octets, odd electrons, expanded shells… none of your precious noble-gas symmetry can bind me. Prove you understand bonding in every form — ionic, covalent, resonant, hybridised, molecular — or I'll leave every atom in this Forge forever unbonded!"},"questions":[{"kind":"numeric","prompt":"What is the lattice enthalpy of NaCl, in kJ/mol — the energy needed to separate one mole of the solid into gaseous ions?","answer":788,"explain":"The lattice enthalpy of NaCl is 788 kJ/mol, the key to its stability as a crystal."},{"kind":"mcq","prompt":"What VSEPR shape does linear BeCl2 (an AB2 molecule with no lone pairs on Be) adopt?","options":["Linear","Bent","Trigonal planar","Tetrahedral"],"answer":0,"explain":"With two bonding pairs and no lone pairs, the electron pairs point in opposite directions, giving a linear shape."},{"kind":"numeric","prompt":"What is the H-C-H bond angle, in degrees, in tetrahedral, sp3-hybridised CH4?","answer":109.5,"explain":"The four sp3 hybrid orbitals point to the corners of a tetrahedron, giving a bond angle of 109.5°."},{"kind":"mcq","prompt":"Which hybridisation scheme does sulfur adopt in octahedral SF6?","options":["sp3d2","sp3d","sp3","sp2"],"answer":0,"explain":"Sulfur mixes one s, three p, and two d orbitals to form six sp3d2 hybrid orbitals pointing to the corners of an octahedron."},{"kind":"numeric","prompt":"By molecular orbital theory, O2 has 10 electrons in bonding orbitals and 6 in antibonding orbitals. What is its bond order?","answer":2,"explain":"Bond order = 1/2(10-6) = 2, matching O2's double bond, and its two unpaired electrons make it paramagnetic."},{"kind":"mcq","prompt":"A hydrogen bond forms between a partially positive hydrogen and which atoms of a neighbouring (or the same) molecule?","options":["F, O or N","Any halogen","Carbon only","Noble gases"],"answer":0,"explain":"Hydrogen bonding specifically involves the strongly electronegative F, O or N atoms."},{"kind":"mcq","prompt":"What best describes the true structure of a molecule like ozone that has multiple valid Lewis structures?","options":["A resonance hybrid of the canonical forms, lower in energy than any single form","Whichever canonical form is drawn first","An equal-time average, flipping between forms like tautomers","None of the canonical forms are ever correct in any way"],"answer":0,"explain":"The molecule's real structure is the resonance hybrid, which is more stable than any individual canonical structure."},{"kind":"numeric","prompt":"What is the dipole moment of H2O, in Debye?","answer":1.85,"explain":"Water's bent shape gives it a dipole moment of 1.85 D, since its O-H bond dipoles do not cancel."}]}]},{"id":"thermodynamics11chem","index":4,"name":"Thermodynamics","subtitle":"Chemistry Chapter 5","emoji":"🌡️","palette":{"from":"#facc15","to":"#713f12"},"lessons":[{"id":"tm1","title":"System, Surroundings & State Functions","icon":"🌍","kind":"lesson","story":"You wake up inside a sealed alchemy dome where the Architects of Energy test every recruit on one question: where does 'you' end and 'everything else' begin? Draw the boundary wrong and the whole simulation collapses into chaos.","teach":[{"title":"System and Surroundings","body":"The system is the part of the universe under observation; everything else is the surroundings. The universe = the system + the surroundings, and the wall separating them (real or imaginary) is called the boundary.","visual":"🔗"},{"title":"Open, Closed and Isolated Systems","body":"An open system exchanges both matter and energy with the surroundings (reactants in an open beaker). A closed system exchanges only energy, not matter (reactants in a sealed conducting vessel). An isolated system exchanges neither matter nor energy (reactants in a thermos flask).","visual":"🧪"},{"title":"State of the System","body":"A system's state is described by measurable macroscopic properties like pressure, volume, temperature and amount (p, V, T, n), called state functions or state variables. Their value depends only on the current state, not on how that state was reached.","visual":"📊"},{"title":"Extensive vs Intensive Properties","body":"An extensive property depends on the amount of matter present, e.g. mass, volume, internal energy, enthalpy and heat capacity. An intensive property does not depend on the amount present, e.g. temperature, density and pressure.","visual":"⚖️"}],"questions":[{"kind":"mcq","prompt":"Which type of system exchanges neither matter nor energy with its surroundings?","options":["Isolated system","Open system","Closed system","All systems do this"],"answer":0,"explain":"A thermos flask is a classic isolated system — no matter or energy crosses its boundary."},{"kind":"mcq","prompt":"Reactants sealed inside a closed steel vessel (which conducts heat) form what type of system?","options":["Closed system","Open system","Isolated system","Universe"],"answer":0,"explain":"Energy (heat) can pass through the conducting steel walls, but no matter can leave or enter — that's a closed system."},{"kind":"mcq","prompt":"Which of these is a state function?","options":["Temperature","Heat absorbed, q","Work done, w","None of these"],"answer":0,"explain":"Temperature depends only on the current state of the system, unlike q and w which depend on the path taken."},{"kind":"mcq","prompt":"Which pair below are BOTH extensive properties?","options":["Volume and internal energy","Temperature and pressure","Density and temperature","Pressure and density"],"answer":0,"explain":"Volume and internal energy both scale with the amount of substance present, so both are extensive properties."}]},{"id":"tm2","title":"Internal Energy, Work, Heat & the First Law","icon":"⚡","kind":"lesson","story":"The Architects hand you a paddle-wheel and an immersion rod submerged in identical flasks of water. 'Churn the water with the paddle, or heat it with the rod,' they say. 'Either way, prove to me that energy keeps its own ledger.'","teach":[{"title":"Internal Energy, U","body":"The internal energy U of a system is the sum of all forms of energy (chemical, electrical, mechanical, etc.) it possesses. J.P. Joule showed that a given amount of adiabatic work always produces the same change of state, however it is done, proving that U is a state function: ∆U = U2 - U1 = w(ad).","visual":"🌀"},{"title":"Sign Convention","body":"By IUPAC convention, work w is positive when done ON the system (internal energy increases) and heat q is positive when transferred FROM surroundings TO the system (internal energy increases). Both are negative in the reverse direction.","visual":"➕➖"},{"title":"The First Law of Thermodynamics","body":"For a general change, ∆U = q + w. This is the mathematical statement of the first law: the energy of an isolated system is constant — energy can neither be created nor destroyed, only converted between forms.","visual":"📐"},{"title":"Adiabatic Walls","body":"An adiabatic wall permits no heat transfer between system and surroundings. In an adiabatic process, q = 0, so ∆U = w(ad) — the change in internal energy equals the work done.","visual":"🧱"}],"questions":[{"kind":"numeric","prompt":"In a process, 701 J of heat is absorbed by a system and 394 J of work is done BY the system (so w = -394 J). What is ∆U in joules?","answer":307,"explain":"∆U = q + w = 701 J + (-394 J) = 307 J."},{"kind":"mcq","prompt":"The first law of thermodynamics is essentially a statement of which principle?","options":["Law of conservation of energy","Law of increasing entropy","Zeroth law of thermodynamics","Third law of thermodynamics"],"answer":0,"explain":"The first law states energy can neither be created nor destroyed — the law of conservation of energy."},{"kind":"mcq","prompt":"No heat is absorbed by a system from the surroundings, but work w is done ON the system. What is ∆U, and what kind of wall must the system have?","options":["∆U = w(ad); the wall is adiabatic","∆U = -w; the wall is conducting","∆U = q - w; the wall is open","∆U = 0; the wall is isolated"],"answer":0,"explain":"With q = 0, ∆U = q + w reduces to ∆U = w(ad), and a wall that blocks heat transfer is called adiabatic."},{"kind":"mcq","prompt":"By IUPAC sign convention, q is positive when...","options":["heat is transferred from the surroundings to the system","heat is transferred from the system to the surroundings","no heat is transferred at all","work is done by the system"],"answer":0,"explain":"Heat flowing INTO the system increases its internal energy and is taken as positive."}]},{"id":"tm3","title":"Pressure-Volume Work & Reversible Paths","icon":"💨","kind":"lesson","story":"A gas-filled cylinder blocks your path, its piston rigged to the Architects' vacuum chamber. To pass, you must calculate exactly how much work the gas does as it expands — whether it rushes into nothing or pushes patiently against a steady wall of pressure.","teach":[{"title":"Pressure-Volume Work","body":"For compression or expansion against a constant external pressure p(ex), the work done on the system is w = -p(ex)∆V. Compression (∆V negative) gives positive w; expansion (∆V positive) gives negative w.","visual":"🛢️"},{"title":"Free Expansion","body":"Expansion of a gas into a vacuum, where p(ex) = 0, is called free expansion. No work is done during free expansion of an ideal gas, whether the process is reversible or irreversible.","visual":"🕳️"},{"title":"Reversible Isothermal Work","body":"In a reversible process, the system changes infinitely slowly through a series of near-equilibrium states with p(ex) always only infinitesimally different from the internal pressure. For isothermal reversible expansion of an ideal gas: w(rev) = -2.303 nRT log(Vf/Vi), and q = -w.","visual":"🔄"},{"title":"Reversible vs Irreversible","body":"A process is reversible if it can, at any moment, be reversed by an infinitesimal change; all other processes are irreversible. Real chemical processes are almost always irreversible.","visual":"♻️"}],"questions":[{"kind":"numeric","prompt":"2 litres of an ideal gas at 10 atm expands isothermally into a vacuum until its total volume is 10 litres (free expansion). How much work is done, in litre-atm?","answer":0,"explain":"Free expansion means p(ex) = 0, so w = -p(ex)∆V = 0 — no work is done regardless of the volume change."},{"kind":"numeric","prompt":"Now the same expansion (2 L to 10 L) happens against a constant external pressure of 1 atm. How much heat, q, is absorbed, in litre-atm?","answer":8,"explain":"q = -w = p(ex)(Vf - Vi) = 1 atm x (10 - 2) L = 8 L atm."},{"kind":"mcq","prompt":"A reversible process is one that...","options":["can be reversed at any moment by an infinitesimal change","always happens instantly","never involves any work","only happens in isolated systems"],"answer":0,"explain":"Reversibility means the system stays essentially in equilibrium, so a tiny nudge can reverse its direction at any point."},{"kind":"mcq","prompt":"For isothermal expansion of an ideal gas into a vacuum, Joule found experimentally that q = 0 and w = 0. What is ∆U?","options":["0","Positive","Negative","Cannot be determined"],"answer":0,"explain":"∆U = q + w = 0 + 0 = 0 for isothermal free expansion of an ideal gas."}]},{"id":"tm4","title":"Enthalpy, Heat Capacity & Cp - Cv","icon":"🔥","kind":"lesson","story":"Most reactions in this world don't happen in sealed bombs — they happen in open flasks under the sky's constant pressure. The Architects introduce you to a new ledger, enthalpy, built exactly for that everyday chaos.","teach":[{"title":"Enthalpy, H","body":"Enthalpy is defined as H = U + pV. At constant pressure, the heat absorbed by the system equals the change in enthalpy: qp = ∆H. ∆H is negative for exothermic reactions and positive for endothermic reactions.","visual":"🌡️"},{"title":"Relating ∆H and ∆U","body":"For reactions involving gases, ∆H = ∆U + ∆n(g)RT, where ∆n(g) is the moles of gaseous products minus moles of gaseous reactants. For solids/liquids the difference between ∆H and ∆U is usually negligible.","visual":"🧮"},{"title":"Heat Capacity","body":"Heat absorbed is q = C∆T, where C is the heat capacity. Molar heat capacity Cm = C/n is the heat needed to raise one mole by one degree; specific heat capacity c is the heat needed per unit mass, so q = mc∆T.","visual":"🌡"},{"title":"Cp minus Cv","body":"At constant volume heat capacity is Cv (qV = Cv∆T); at constant pressure it is Cp (qp = Cp∆T). For one mole of an ideal gas, ∆H = ∆U + R∆T, which leads directly to Cp - Cv = R.","visual":"➕"}],"questions":[{"kind":"mcq","prompt":"At constant pressure, the heat absorbed by a chemical system is equal to...","options":["∆H","∆U","∆S","∆G"],"answer":0,"explain":"By definition, qp = ∆H — that is precisely why enthalpy is so useful for reactions run open to the atmosphere."},{"kind":"numeric","prompt":"1 mol of water vaporises at 1 bar and 100 degC with ∆H = 41 kJ/mol. Using ∆U = ∆H - ∆n(g)RT with ∆n(g) = 1, R = 8.3 J/mol/K and T = 373 K, find ∆U in kJ/mol (2 decimal places).","answer":37.9,"explain":"∆U = 41.00 - (1 x 8.3 x 373 x 10^-3) = 41.00 - 3.096 = 37.904, i.e. about 37.9 kJ/mol."},{"kind":"numeric","prompt":"60.0 g of aluminium (molar mass 27 g/mol, Cp = 24 J/mol/K) is heated from 35 degC to 55 degC. How much heat, in kJ, is needed (2 decimal places)?","answer":1.07,"explain":"n = 60/27 = 2.22 mol; q = n Cp ∆T = 2.22 x 24 x 20 = 1067 J = 1.07 kJ."},{"kind":"mcq","prompt":"For an ideal gas, the relationship between Cp and Cv is:","options":["Cp - Cv = R","Cp + Cv = R","Cp - Cv = 2R","Cp = Cv"],"answer":0,"explain":"Derived from ∆H = ∆U + R∆T for one mole of an ideal gas, giving Cp - Cv = R."}]},{"id":"tm5","title":"Calorimetry: Measuring ∆U and ∆H","icon":"🌡️","kind":"lesson","story":"Deep in the Architects' laboratory sits a sealed steel bomb, waiting to devour a pinch of graphite in pure oxygen. Your job: read the thermometer, trust the heat capacity, and drag a hidden number out of a one-degree temperature rise.","teach":[{"title":"Bomb Calorimeter (∆U)","body":"In calorimetry, a process is carried out in a calorimeter immersed in a known volume of liquid. In a bomb calorimeter, a combustible substance burns in pure dioxygen inside a sealed steel bomb of fixed volume, so no pV work is done — the measured heat is qV = ∆U.","visual":"💣"},{"title":"Constant-Pressure Calorimeter (∆H)","body":"Heat changes at constant (usually atmospheric) pressure are measured in a different calorimeter. Since ∆H = qp, this measured heat is also called the enthalpy of reaction, ∆rH. Exothermic reactions give negative qp; endothermic reactions give positive qp.","visual":"🧪"}],"questions":[{"kind":"numeric","prompt":"1 g of graphite burns completely in a bomb calorimeter (heat capacity 20.7 kJ/K); temperature rises from 298 K to 299 K. What magnitude of heat, in kJ, is released by this 1 g sample?","answer":20.7,"explain":"q = Cv x ∆T = 20.7 kJ/K x 1 K = 20.7 kJ, released (exothermic) since the calorimeter absorbed it."},{"kind":"numeric","prompt":"Scaling that result to 1 mole of graphite (12.0 g), what is ∆U for the combustion, in kJ/mol?","answer":-248,"explain":"-20.7 kJ/g x 12.0 g/mol = -248.4 kJ/mol, i.e. about -2.48 x 10^2 kJ/mol."},{"kind":"mcq","prompt":"Inside a bomb calorimeter, since the volume is fixed, the pV work done during the reaction is:","options":["Zero","Positive","Negative","Impossible to know"],"answer":0,"explain":"With ∆V = 0, w = -p(ex)∆V = 0, so all the measured heat directly equals ∆U."},{"kind":"mcq","prompt":"For C(graphite) + O2(g) -> CO2(g), ∆n(g) = 0. This means:","options":["∆H = ∆U for this reaction","∆H is always greater than ∆U","∆H is always less than ∆U","∆H cannot be calculated"],"answer":0,"explain":"∆H = ∆U + ∆n(g)RT; when ∆n(g) = 0 the extra term vanishes, so ∆H = ∆U exactly."}]},{"id":"tm6","title":"Standard States & Phase-Change Enthalpies","icon":"❄️","kind":"lesson","story":"A wall of ice blocks the corridor, and beyond it a pool of steaming water hisses into vapour. Melt it, boil it, or let it sublime — every change of phase in this world costs (or refunds) a precisely known packet of heat.","teach":[{"title":"Standard States","body":"The standard state of a substance at a given temperature is its pure form at 1 bar (data are usually quoted at 298 K). Standard conditions are shown with a degree superscript on ∆H.","visual":"📏"},{"title":"Enthalpy of Fusion","body":"The standard enthalpy of fusion, ∆fusH, is the enthalpy change accompanying the melting of one mole of a solid in its standard state. For ice, H2O(s) -> H2O(l); ∆fusH = 6.00 kJ/mol. Melting is always endothermic, so all fusion enthalpies are positive.","visual":"🧊"},{"title":"Enthalpy of Vaporisation & Sublimation","body":"∆vapH is the heat needed to vaporise one mole of liquid at constant temperature and standard pressure; for water at its boiling point, ∆vapH = +40.79 kJ/mol. ∆subH is for direct solid-to-vapour conversion, e.g. dry ice at 195 K, ∆subH = 25.2 kJ/mol, and naphthalene, ∆subH = 73.0 kJ/mol.","visual":"💨"}],"questions":[{"kind":"numeric","prompt":"A swimmer's skin carries an 18 g film of water (1 mol), which evaporates at 298 K where ∆vapH for water is 44.01 kJ/mol. How much heat, in kJ, must be supplied?","answer":44.01,"explain":"Heat = n x ∆vapH = 1 mol x 44.01 kJ/mol = 44.01 kJ."},{"kind":"numeric","prompt":"Using ∆vapU = ∆vapH - ∆n(g)RT with ∆n(g) = 1, R = 8.314 x 10^-3 kJ/mol/K and T = 298 K, find ∆vapU in kJ/mol (2 decimal places).","answer":41.53,"explain":"∆vapU = 44.01 - (8.314e-3 x 298) = 44.01 - 2.48 = 41.53 kJ/mol."},{"kind":"numeric","prompt":"1 mol of water at 100 degC cools to 0 degC (Cp[l] = 4.2 J/g degC, 18 g/mol) and then freezes to ice at 0 degC (∆fusH = 6.00 kJ/mol). What is the total ∆H, in kJ/mol?","answer":-13.56,"explain":"Cooling: -(18 x 4.2 x 100) J = -7.56 kJ/mol. Freezing: -6.00 kJ/mol. Total: -7.56 + (-6.00) = -13.56 kJ/mol."},{"kind":"mcq","prompt":"All enthalpies of fusion are positive because melting a solid is always...","options":["endothermic","exothermic","spontaneous only above 500 K","impossible at 1 bar"],"answer":0,"explain":"Melting always requires heat input to overcome the ordered lattice, so ∆fusH > 0 for every substance."}]},{"id":"tm7","title":"Hess's Law & Standard Enthalpy of Formation","icon":"🧮","kind":"lesson","story":"The Architects refuse to let you measure the reaction C(graphite) + O2 -> CO directly — some stray CO2 always sneaks in. Fortunately, enthalpy doesn't care which road you take to a destination, only where you started and where you end up.","teach":[{"title":"Hess's Law of Constant Heat Summation","body":"Because enthalpy is a state function, the enthalpy change for a reaction is the same whether it occurs in one step or several. If a reaction takes place in steps, its standard reaction enthalpy is the sum of the standard enthalpies of the intermediate steps: ∆rH = ∆rH1 + ∆rH2 + ∆rH3 + ...","visual":"🧩"},{"title":"Worked Example: Forming CO","body":"Given C(graphite)+O2->CO2, ∆rH = -393.5 kJ/mol, and CO+1/2O2->CO2, ∆rH = -283.0 kJ/mol, reversing the second (now +283.0 kJ/mol) and adding it to the first gives C(graphite)+1/2O2->CO(g), ∆rH = -393.5 + 283.0 = -110.5 kJ/mol.","visual":"➕"},{"title":"Standard Enthalpy of Formation","body":"∆fH is the standard enthalpy change forming one mole of a compound from its elements in their most stable reference states (e.g. H2 gas, O2 gas, C-graphite). By convention, ∆fH of an element in its reference state is zero.","visual":"🌟"},{"title":"Enthalpy from Formation Data","body":"For any reaction, ∆rH = Σ ai∆fH(products) - Σ bi∆fH(reactants), where ai and bi are the stoichiometric coefficients.","visual":"🔢"}],"questions":[{"kind":"numeric","prompt":"Given ∆fH: CaO(s) = -635.1, CO2(g) = -393.5, CaCO3(s) = -1206.9 kJ/mol, find ∆rH (kJ/mol) for CaCO3(s) -> CaO(s) + CO2(g).","answer":178.3,"explain":"∆rH = [(-635.1) + (-393.5)] - (-1206.9) = -1028.6 + 1206.9 = 178.3 kJ/mol (endothermic — you must heat limestone to decompose it)."},{"kind":"numeric","prompt":"Using Hess's law: C(graphite)+O2->CO2, ∆rH = -393.5 kJ/mol; CO+1/2O2->CO2, ∆rH = -283.0 kJ/mol. Find ∆rH (kJ/mol) for C(graphite) + 1/2O2(g) -> CO(g).","answer":-110.5,"explain":"Reverse the second equation (+283.0 kJ/mol) and add to the first: -393.5 + 283.0 = -110.5 kJ/mol."},{"kind":"numeric","prompt":"For Fe2O3(s) + 3H2(g) -> 2Fe(s) + 3H2O(l), given ∆fH[H2O(l)] = -285.83 kJ/mol and ∆fH[Fe2O3(s)] = -824.2 kJ/mol (Fe and H2 = 0), find ∆rH (kJ/mol).","answer":-33.3,"explain":"∆rH = 3(-285.83) - (-824.2) = -857.49 + 824.2 = -33.3 kJ/mol."},{"kind":"mcq","prompt":"By convention, the standard enthalpy of formation of an element in its most stable reference state is:","options":["zero","one","always negative","different for every element"],"answer":0,"explain":"Elements in their reference states (H2 gas, O2 gas, C-graphite, etc.) are defined to have ∆fH = 0."},{"kind":"mcq","prompt":"Hess's law works because enthalpy is a...","options":["state function","path function","purely intensive quantity","quantity that cannot be summed"],"answer":0,"explain":"Since ∆H depends only on initial and final states, the same total ∆H results whether the reaction proceeds directly or via intermediate steps."}]},{"id":"tm8","title":"Combustion, Bond, Lattice & Solution Enthalpies","icon":"💥","kind":"lesson","story":"A vault of named enthalpies awaits: fuels that burn, bonds that snap, crystals that shatter into gaseous ions, and salts that quietly dissolve. Each has its own signature number, and the Architects want you fluent in all of them.","teach":[{"title":"Standard Enthalpy of Combustion","body":"∆cH is the enthalpy change per mole when a substance burns completely, all reactants and products in standard states. Complete combustion of one mole of butane releases 2658.0 kJ; one mole of glucose releases 2802.0 kJ.","visual":"🔥"},{"title":"Bond Enthalpy","body":"Bond dissociation enthalpy is the enthalpy change when one mole of covalent bonds in a gaseous molecule is broken, e.g. H2(g)->2H(g), ∆H = 435.0 kJ/mol. For polyatomic molecules like CH4, individual C-H bonds differ slightly, so a mean bond enthalpy is used: atomising CH4(g)->C(g)+4H(g) needs 1665 kJ/mol, so mean C-H bond enthalpy = 1665/4 = 416 kJ/mol.","visual":"🔗"},{"title":"Lattice Enthalpy (Born-Haber Cycle)","body":"Lattice enthalpy is the enthalpy change when one mole of an ionic solid dissociates into gaseous ions, e.g. NaCl(s)->Na+(g)+Cl-(g), ∆latticeH = +788 kJ/mol. Because it can't be measured directly, it is calculated indirectly from a Born-Haber cycle summing sublimation, ionization, bond dissociation and electron-gain enthalpies.","visual":"🧱"},{"title":"Enthalpy of Solution & Dilution","body":"∆solH = ∆latticeH + ∆hydH; for NaCl, +788 + (-784) = +4 kJ/mol, so dissolving NaCl in water involves very little heat change. Enthalpy of dilution is the extra heat change on adding more solvent to an existing solution.","visual":"💧"}],"questions":[{"kind":"numeric","prompt":"Combustion of 1 mol of benzene liberates 3267.0 kJ. Using ∆fH(CO2,g) = -393.5 and ∆fH(H2O,l) = -285.83 kJ/mol, calculate ∆fH of benzene C6H6(l), in kJ/mol (2 decimal places).","answer":48.51,"explain":"6(-393.5) + 3(-285.83) = -3218.49 kJ/mol for the elements to CO2/H2O route; adding the reverse of combustion (+3267.0) gives -3218.49 + 3267.0 = +48.51 kJ/mol."},{"kind":"numeric","prompt":"Atomisation of methane, CH4(g) -> C(g) + 4H(g), needs 1665 kJ/mol. What is the mean C-H bond enthalpy, in kJ/mol?","answer":416,"explain":"Mean bond enthalpy = total atomization enthalpy / number of bonds = 1665 / 4 = 416.25, i.e. about 416 kJ/mol."},{"kind":"numeric","prompt":"For NaCl, ∆latticeH = +788 kJ/mol and ∆hydH = -784 kJ/mol. What is ∆solH, in kJ/mol?","answer":4,"explain":"∆solH = ∆latticeH + ∆hydH = 788 + (-784) = +4 kJ/mol — almost no net heat change on dissolving."},{"kind":"numeric","prompt":"HCl.25aq has ∆H = -72.03 kJ/mol and HCl.40aq has ∆H = -72.79 kJ/mol. What is the enthalpy of dilution (kJ/mol) on adding the extra 15 mol of water?","answer":-0.76,"explain":"∆H(dilution) = -72.79 - (-72.03) = -0.76 kJ/mol."},{"kind":"mcq","prompt":"Combustion reactions, by their very nature, are always...","options":["exothermic","endothermic","at equilibrium","zero enthalpy change"],"answer":0,"explain":"Combustion releases heat as bonds reorganize into the very stable products CO2 and H2O, so ∆cH is always negative."}]},{"id":"tm9","title":"Spontaneity, Entropy & Gibbs Energy","icon":"⚖️","kind":"lesson","story":"The final gate only opens for processes that truly want to happen on their own. The Architects warn you: a reaction releasing heat isn't always willing, and one absorbing heat isn't always reluctant — you need a better compass than enthalpy alone.","teach":[{"title":"Spontaneous Processes","body":"A spontaneous process has the potential to proceed without help from an external agency (it says nothing about rate) and is an irreversible process that can only be reversed by an external agency.","visual":"➡️"},{"title":"Enthalpy Alone Isn't Enough","body":"Many spontaneous reactions are exothermic, but some, like 1/2N2(g)+O2(g)->NO2(g), ∆rH = +33.2 kJ/mol, are endothermic yet still spontaneous. So decrease in enthalpy can contribute to spontaneity but is not a sufficient criterion by itself.","visual":"❓"},{"title":"Entropy and the Second Law","body":"Entropy, S, is a state function measuring the disorder of a system; ∆S = q(rev)/T for a reversible change. For a spontaneous process, the total entropy change ∆Stotal = ∆Ssys + ∆Ssurr > 0 (the second law); at equilibrium ∆Stotal = 0.","visual":"🌀"},{"title":"Gibbs Energy","body":"Gibbs energy is defined as G = H - TS, so at constant temperature ∆G = ∆H - T∆S. If ∆G < 0 the process is spontaneous; if ∆G > 0 it is non-spontaneous; at equilibrium ∆G = 0.","visual":"🎯"},{"title":"Gibbs Energy and Equilibrium Constant","body":"For a reaction with all reactants and products in standard states, ∆rG = -RT ln K = -2.303 RT log K. A large negative ∆rG gives a large K (reaction goes nearly to completion); a large positive ∆rG gives a very small K.","visual":"🔑"}],"questions":[{"kind":"numeric","prompt":"For 4Fe(s)+3O2(g)->2Fe2O3(s), ∆Ssys = -549.4 J/K/mol and ∆rH = -1648 x 10^3 J/mol at 298 K. Using ∆Ssurr = -∆rH/T, find ∆Stotal = ∆Ssys + ∆Ssurr, in J/K/mol (1 decimal place).","answer":4980.6,"explain":"∆Ssurr = 1648000/298 = 5530 J/K/mol. ∆Stotal = 5530 + (-549.4) = 4980.6 J/K/mol, positive, so the reaction is spontaneous despite ∆Ssys being negative."},{"kind":"mcq","prompt":"At constant temperature and pressure, if ∆G for a process is negative, the process is:","options":["spontaneous","non-spontaneous","at equilibrium","physically impossible"],"answer":0,"explain":"∆G < 0 is the criterion for a spontaneous process at constant T and p."},{"kind":"numeric","prompt":"For 3/2O2(g) -> O3(g) at 298 K, Kp = 2.47 x 10^-29. Using ∆rG = -2.303RT log Kp with R = 8.314 J/K/mol, find ∆rG in kJ/mol (nearest whole number).","answer":163,"explain":"∆rG = -2.303 x 8.314 x 298 x log(2.47e-29) = +163000 J/mol = +163 kJ/mol — strongly positive, explaining why ozone is thermodynamically unstable relative to O2."},{"kind":"numeric","prompt":"For 2A + B -> C, ∆H = 400 kJ/mol and ∆S = 0.2 kJ/K/mol (both roughly constant with T). At what temperature, in K, does the reaction become spontaneous (∆G = 0)?","answer":2000,"explain":"At ∆G = 0, T = ∆H/∆S = 400/0.2 = 2000 K; above this temperature ∆G turns negative."},{"kind":"mcq","prompt":"The third law of thermodynamics states that the entropy of a perfectly crystalline substance at absolute zero is:","options":["zero","maximum possible","negative","undefined"],"answer":0,"explain":"At 0 K a perfect crystal has complete order, so its entropy approaches zero — this is the third law."}]},{"id":"tm-boss","title":"Final Boss: The Entropy Devourer","icon":"👑","kind":"boss","story":"At the summit of the Architects' tower waits the Entropy Devourer, a swirling storm that has consumed every loose fact you've gathered — system boundaries, Hess's law, calorimeter readings, Gibbs energy. Only a flawless run through the whole chapter of thermodynamics will banish it.","boss":{"name":"The Entropy Devourer","taunt":"Order is an illusion, little chemist — every fact you know will dissolve into chaos before I'm through with you!"},"teach":[{"title":"One Last Recap","body":"Remember the chain: ∆U = q + w (first law) defines internal energy; ∆H = ∆U + ∆n(g)RT extends it to constant pressure; Hess's law lets you build any ∆rH from known steps; and ∆G = ∆H - T∆S finally tells you whether a process runs on its own.","visual":"🧠"}],"questions":[{"kind":"mcq","prompt":"The enthalpies of all elements in their standard reference states are taken to be:","options":["zero","unity","less than zero","different for each element"],"answer":0,"explain":"This is the defining convention behind every ∆fH table."},{"kind":"mcq","prompt":"∆U of combustion of methane is -X kJ/mol (X positive). Since ∆ng for this combustion is negative, ∆H compared to ∆U is:","options":["less than ∆U","equal to ∆U","greater than ∆U","always zero"],"answer":0,"explain":"∆H = ∆U + ∆ngRT; with ∆ng negative, the RT term is negative, making ∆H more negative (less) than ∆U."},{"kind":"numeric","prompt":"Enthalpies of combustion of methane, graphite and dihydrogen at 298 K are -890.3, -393.5 and -285.8 kJ/mol respectively. Find the standard enthalpy of formation of CH4(g), in kJ/mol (1 decimal place).","answer":-74.8,"explain":"∆fH(CH4) = ∆cH(C) + 2∆cH(H2) - ∆cH(CH4) = -393.5 + 2(-285.8) - (-890.3) = -74.8 kJ/mol."},{"kind":"numeric","prompt":"For 2A(g)+B(g)->2D(g) at 298 K, ∆U = -10.5 kJ and ∆S = -44.1 J/K (∆ng = -1). First find ∆H = ∆U + ∆ngRT (R = 8.314 J/mol/K), then ∆G = ∆H - T∆S, in kJ (2 decimal places).","answer":0.16,"explain":"∆H = -10.5 + (-1)(8.314)(298)/1000 = -12.98 kJ. T∆S = 298 x (-0.0441) = -13.14 kJ. ∆G = -12.98 - (-13.14) = +0.16 kJ, so the reaction is not spontaneous at 298 K."},{"kind":"numeric","prompt":"The equilibrium constant for a reaction is K = 10 at T = 300 K (R = 8.314 J/K/mol). Find ∆G in kJ/mol (2 decimal places).","answer":-5.74,"explain":"∆G = -RT ln K = -8.314 x 300 x ln(10) = -8.314 x 300 x 2.303 = -5744 J/mol = -5.74 kJ/mol."},{"kind":"numeric","prompt":"Find the entropy change of the surroundings when 1.00 mol of H2O(l) forms under standard conditions at 298 K, given ∆fH = -286 kJ/mol. Give ∆Ssurr in J/K/mol (1 decimal place).","answer":959.7,"explain":"∆Ssurr = -∆H/T = 286000/298 = 959.7 J/K/mol."},{"kind":"numeric","prompt":"For CCl4(g) -> C(g) + 4Cl(g), given ∆vapH(CCl4) = 30.5, ∆fH(CCl4,l) = -135.5, ∆aH(C) = 715.0, and ∆aH(Cl2) = 242 kJ/mol (all per mole), find the mean C-Cl bond enthalpy, in kJ/mol.","answer":326,"explain":"∆fH(CCl4,g) = -135.5+30.5 = -105.0. Reaction enthalpy = -(-105.0) + 715.0 + 2(242) = 105.0+715.0+484.0 = 1304.0 kJ/mol for all 4 bonds, so mean C-Cl bond enthalpy = 1304.0/4 = 326 kJ/mol."},{"kind":"mcq","prompt":"For a spontaneous process occurring in a truly isolated system, the total entropy change ∆Stotal is:","options":["positive (> 0)","negative (< 0)","always exactly zero","undefined"],"answer":0,"explain":"The second law: spontaneous change in an isolated system always increases total entropy."}]}]},{"id":"equilibrium11","index":5,"name":"Equilibrium","subtitle":"Chemistry Chapter 6","emoji":"⚖️","palette":{"from":"#4ade80","to":"#14532d"},"lessons":[{"id":"eq1","title":"The Sanctuary of Balance","icon":"🧊","kind":"lesson","story":"You enter a sealed crystal sanctuary where ice and water sit frozen in a silent standoff, neither ever fully winning. The Keeper of the Sanctuary will only let you deeper if you can explain why nothing here ever truly stops moving, even though nothing seems to change.","teach":[{"title":"Solid-liquid equilibrium","body":"Ice and water sealed in an insulated flask at 273 K and atmospheric pressure show no change in mass over time, yet molecules constantly cross the ice-water boundary in both directions at equal rates. This is dynamic equilibrium: the temperature at which solid and liquid coexist at 1 atm is called the normal melting (or freezing) point.","visual":"🧊"},{"title":"Liquid-vapour equilibrium","body":"In a closed container, the rate of evaporation eventually equals the rate of condensation, giving a constant equilibrium vapour pressure at a given temperature: H2O(l) <=> H2O(vap). At 1.013 bar, water and its vapour are in equilibrium at exactly 100 degrees C, the normal boiling point. In an OPEN container, escaping vapour disperses into the room faster than it can condense back, so equilibrium is never reached and the liquid eventually disappears entirely.","visual":"💨"},{"title":"Solid-vapour equilibrium","body":"Some solids sublime directly to vapour and back: solid iodine sealed in a vessel fills with violet vapour whose colour intensity becomes constant at equilibrium, I2(solid) <=> I2(vapour). Camphor and NH4Cl show the same behaviour.","visual":"🟣"},{"title":"Dissolution equilibria","body":"A saturated solution is one where the rate of dissolution equals the rate of crystallisation, confirmed using radioactive sugar: dropped into a saturated non-radioactive sugar solution, radioactivity appears in BOTH the solution and the solid crystals over time, proving molecules keep exchanging even at constant concentration. For gases dissolving in liquids (like CO2 in soda water), Henry's law applies: the mass of dissolved gas is proportional to the pressure of gas above the liquid.","visual":"🥤"},{"title":"What all physical equilibria share","body":"Equilibrium is only possible in a CLOSED system at a fixed temperature. Both opposing processes occur simultaneously and at the same rate. Every measurable property (vapour pressure, melting point, solubility, dissolved-gas ratio) stays constant once equilibrium is reached.","visual":"🔒"}],"questions":[{"kind":"numeric","prompt":"Ice and water are sealed in an insulated flask at atmospheric pressure. At what Kelvin temperature are they in equilibrium?","answer":273,"explain":"273 K (0 degrees C) is the normal melting/freezing point of water at atmospheric pressure, where the rates of melting and freezing are equal."},{"kind":"numeric","prompt":"At 1.013 bar pressure in a closed vessel, water and water vapour are in equilibrium at what Kelvin temperature (water's normal boiling point)?","answer":373,"explain":"373 K (100 degrees C) is water's normal boiling point at 1.013 bar."},{"kind":"mcq","prompt":"Why can liquid-vapour equilibrium never be reached in an OPEN watch glass exposed to the atmosphere?","options":["Evaporated molecules disperse into the room faster than they can condense back","Evaporation stops immediately in open air","Open containers have no vapour pressure at all","The liquid instantly boils away"],"answer":0,"explain":"In an open system, the rate of condensation is much lower than the rate of evaporation because molecules escape into a large volume, so equilibrium is never established and the liquid eventually evaporates completely."},{"kind":"mcq","prompt":"In the radioactive-sugar experiment used to demonstrate the dynamic nature of a saturated solution, what is eventually observed?","options":["Radioactivity appears in both the solution and the undissolved solid sugar","Radioactivity stays only in the solid sugar","Radioactivity stays only in the solution","No radioactivity is detected anywhere"],"answer":0,"explain":"Even though the solution is saturated and concentration is constant, molecules keep exchanging between solid and solution, so radioactive sugar molecules spread into both phases over time."},{"kind":"mcq","prompt":"Which of these is a general characteristic shared by ALL physical equilibria discussed (solid-liquid, liquid-vapour, solid-vapour, dissolution)?","options":["Equilibrium is possible only in a closed system at a given temperature","Equilibrium can be reached in any open container","Only one of the two opposing processes occurs at equilibrium","Measurable properties keep changing forever"],"answer":0,"explain":"A closed system at constant temperature is required for true equilibrium; in every case, both opposing processes continue at equal rates, keeping measurable properties constant."}]},{"id":"eq2","title":"Haber's Ghostly Reaction","icon":"🧪","kind":"lesson","story":"A spectral apparatus hums in the next chamber, replaying Fritz Haber's original ammonia experiments over and over. The ghostly chemist insists you prove that his reaction never actually 'stops' at equilibrium, no matter how still it appears, before he lets the apparatus power down.","teach":[{"title":"From physical to chemical equilibrium","body":"For a general reversible reaction A + B <=> C + D, reactant concentrations fall and product concentrations rise until the forward and reverse rates become equal. This is chemical equilibrium: concentrations stop changing not because the reaction stopped, but because both directions now proceed at the same rate.","visual":"🔄"},{"title":"Haber's ammonia experiments","body":"Starting with known amounts of N2 and H2 at high temperature and pressure, Haber found the composition of the N2 + 3H2 <=> 2NH3 mixture became constant over time, even though unreacted N2 and H2 were still present. This constancy signals equilibrium, not completion of the reaction.","visual":"⚗️"},{"title":"Proving the dynamic nature with deuterium","body":"Running the same synthesis with D2 (deuterium) instead of H2 gives an equilibrium mixture of D2, N2 and ND3. When the H2/N2/NH3 mixture and the D2/N2/ND3 mixture are later combined, mass spectrometry reveals scrambled species like NH2D, NHD2, H2, HD and D2 -- proof that the forward and reverse reactions kept running even after 'equilibrium' concentrations were reached.","visual":"🔬"},{"title":"Equilibrium from either direction","body":"For H2(g) + I2(g) <=> 2HI(g), starting from pure H2 and I2 or starting from pure HI alone leads to the SAME equilibrium mixture, provided the total number of H and I atoms in the given volume is the same. Equilibrium can be approached from either side."}],"questions":[{"kind":"mcq","prompt":"What does the deuterium (D2) experiment in ammonia synthesis ultimately prove?","options":["The forward and reverse reactions keep occurring even after equilibrium concentrations are reached","Deuterium never reacts with nitrogen","Equilibrium is a static, unchanging state","Ammonia cannot form from D2 and N2"],"answer":0,"explain":"Finding scrambled H/D species like NH2D and HD after mixing the two equilibrated batches shows that forward and reverse reactions never truly stop -- equilibrium is dynamic."},{"kind":"mcq","prompt":"In Haber's ammonia experiments, why does the reaction mixture's constant composition NOT mean the reaction has 'finished'?","options":["Unreacted N2 and H2 are still present alongside NH3, showing the reaction reached a balance point, not completion","The apparatus was broken","Ammonia decomposes back to N2 and H2 completely","No nitrogen or hydrogen was used in the experiment"],"answer":0,"explain":"At equilibrium, concentrations stop changing because forward and reverse rates are equal -- but reactants are never fully consumed, unlike in a reaction that goes to completion."},{"kind":"mcq","prompt":"For H2(g) + I2(g) <=> 2HI(g), if you start with pure HI alone (no H2 or I2), what happens?","options":["The reaction proceeds in reverse and reaches the same equilibrium mixture as starting from H2 and I2 with matching atom counts","No reaction occurs at all","HI immediately decomposes completely into H2 and I2","Equilibrium can never be reached this way"],"answer":0,"explain":"Equilibrium can be approached from either direction; starting from pure product gives the same equilibrium mixture as starting from pure reactants, provided total atom counts match."},{"kind":"mcq","prompt":"At chemical equilibrium, what is true about the forward and reverse reaction rates?","options":["They are equal to each other and both continue to occur (dynamic, not static)","The forward rate becomes zero","The reverse rate becomes zero","Both rates become infinite"],"answer":0,"explain":"Chemical equilibrium is dynamic: both forward and reverse reactions keep occurring, just at matched, non-zero rates, so net composition stays constant."}]},{"id":"eq3","title":"Guldberg and Waage's Cipher","icon":"🔢","kind":"lesson","story":"An old cipher wheel etched with six sets of H2, I2 and HI concentration data blocks the passage. Only by finding the ONE combination of numbers that stays constant across all six experiments -- exactly as Guldberg and Waage did in 1864 -- will the wheel unlock.","teach":[{"title":"The Law of Chemical Equilibrium","body":"For a general reaction A + B <=> C + D, Norwegian chemists Cato Guldberg and Peter Waage proposed in 1864 that Kc = [C][D] / [A][B] at equilibrium, where Kc is a constant at a given temperature. This is also called the Law of Mass Action, since concentration was once called 'active mass.'","visual":"⚖️"},{"title":"Finding the right expression for H2 + I2 <=> 2HI","body":"Six experiments with varying starting concentrations of H2, I2 and HI were run at 731 K. The simple ratio [HI]eq / ([H2]eq[I2]eq) was NOT constant across the six experiments, but [HI]eq^2 / ([H2]eq[I2]eq) WAS constant every time -- revealing that the exponent in the equilibrium expression equals the substance's stoichiometric coefficient.","visual":"🧮"},{"title":"The general equilibrium constant expression","body":"For a A + b B <=> c C + d D, the equilibrium constant is Kc = [C]^c [D]^d / ([A]^a [B]^b). Phase labels are dropped and concentrations are understood to be equilibrium values.","visual":"📐"},{"title":"Reversing and scaling the equation","body":"The equilibrium constant of the REVERSE reaction is the reciprocal of the forward one: K'c = 1/Kc. If every stoichiometric coefficient is multiplied by n, the new equilibrium constant becomes Kc raised to the power n."}],"questions":[{"kind":"numeric","prompt":"At equilibrium in a sealed vessel at 800 K, [N2] = 3.0x10^-3 M, [O2] = 4.2x10^-3 M and [NO] = 2.8x10^-3 M for N2(g) + O2(g) <=> 2NO(g). Calculate Kc (to 2 decimal places).","answer":0.62,"explain":"Kc = [NO]^2 / ([N2][O2]) = (2.8x10^-3)^2 / (3.0x10^-3 x 4.2x10^-3) = 0.622, approximately 0.62."},{"kind":"numeric","prompt":"At 500 K, a mixture at equilibrium for N2(g) + 3H2(g) <=> 2NH3(g) has [N2] = 1.5x10^-2 M, [H2] = 3.0x10^-2 M and [NH3] = 1.2x10^-2 M. Kc works out to 3.55x10^2 -- enter just the coefficient (the '3.55' part).","answer":3.55,"explain":"Kc = [NH3]^2 / ([N2][H2]^3) = (1.2x10^-2)^2 / (1.5x10^-2 x (3.0x10^-2)^3) = 3.55x10^2."},{"kind":"mcq","prompt":"For the reverse reaction 2HI(g) <=> H2(g) + I2(g), how does its equilibrium constant K'c relate to Kc of the forward reaction H2(g) + I2(g) <=> 2HI(g)?","options":["K'c = 1 / Kc","K'c = Kc","K'c = Kc^2","K'c = -Kc"],"answer":0,"explain":"The equilibrium constant of a reverse reaction is always the reciprocal of the forward reaction's equilibrium constant."},{"kind":"mcq","prompt":"If every stoichiometric coefficient in a balanced equation is multiplied by a factor n, how does the new equilibrium constant relate to the original Kc?","options":["It becomes Kc raised to the power n (Kc^n)","It stays exactly the same","It becomes n times Kc","It becomes Kc divided by n"],"answer":0,"explain":"Multiplying the equation by n means each concentration term's exponent is also multiplied by n, so the new equilibrium constant is Kc^n."},{"kind":"mcq","prompt":"In the H2(g) + I2(g) <=> 2HI(g) experiments, which expression gave a genuinely CONSTANT value across all six trials?","options":["[HI]^2 / ([H2][I2])","[HI] / ([H2][I2])","[HI] / ([H2] + [I2])","[H2][I2] / [HI]"],"answer":0,"explain":"Only [HI]^2 / ([H2][I2]) -- with HI's stoichiometric coefficient of 2 as the exponent -- gave the same constant value in every experiment."}]},{"id":"eq4","title":"The Pressure Vault","icon":"🌡️","kind":"lesson","story":"A vault of gas cylinders demands you convert every concentration-based lock into a pressure-based one. Deep inside, a limestone kiln guards the final chamber, and it only cares about ONE gas in the whole reaction -- ignore the solids, or the vault stays sealed forever.","teach":[{"title":"From Kc to Kp","body":"For gaseous reactions, it's often more convenient to use partial pressures: p = [gas]RT, with R = 0.0831 bar L/mol K. For H2(g) + I2(g) <=> 2HI(g), since the moles of gas don't change (Delta n = 0), Kp = Kc exactly.","visual":"🌡️"},{"title":"The general Kp-Kc relation","body":"For a general reaction, Kp = Kc(RT)^Delta n, where Delta n = (moles of gaseous products) - (moles of gaseous reactants). Pressure must be expressed in bar, since the standard state for pressure is 1 bar.","visual":"📊"},{"title":"Heterogeneous equilibria","body":"When an equilibrium involves more than one phase, the concentration of any PURE solid or PURE liquid is left out of the equilibrium expression, since it stays constant no matter how much is present. For CaCO3(s) <=> CaO(s) + CO2(g), this simplifies to Kc' = [CO2(g)] and Kp = pCO2. At 1100 K, the experimentally measured pCO2 in equilibrium with CaCO3(s) and CaO(s) is 2.0x10^5 Pa.","visual":"🏔️"},{"title":"Another heterogeneous example","body":"For Ni(s) + 4CO(g) <=> Ni(CO)4(g) (used to purify nickel), the solid nickel is omitted, giving Kc = [Ni(CO)4] / [CO]^4. Pure solids/liquids must still be PRESENT for the equilibrium to exist, even though they don't appear in the expression."}],"questions":[{"kind":"numeric","prompt":"For 2NOCl(g) <=> 2NO(g) + Cl2(g) at 1069 K, Kc = 3.75x10^-6 and Delta n = 1. Using Kp = Kc(RT)^Delta n with R = 0.0831 bar L/mol K, calculate Kp (to 3 decimal places).","answer":0.033,"explain":"Kp = 3.75x10^-6 x (0.0831 x 1069)^1 = 3.75x10^-6 x 88.83 = 0.033."},{"kind":"numeric","prompt":"At 1100 K, the equilibrium pressure of CO2 over CaCO3(s) and CaO(s) is 2.0x10^5 Pa. Since 1 bar = 10^5 Pa, what is Kp for CaCO3(s) <=> CaO(s) + CO2(g) at this temperature?","answer":2,"explain":"Kp = pCO2 (in standard-state units) = 2.0x10^5 Pa / 10^5 Pa = 2.00, a dimensionless number."},{"kind":"mcq","prompt":"In the heterogeneous equilibrium CaCO3(s) <=> CaO(s) + CO2(g), which species actually appears in the equilibrium constant expression?","options":["Only CO2(g)","All three: CaCO3, CaO and CO2","Only CaCO3(s)","Only CaO(s)"],"answer":0,"explain":"Since CaCO3(s) and CaO(s) are pure solids with constant concentration, only the gas-phase CO2 appears in Kc' = [CO2(g)]."},{"kind":"mcq","prompt":"For H2(g) + I2(g) <=> 2HI(g), Delta n = 0. What does this mean for the relationship between Kp and Kc?","options":["Kp = Kc exactly, since (RT)^0 = 1","Kp is always much larger than Kc","Kp cannot be calculated for this reaction","Kp = Kc / RT"],"answer":0,"explain":"When the moles of gaseous products equal the moles of gaseous reactants, (RT)^Delta n = (RT)^0 = 1, so Kp equals Kc."},{"kind":"mcq","prompt":"For Ni(s) + 4CO(g) <=> Ni(CO)4(g), which is the correct equilibrium constant expression?","options":["Kc = [Ni(CO)4] / [CO]^4","Kc = [Ni][CO]^4 / [Ni(CO)4]","Kc = [Ni(CO)4][Ni] / [CO]^4","Kc = [CO]^4 / [Ni(CO)4]"],"answer":0,"explain":"Solid nickel is omitted since it's a pure solid; only the gas-phase CO and Ni(CO)4 appear, with CO raised to its coefficient of 4."}]},{"id":"eq5","title":"The Oracle of Q and K","icon":"🔮","kind":"lesson","story":"An oracle in the shape of a great balance scale demands to know which way a reaction 'wants' to go before it has even happened. Learn to read her language of Kc, Qc and Gibbs energy, and she will point the way forward.","teach":[{"title":"What the size of Kc tells you","body":"If Kc > 10^3, products predominate and the reaction proceeds nearly to completion (e.g. H2 + Cl2 <=> 2HCl at 300 K has Kc = 4.0x10^31). If Kc < 10^-3, reactants predominate and the reaction barely proceeds (e.g. N2 + O2 <=> 2NO at 298 K has Kc = 4.8x10^-31). If Kc is between 10^-3 and 10^3, both reactants and products are present in appreciable amounts (e.g. H2 + I2 <=> 2HI at 700 K has Kc = 57.0).","visual":"📏"},{"title":"The reaction quotient, Qc","body":"Qc has the exact same form as Kc, but uses concentrations at ANY moment, not necessarily at equilibrium. Comparing Qc to Kc predicts direction: if Qc < Kc, the reaction proceeds forward (left to right); if Qc > Kc, it proceeds in reverse; if Qc = Kc, the system is already at equilibrium.","visual":"🧭"},{"title":"Worked example","body":"For H2(g) + I2(g) <=> 2HI(g), Kc = 57.0 at 700 K. If at some moment [H2]t = 0.10 M, [I2]t = 0.20 M and [HI]t = 0.40 M, then Qc = (0.40)^2 / (0.10 x 0.20) = 8.0. Since Qc (8.0) is less than Kc (57.0), more H2 and I2 will react to form more HI until Qc rises to equal Kc.","visual":"➡️"},{"title":"Connecting K to Gibbs energy","body":"At equilibrium, Delta G-degree = -RT ln K. If Delta G-degree is negative, K > 1 and the reaction is spontaneous in the forward direction. If Delta G-degree is positive, K < 1 and the forward reaction barely proceeds. For glucose phosphorylation, Delta G-degree = 13.8 kJ/mol at 298 K gives Kc = 3.81x10^-3, showing the reaction proceeds only to a very small extent on its own."}],"questions":[{"kind":"numeric","prompt":"For H2(g) + I2(g) <=> 2HI(g) at 700 K, at some moment [H2] = 0.10 M, [I2] = 0.20 M and [HI] = 0.40 M. Calculate the reaction quotient Qc.","answer":8,"explain":"Qc = [HI]^2 / ([H2][I2]) = (0.40)^2 / (0.10 x 0.20) = 0.16 / 0.02 = 8.0."},{"kind":"mcq","prompt":"In the question above, Qc = 8.0 while Kc = 57.0 at 700 K. Which direction will the reaction proceed to reach equilibrium?","options":["Forward, producing more HI (since Qc < Kc)","Reverse, consuming HI (since Qc < Kc)","The system is already at equilibrium","The reaction cannot proceed further"],"answer":0,"explain":"Since Qc (8.0) is less than Kc (57.0), the reaction moves forward, converting more H2 and I2 into HI until Qc rises to equal Kc."},{"kind":"mcq","prompt":"A reaction has Kc = 4.0x10^31 at a given temperature. What does this large value suggest?","options":["The reaction proceeds nearly to completion; products strongly predominate","The reaction barely proceeds at all","Reactants and products are present in comparable, appreciable amounts","The reaction is at equilibrium only at absolute zero"],"answer":0,"explain":"A Kc value greater than 10^3 indicates the equilibrium mixture is overwhelmingly products, just as in H2 + Cl2 <=> 2HCl."},{"kind":"numeric","prompt":"Using Delta G-degree = -RT ln Kc, if Delta G-degree = 13.8 kJ/mol at 298 K for glucose phosphorylation, Kc works out to 3.81x10^-3. Enter this Kc value as a decimal (for example, enter 0.5 for 5x10^-1).","answer":0.00381,"explain":"ln Kc = -13.8x10^3 / (8.314 x 298) = -5.569, so Kc = e^-5.569 = 3.81x10^-3 = 0.00381."},{"kind":"mcq","prompt":"If Delta G-degree for a reaction is NEGATIVE, what does this imply about its equilibrium constant K?","options":["K > 1, favouring products (a spontaneous forward reaction)","K < 1, favouring reactants","K = 0 always","K cannot be determined from Delta G-degree"],"answer":0,"explain":"Delta G-degree = -RT ln K; a negative Delta G-degree makes ln K positive, so K > 1, meaning the forward reaction is favoured at equilibrium."}]},{"id":"eq6","title":"The ICE Table Trials","icon":"🧊","kind":"lesson","story":"Four sealed reaction chambers await, each holding only the INITIAL concentrations of a mixture. To pass through, you must build the table of Initial, Change and Equilibrium values for each one, and solve for the mysterious 'x' guarding the exit.","teach":[{"title":"The five-step method","body":"1) Write the balanced equation. 2) Tabulate initial concentration, change (in terms of x), and equilibrium concentration for each species. 3) Substitute equilibrium concentrations into the Kc expression and solve for x, picking the root that makes chemical sense (never negative, never exceeding the initial amount). 4) Calculate final equilibrium concentrations. 5) Check by substituting back into the equilibrium expression.","visual":"📋"},{"title":"Worked example: CO + H2O <=> CO2 + H2","body":"At 800 K, Kc = 4.24. Starting with 0.10 M CO and 0.10 M H2O (no products), solving x^2/(0.1-x)^2 = 4.24 gives the quadratic 3.24x^2 - 0.848x + 0.0424 = 0, with roots x = 0.067 and x = 0.194. Since 0.194 would exceed the initial 0.10 M, x = 0.067 is chosen: [CO2] = [H2] = 0.067 M and [CO] = [H2O] = 0.033 M.","visual":"🧪"},{"title":"Worked example: PCl5 <=> PCl3 + Cl2","body":"3.00 mol of PCl5 in a 1 L vessel at 380 K, with Kc = 1.80, gives the quadratic x^2 + 1.8x - 5.4 = 0, solved as x = 1.59. So at equilibrium, [PCl5] = 3.0 - 1.59 = 1.41 M, and [PCl3] = [Cl2] = 1.59 M.","visual":"⚗️"},{"title":"Worked example with pressures: CO2(g) + C(s) <=> 2CO(g)","body":"At 1000 K, Kp = 3.0. Starting with pCO2 = 0.48 bar and pCO = 0, solving 4x^2 + 3x - 1.44 = 0 gives x = 0.33 (after rejecting the negative root). So at equilibrium, pCO = 2x = 0.66 bar and pCO2 = 0.48 - x = 0.15 bar.","visual":"💨"}],"questions":[{"kind":"numeric","prompt":"For CO(g) + H2O(g) <=> CO2(g) + H2(g) at 800 K (Kc = 4.24), starting with 0.10 M CO and 0.10 M H2O, the equilibrium value of x works out to 0.067. What is [CO2] at equilibrium, in M?","answer":0.067,"explain":"[CO2] = x = 0.067 M, since CO2 starts at 0 and gains x mol/L as the reaction proceeds forward."},{"kind":"numeric","prompt":"In the same CO + H2O equilibrium, what is [CO] remaining at equilibrium, in M?","answer":0.033,"explain":"[CO] = 0.10 - x = 0.10 - 0.067 = 0.033 M."},{"kind":"numeric","prompt":"3.00 mol/L of PCl5 is allowed to reach equilibrium at 380 K for PCl5(g) <=> PCl3(g) + Cl2(g), with Kc = 1.80. Solving the quadratic gives x = 1.59. What is [PCl3] at equilibrium, in M?","answer":1.59,"explain":"[PCl3] = [Cl2] = x = 1.59 M, since both start at 0 and increase by x."},{"kind":"numeric","prompt":"For CO2(g) + C(s) <=> 2CO(g) at 1000 K (Kp = 3.0), starting with pCO2 = 0.48 bar and pCO = 0, solving gives x = 0.33. What is the equilibrium pCO, in bar?","answer":0.66,"explain":"pCO = 2x = 2 x 0.33 = 0.66 bar, since 2 mol of CO form for every mol of CO2 consumed."},{"kind":"mcq","prompt":"When an ICE-table calculation produces a quadratic equation with two roots, which root should you choose?","options":["The one that makes chemical sense -- never negative, and never exceeding the initial concentration","Always the larger root","Always the smaller root","Either root works equally well"],"answer":0,"explain":"A negative concentration or one exceeding the starting amount is physically impossible, so the chemically sensible root must be chosen, as in the CO + H2O example where x = 0.194 was rejected."}]},{"id":"eq7","title":"The Counterweight Corridor","icon":"⚖️","kind":"lesson","story":"Every step in this corridor tips a hidden scale -- add weight on one side, and the floor tilts to fight back. You must learn to predict exactly how the corridor 'counteracts' every change you make, or the shifting floor will never let you pass.","teach":[{"title":"Le Chatelier's Principle","body":"A change in any factor that determines the equilibrium conditions of a system (concentration, pressure, temperature) causes the system to shift in a direction that reduces or counteracts the effect of that change. This applies to all physical and chemical equilibria.","visual":"⚖️"},{"title":"Effect of concentration","body":"Adding H2 to the equilibrium H2(g) + I2(g) <=> 2HI(g) makes Qc smaller than Kc, so the reaction shifts forward, consuming the added H2. Continuously removing a product (like liquefying and removing NH3, or removing CO2 from a lime kiln) keeps Qc below Kc and drives the reaction forward indefinitely -- a strategy used in industrial synthesis.","visual":"➕"},{"title":"A colourful demonstration","body":"In Fe3+(aq) + SCN-(aq) <=> [Fe(SCN)]2+(aq) (yellow + colourless -> deep red), adding oxalic acid or HgCl2 removes free Fe3+ or SCN- ions, shifting the equilibrium LEFT and fading the red colour. Adding more potassium thiocyanate (SCN-) shifts the equilibrium RIGHT, intensifying the red colour.","visual":"🔴"},{"title":"Effect of pressure (volume) change","body":"For CO(g) + 3H2(g) <=> CH4(g) + H2O(g), 4 mol of gaseous reactants become 2 mol of gaseous products. Halving the volume (doubling the pressure) shifts equilibrium FORWARD, toward the side with fewer moles of gas. In C(s) + CO2(g) <=> 2CO(g), increasing pressure instead shifts the equilibrium in REVERSE, since that side has fewer gas moles.","visual":"🗜️"},{"title":"Effect of an inert gas","body":"Adding an inert gas like argon AT CONSTANT VOLUME does not change the partial pressures or concentrations of the reacting species, so the equilibrium is completely undisturbed. The reaction quotient only changes if the added gas is itself a reactant or product."}],"questions":[{"kind":"mcq","prompt":"Le Chatelier's principle states that a system at equilibrium, when disturbed by a change in conditions, will shift in a direction that:","options":["Reduces or counteracts the effect of that change","Amplifies the effect of that change","Has no effect on the system at all","Always favours the reverse reaction, regardless of the change"],"answer":0,"explain":"This is the defining statement of Le Chatelier's principle -- the system responds to minimize the disturbance."},{"kind":"mcq","prompt":"Adding more H2 to the equilibrium H2(g) + I2(g) <=> 2HI(g) shifts the equilibrium in which direction?","options":["Forward, toward more HI (since Qc becomes less than Kc)","Reverse, toward more H2 and I2","No shift occurs","It depends on the temperature only"],"answer":0,"explain":"Adding H2 lowers Qc below Kc, so the reaction proceeds forward to consume the added H2 and restore Qc = Kc."},{"kind":"mcq","prompt":"For CO(g) + 3H2(g) <=> CH4(g) + H2O(g), compressing the mixture to HALF its original volume shifts the equilibrium in which direction?","options":["Forward, toward fewer moles of gas (4 mol reactants become 2 mol products)","Reverse, toward more moles of gas","No shift, since pressure doesn't affect gaseous equilibria","It depends only on temperature, not pressure"],"answer":0,"explain":"Increasing pressure shifts equilibrium toward the side with fewer gas moles; here that's the product side (2 mol vs 4 mol)."},{"kind":"mcq","prompt":"Adding an inert gas like argon to an equilibrium mixture AT CONSTANT VOLUME has what effect on the equilibrium?","options":["No effect -- the partial pressures and concentrations of the reacting species are unchanged","It always shifts the equilibrium forward","It always shifts the equilibrium in reverse","It doubles the equilibrium constant"],"answer":0,"explain":"At constant volume, adding an inert gas doesn't change the partial pressures or concentrations of the species actually involved in the reaction, so Qc stays equal to Kc."},{"kind":"mcq","prompt":"In the Fe3+(aq) + SCN-(aq) <=> [Fe(SCN)]2+(aq) equilibrium (deep red complex), adding oxalic acid (which binds up free Fe3+ ions) causes the red colour to:","options":["Fade, as the equilibrium shifts left to replenish the removed Fe3+","Intensify, as the equilibrium shifts right","Stay exactly the same","Turn yellow permanently"],"answer":0,"explain":"Removing Fe3+ by complexing it with oxalate shifts the equilibrium in the reverse direction (dissociating [Fe(SCN)]2+) to replace the lost Fe3+, decreasing the red colour's intensity."}]},{"id":"eq8","title":"The Forge of Fritz Haber","icon":"🔥","kind":"lesson","story":"A roaring industrial forge blocks your path, its dials locked to the exact temperature and pressure Fritz Haber once used to mass-produce ammonia. Set the dials correctly -- and understand why a catalyst never appears on them -- to shut the forge down safely.","teach":[{"title":"Temperature and the equilibrium constant","body":"Unlike concentration or pressure changes, a change in temperature actually changes the VALUE of Kc. For an exothermic reaction (negative Delta H), Kc DECREASES as temperature rises. For an endothermic reaction (positive Delta H), Kc INCREASES as temperature rises.","visual":"🌡️"},{"title":"Ammonia synthesis is exothermic","body":"N2(g) + 3H2(g) <=> 2NH3(g), Delta H = -92.38 kJ/mol. Raising the temperature shifts the equilibrium LEFT, decreasing the yield of ammonia. Low temperature favours high yield, but reactions are too slow at low temperature -- hence the need for a catalyst that speeds up the approach to equilibrium without changing its position.","visual":"🏭"},{"title":"A colour-change demonstration","body":"For 2NO2(g) <=> N2O4(g), Delta H = -57.2 kJ/mol (exothermic), a cold beaker favours the colourless N2O4 (forward, exothermic direction), while a hot beaker favours brown NO2 (reverse direction). Similarly, [Co(H2O)6]3+ (pink) + 4Cl- <=> [CoCl4]2- (blue) + 6H2O is endothermic, turning blue at room temperature but pink when cooled.","visual":"🎨"},{"title":"Catalysts don't shift equilibrium","body":"A catalyst speeds up BOTH the forward and reverse reactions by exactly the same amount, by lowering the activation energy of both through a new pathway. It does NOT change the equilibrium composition, and never appears in the equilibrium constant expression.","visual":"⚡"},{"title":"Industrial optimum conditions","body":"The Haber process uses an iron catalyst at roughly 500 degrees C and 200 atm to balance rate and yield for N2 + 3H2 <=> 2NH3. In the Contact process, 2SO2(g) + O2(g) <=> 2SO3(g) has Kc = 1.7x10^26 (suggesting near-total conversion), yet platinum or V2O5 catalyst is still needed because the uncatalysed reaction is far too slow."}],"questions":[{"kind":"mcq","prompt":"For the exothermic reaction N2(g) + 3H2(g) <=> 2NH3(g), Delta H = -92.38 kJ/mol, what happens to the equilibrium if temperature is RAISED?","options":["It shifts left, decreasing the equilibrium yield of ammonia","It shifts right, increasing the equilibrium yield of ammonia","It has no effect on the equilibrium at all","It only affects the reaction rate, never the position"],"answer":0,"explain":"Since the forward reaction is exothermic, its equilibrium constant decreases as temperature rises, shifting the equilibrium toward reactants and lowering NH3 yield."},{"kind":"mcq","prompt":"In the 2NO2(g) <=> N2O4(g) demonstration (exothermic, Delta H = -57.2 kJ/mol), which colour appears more intensely in the HOT water beaker?","options":["Brown (more NO2), since high temperature favours the reverse, endothermic direction","Colourless (more N2O4)","The mixture turns completely black","No colour change occurs with temperature"],"answer":0,"explain":"Raising temperature shifts an exothermic equilibrium in reverse, favouring the brown NO2 over the colourless N2O4 dimer."},{"kind":"numeric","prompt":"Using an iron catalyst, the Haber process for ammonia synthesis uses optimum conditions of around 500 degrees C and what pressure, in atm?","answer":200,"explain":"Industrial ammonia synthesis typically runs at around 500 degrees C and 200 atm to balance a satisfactory rate against a reasonable equilibrium yield."},{"kind":"mcq","prompt":"What effect does a catalyst have on a reaction's equilibrium composition?","options":["None -- it speeds up the forward and reverse reactions equally, without shifting the equilibrium position","It always shifts equilibrium toward products","It always shifts equilibrium toward reactants","It changes the numerical value of the equilibrium constant"],"answer":0,"explain":"A catalyst lowers the activation energy for both forward and reverse reactions by the same amount, speeding up how fast equilibrium is reached without changing where that equilibrium lies."},{"kind":"mcq","prompt":"The Contact process reaction 2SO2(g) + O2(g) <=> 2SO3(g) has an enormous Kc of 1.7x10^26, suggesting the reaction should go almost to completion. Why is a Pt or V2O5 catalyst still needed?","options":["Because the uncatalysed reaction, although thermodynamically favourable, proceeds far too slowly","Because the equilibrium constant is actually too small without a catalyst","Because SO3 is unstable and decomposes without a catalyst","Because catalysts are needed to change the equilibrium constant's value"],"answer":0,"explain":"A large K value only tells you the reaction is favourable at equilibrium, not how fast it gets there; the uncatalysed oxidation of SO2 is very slow, so a catalyst is used to speed up the rate."}]},{"id":"eq9","title":"Three Faces of an Acid","icon":"🧫","kind":"lesson","story":"Three ghostly chemists -- Arrhenius, Bronsted-Lowry and Lewis -- each guard a different doorway, and each insists THEIR definition of 'acid' is the only correct one. To move on, you'll need to satisfy all three, including one door that opens for a species with no proton at all.","teach":[{"title":"Strong vs weak electrolytes","body":"Substances that conduct electricity in aqueous solution are electrolytes. Strong electrolytes (like NaCl) ionize almost completely, while weak electrolytes (like acetic acid) are less than 5% ionized, existing mostly as unionized molecules in dynamic equilibrium with their ions -- this is ionic equilibrium.","visual":"🔋"},{"title":"Arrhenius concept","body":"Acids dissociate in water to give H+(aq) (or H3O+); bases dissociate to give OH-(aq). This concept is limited to aqueous solutions and can't explain the basicity of substances like NH3, which has no hydroxyl group.","visual":"💧"},{"title":"Bronsted-Lowry concept","body":"An acid is a proton (H+) donor; a base is a proton acceptor. Species that differ by exactly one proton form a conjugate acid-base pair -- for example, Cl- is the conjugate base of HCl, and H3O+ is the conjugate acid of H2O. Water plays a DUAL role: it acts as a base accepting a proton from HCl, but as an acid donating a proton to NH3.","visual":"🔁"},{"title":"Lewis concept","body":"An acid is an electron-pair ACCEPTOR; a base is an electron-pair DONOR. This is broader still: BF3 has no proton at all, yet it acts as a Lewis acid by accepting the lone pair from :NH3, forming BF3:NH3. Species like AlCl3, Co3+ and Mg2+ are electron-deficient Lewis acids; H2O, NH3 and OH- are Lewis bases."}],"questions":[{"kind":"mcq","prompt":"According to the Bronsted-Lowry theory, an acid is defined as a species that:","options":["Donates a proton (H+)","Accepts a proton (H+)","Donates a pair of electrons","Accepts hydroxide ions"],"answer":0,"explain":"Bronsted-Lowry acids are proton donors, and bases are proton acceptors -- a broader concept than Arrhenius's."},{"kind":"mcq","prompt":"What is the conjugate ACID of the Bronsted-Lowry base NH3?","options":["NH4+","NH2-","N2H4","NH3 has no conjugate acid"],"answer":0,"explain":"Gaining one proton turns NH3 into its conjugate acid, NH4+."},{"kind":"mcq","prompt":"What is the conjugate BASE of HSO4- (which can itself act as a Bronsted acid)?","options":["SO4(2-)","H2SO4","HSO3-","SO3(2-)"],"answer":0,"explain":"Losing one more proton from HSO4- gives its conjugate base, SO4(2-)."},{"kind":"mcq","prompt":"In the reaction BF3 + :NH3 -> BF3:NH3, BF3 acts as a Lewis acid even though it has no proton to donate. Why?","options":["Because it accepts the lone electron pair donated by NH3","Because it donates an electron pair to NH3","Because it releases H+ ions in the reaction","Because BF3 is actually a strong Arrhenius base"],"answer":0,"explain":"The Lewis definition of an acid is an electron-pair acceptor -- BF3 is electron-deficient and accepts NH3's lone pair, qualifying it as a Lewis acid despite having no proton."},{"kind":"mcq","prompt":"Why does the Arrhenius concept fail to explain why ammonia (NH3) is basic?","options":["NH3 has no hydroxyl (OH) group to dissociate, which the Arrhenius concept requires for a base","NH3 is actually an Arrhenius acid, not a base","Arrhenius theory only applies to solids","NH3 does not dissolve in water"],"answer":0,"explain":"The Arrhenius concept defines a base strictly as a substance that produces OH- ions by dissociation; since NH3 has no OH group, its basicity needs the broader Bronsted-Lowry (proton acceptor) concept instead."}]},{"id":"eq10","title":"The Well of Ten to the Minus Seven","icon":"💧","kind":"lesson","story":"A bottomless well reflects nothing but a single number, endlessly repeating: 10 to the minus 7. Only by mastering the logarithmic language of pH -- and the strange arithmetic of a nearly-empty solution -- can you read what the well is trying to tell you.","teach":[{"title":"Water's dual role and its ionic product","body":"In pure water, one H2O molecule donates a proton (acting as an acid) while another accepts it (acting as a base): 2H2O(l) <=> H3O+(aq) + OH-(aq). Since [H2O] is constant, it's folded into a new constant, the ionic product of water: Kw = [H3O+][OH-]. At 298 K, [H+] = [OH-] = 1.0x10^-7 M, so Kw = (1x10^-7)^2 = 1x10^-14 M^2.","visual":"💧"},{"title":"The pH scale","body":"pH = -log[H+]. Pure water has pH = -log(10^-7) = 7 (neutral). Acidic solutions have [H+] > 10^-7 M (pH < 7); basic solutions have [H+] < 10^-7 M (pH > 7). Since the scale is logarithmic, a change of just 1 pH unit means a 10-fold change in [H+].","visual":"📏"},{"title":"pKw and the master equation","body":"Taking -log of both sides of Kw = [H3O+][OH-] gives pKw = pH + pOH = 14 (at 298 K). This equation always holds for aqueous solutions at 298 K, letting you find pOH from pH or vice versa.","visual":"➕"},{"title":"Very dilute solutions need care","body":"For an extremely dilute 1.0x10^-8 M HCl solution, you can't just say pH = 8 -- water's own ionization also contributes H3O+ ions. Solving Kw = (10^-8 + x)(x) = 10^-14 gives [OH-] = x = 9.5x10^-8 M, so pOH = 7.02 and pH = 6.98, just barely acidic (not the naively expected pH 8)."}],"questions":[{"kind":"numeric","prompt":"The [H+] concentration in a sample of soft drink is 3.8x10^-3 M. Calculate its pH (to 2 decimal places).","answer":2.42,"explain":"pH = -log(3.8x10^-3) = -(0.58 - 3.0) = 2.42, confirming the soft drink is acidic."},{"kind":"numeric","prompt":"At 298 K, the ionic product of water Kw = [H3O+][OH-] equals what value, in units of 10^-14 M^2 (i.e., enter just the coefficient, 1 for 1x10^-14)?","answer":1,"explain":"Since [H+] = [OH-] = 1.0x10^-7 M at 298 K, Kw = (1.0x10^-7)^2 = 1.0x10^-14 M^2."},{"kind":"mcq","prompt":"Human blood has a pH of 7.4. This means blood is:","options":["Very slightly basic (pH is just above 7)","Strongly acidic","Perfectly neutral, exactly like pure water","Strongly basic, like concentrated NaOH"],"answer":0,"explain":"A pH just above 7 (neutral) places blood in the mildly basic range, consistent with Table 6.5's listed pH of 7.4."},{"kind":"mcq","prompt":"For a very dilute (1.0x10^-8 M) HCl solution, why is it wrong to simply assume pH = 8?","options":["At such low concentrations, H+ contributed by water's own ionization becomes significant and must be included","HCl never ionizes at such low concentrations","The solution is actually a strong base at this concentration","pH cannot be calculated below 10^-7 M"],"answer":0,"explain":"When added acid is comparable to or less than water's own [H+] (10^-7 M), water's autoionization can no longer be ignored, giving a pH close to (but just below) 7, not 8."},{"kind":"mcq","prompt":"At 298 K, pH + pOH equals which constant value?","options":["14 (pKw)","7 (neutral pH)","1 (Kw itself)","0"],"answer":0,"explain":"Taking the negative logarithm of Kw = [H3O+][OH-] = 10^-14 gives pKw = pH + pOH = 14 at 298 K."}]},{"id":"eq11","title":"The Weighing of Weak Acids","icon":"🧪","kind":"lesson","story":"A market of tiny scales lines the corridor, each one measuring how eagerly a different weak acid or base gives up its proton. Weigh HF against ammonia, and learn the strange rule that ties every conjugate pair's strength together, before the scales will balance and let you through.","teach":[{"title":"Ionization constant of a weak acid, Ka","body":"For HX(aq) + H2O(l) <=> H3O+(aq) + X-(aq), Ka = [H+][X-] / [HX]. A larger Ka means a stronger acid. Acetic acid has Ka = 1.74x10^-5; hydrofluoric acid HF has Ka = 3.5x10^-4; hydrocyanic acid HCN has a tiny Ka = 4.9x10^-10 (a very weak acid).","visual":"⚖️"},{"title":"Worked example: HF dissociation","body":"For 0.02 M HF with Ka = 3.2x10^-4, the degree of ionization alpha works out to 0.12 (12%). This gives [H3O+] = [F-] = c*alpha = 0.02 x 0.12 = 2.4x10^-3 M, and pH = -log(2.4x10^-3) = 2.62.","visual":"🧮"},{"title":"Ionization constant of a weak base, Kb","body":"For MOH(aq) <=> M+(aq) + OH-(aq), Kb = [M+][OH-] / [MOH]. Ammonia has Kb = 1.77x10^-5. For a 0.05 M ammonia solution, alpha works out to 0.018 (1.8%), giving [OH-] = 9.4x10^-4 M and pH = 10.97.","visual":"🌊"},{"title":"Ka x Kb = Kw for conjugate pairs","body":"For the NH4+/NH3 conjugate pair, Ka(NH4+) = 5.6x10^-10 and Kb(NH3) = 1.8x10^-5. Their product equals Kw exactly: (5.6x10^-10)(1.8x10^-5) = 1.0x10^-14. Knowing one lets you find the other, and pKa + pKb = pKw = 14.","visual":"🔗"},{"title":"Common ion effect and polyprotic acids","body":"Adding acetate ions (from sodium acetate) to acetic acid SUPPRESSES its ionization, shifting CH3COOH <=> H+ + CH3COO- to the left -- this is the common ion effect, a direct application of Le Chatelier's principle. For polyprotic acids like H2CO3 or H3PO4, each successive ionization constant is smaller than the last (Ka1 > Ka2 > Ka3), because it's harder to pull a positive proton away from an already negatively charged ion."}],"questions":[{"kind":"numeric","prompt":"HF has Ka = 3.2x10^-4. In a 0.02 M solution, the degree of dissociation alpha works out to 0.12. What is [H3O+] at equilibrium, in units of 10^-3 M (i.e., enter 2.4 for 2.4x10^-3 M)?","answer":2.4,"explain":"[H3O+] = c x alpha = 0.02 x 0.12 = 2.4x10^-3 M."},{"kind":"numeric","prompt":"Using the [H3O+] = 2.4x10^-3 M found for the 0.02 M HF solution above, what is its pH (to 2 decimal places)?","answer":2.62,"explain":"pH = -log(2.4x10^-3) = 2.62."},{"kind":"numeric","prompt":"For the conjugate pair NH4+/NH3, Kb(NH3) = 1.8x10^-5 and Ka(NH4+) = 5.6x10^-10. Their product Ka x Kb equals Kw. Enter Kw in units of 10^-14 (i.e., enter 1 for 1x10^-14 M^2).","answer":1,"explain":"(5.6x10^-10)(1.8x10^-5) = 1.0x10^-14 = Kw, confirming Ka x Kb = Kw for any conjugate acid-base pair."},{"kind":"mcq","prompt":"Adding sodium acetate (providing extra acetate ions) to an acetic acid solution shifts the equilibrium CH3COOH <=> H+ + CH3COO- in which direction, and what is this phenomenon called?","options":["Reverse (suppressing ionization) -- the common ion effect","Forward (boosting ionization) -- the salt effect","No shift occurs at all","Reverse, but only because the temperature changes"],"answer":0,"explain":"The added acetate ion is a product of the equilibrium, so by Le Chatelier's principle the equilibrium shifts left, suppressing acetic acid's own ionization -- this is the common ion effect."},{"kind":"mcq","prompt":"For a diprotic acid H2X, why is the second ionization constant Ka2 always smaller than the first, Ka1?","options":["It's harder to remove a positively charged proton from an already negatively charged ion (HX-) due to electrostatic attraction","The second proton doesn't actually exist","Ka2 is always exactly equal to Ka1 in reality","Removing the first proton makes the molecule neutral, so the second is easier"],"answer":0,"explain":"Once HX- carries a negative charge, the electrostatic pull on the remaining proton is stronger, making it harder to remove than the first proton from neutral H2X."}]},{"id":"eq12","title":"The Buffer Sanctum","icon":"🧬","kind":"lesson","story":"A sanctum of blood and body fluids hums quietly around you, its pH refusing to budge no matter what acid or base you pour in. To earn the sanctum's trust, prove you understand WHY salts turn water acidic or basic, and how a buffer resists change altogether.","teach":[{"title":"Hydrolysis of salts","body":"Salts of a STRONG acid and STRONG base (like NaCl) simply hydrate without hydrolysing, giving a neutral solution, pH = 7. Salts of a WEAK acid and strong base (like CH3COONa) hydrolyse to make the solution BASIC (pH > 7), since the acetate ion reacts with water to regenerate some acetic acid and OH-. Salts of a strong acid and WEAK base (like NH4Cl) hydrolyse to make the solution ACIDIC (pH < 7), since NH4+ reacts with water to regenerate NH4OH and H+.","visual":"🧂"},{"title":"Salts of weak acid AND weak base","body":"For a salt like ammonium acetate (weak acid + weak base), pH = 7 + 1/2(pKa - pKb). With pKa(acetic acid) = 4.76 and pKb(ammonium hydroxide) = 4.75, pH = 7 + 1/2(4.76 - 4.75) = 7.005 -- nearly neutral, since the acid and base are almost equally weak.","visual":"🔬"},{"title":"What is a buffer solution?","body":"A buffer solution resists changes in pH when small amounts of acid or base are added, or when diluted. A mixture of acetic acid and sodium acetate buffers around pH 4.75; a mixture of ammonium chloride and ammonium hydroxide buffers around pH 9.25. Buffers are essential for keeping body fluids like blood at a stable pH.","visual":"🩸"},{"title":"The Henderson-Hasselbalch equation","body":"For an acidic buffer, pH = pKa + log([Salt]/[Acid]). When the salt (conjugate base) and acid concentrations are EQUAL, log(1) = 0, so pH = pKa exactly -- this is why you choose an acid whose pKa is close to your target pH. For a basic buffer, pH = pKa(of the conjugate acid) + log([Base]/[Conjugate acid]). A buffer's pH is unaffected by dilution, since the ratio inside the logarithm stays the same."}],"questions":[{"kind":"numeric","prompt":"Ammonium acetate is a salt of a weak acid (acetic, pKa = 4.76) and a weak base (ammonium hydroxide, pKb = 4.75). Using pH = 7 + 1/2(pKa - pKb), calculate its pH (to 3 decimal places).","answer":7.005,"explain":"pH = 7 + 1/2(4.76 - 4.75) = 7 + 1/2(0.01) = 7 + 0.005 = 7.005, essentially neutral since the acid and base are similarly weak."},{"kind":"mcq","prompt":"A salt formed from a strong acid and a strong base, like NaCl, dissolved in water gives a solution that is:","options":["Neutral (pH = 7), since neither ion undergoes hydrolysis","Always strongly acidic","Always strongly basic","Unpredictable without more information"],"answer":0,"explain":"Strong-acid/strong-base salt ions simply hydrate without reacting further with water, leaving the solution neutral."},{"kind":"mcq","prompt":"Sodium acetate (CH3COONa), a salt of a weak acid and a strong base, gives an aqueous solution that is:","options":["Basic (pH > 7), due to hydrolysis of the acetate ion","Acidic (pH < 7)","Perfectly neutral (pH = 7)","It depends only on temperature, never on the salt itself"],"answer":0,"explain":"The acetate ion reacts with water (CH3COO- + H2O <=> CH3COOH + OH-), releasing OH- and making the solution basic."},{"kind":"mcq","prompt":"According to the Henderson-Hasselbalch equation, pH = pKa + log([Salt]/[Acid]), what happens when the salt and acid concentrations in a buffer are EXACTLY EQUAL?","options":["pH equals the acid's pKa exactly, since log(1) = 0","pH is always 7, regardless of the acid used","pH becomes undefined","pH equals pKa plus 1"],"answer":0,"explain":"When [Salt] = [Acid], the ratio is 1 and log(1) = 0, leaving pH = pKa."},{"kind":"mcq","prompt":"A mixture of acetic acid and sodium acetate acts as a buffer around what pH, given acetic acid's pKa of 4.76?","options":["Around pH 4.75-4.76","Around pH 7.00","Around pH 9.25","Around pH 14.00"],"answer":0,"explain":"When the acid and its salt are present in roughly equal concentration, the buffer's pH sits close to the acid's own pKa, about 4.75-4.76."}]},{"id":"eq-boss","title":"The Solubility Sentinel","icon":"👑","kind":"boss","story":"At the very heart of the Sanctuary of Balance stands the Solubility Sentinel, built from crystals of every sparingly-soluble salt you've never quite dissolved. It commands the full power of everything you've learned -- from vapour pressure to buffers to the solubility product itself -- before it will let you leave.","boss":{"name":"The Solubility Sentinel","taunt":"Physical equilibria, Kc, Le Chatelier, pH, buffers -- and now MY specialty, solubility product. Show me you can balance it all!"},"teach":[{"title":"The Solubility Product Constant, Ksp","body":"For a sparingly soluble salt like BaSO4(s) <=> Ba2+(aq) + SO4(2-)(aq), the concentration of the pure solid is constant, giving Ksp = [Ba2+][SO4(2-)]. At 298 K, Ksp(BaSO4) = 1.1x10^-10. If molar solubility is S, then Ksp = S^2, so S = sqrt(1.1x10^-10) = 1.05x10^-5 mol/L.","visual":"💎"},{"title":"Solubility of salts with unequal ion ratios","body":"For a general salt MxXy with molar solubility S, [M] = xS and [X] = yS, so Ksp = x^x . y^y . S^(x+y). For A2X3, Ksp = (2S)^2(3S)^3 = 108S^5; given Ksp = 1.1x10^-23, this gives S = 1.0x10^-5 mol/L, exactly as in Problem 6.26.","visual":"🧊"},{"title":"Common ion effect on solubility","body":"By Le Chatelier's principle, adding a common ion suppresses a salt's solubility. For Ni(OH)2 (Ksp = 2.0x10^-15) dissolved in 0.10 M NaOH (which already supplies OH-), the solubility drops dramatically to S = 2.0x10^-13 M, far below its solubility in pure water -- because the excess OH- pushes the dissolution equilibrium strongly to the left."}],"questions":[{"kind":"numeric","prompt":"The Ksp of BaSO4 at 298 K is 1.1x10^-10. Since Ksp = S^2 for this 1:1 salt, calculate its molar solubility S, in units of 10^-5 M (i.e., enter 1.05 for 1.05x10^-5 M).","answer":1.05,"explain":"S = sqrt(Ksp) = sqrt(1.1x10^-10) = 1.05x10^-5 mol/L."},{"kind":"numeric","prompt":"Ni(OH)2 has Ksp = 2.0x10^-15. In 0.10 M NaOH (a common-ion source of OH-), its molar solubility drops to S = 2.0x10^-13 M. Enter this value in units of 10^-13 M (i.e., enter 2.0).","answer":2,"explain":"With [OH-] already at 0.10 M from NaOH, Ksp = S(0.10)^2 approximately, giving S = 2.0x10^-15 / 0.01 = 2.0x10^-13 M -- far less soluble than in pure water, due to the common ion effect."},{"kind":"numeric","prompt":"For A2X3 with Ksp = 1.1x10^-23, solving Ksp = 108S^5 gives S^5 = 1x10^-25, so S = 1.0x10^-5 mol/L. Enter S in units of 10^-5 mol/L (i.e., enter 1.0).","answer":1,"explain":"A2X3 -> 2A3+ + 3X2-, so Ksp = (2S)^2(3S)^3 = 108S^5 = 1.1x10^-23, giving S^5 = 1x10^-25 and S = 1.0x10^-5 mol/L."},{"kind":"mcq","prompt":"Ni(OH)2 has Ksp = 2.0x10^-15 and AgCN has Ksp = 6x10^-17. Comparing their calculated molar solubilities, which salt is actually MORE soluble?","options":["Ni(OH)2 (its molar solubility, about 0.58x10^-4 M, exceeds AgCN's 7.8x10^-9 M)","AgCN, since its Ksp value is the smaller number","They are equally soluble since both are 'sparingly soluble' salts","Solubility cannot be compared between different types of salts"],"answer":0,"explain":"A smaller Ksp doesn't always mean smaller solubility when the salts have different ion ratios -- Ni(OH)2's stoichiometry (1 cation : 2 anions) actually gives it a much higher molar solubility than AgCN's simple 1:1 ratio."},{"kind":"mcq","prompt":"For the exothermic reaction N2(g) + 3H2(g) <=> 2NH3(g), what happens to the equilibrium yield of NH3 if temperature is increased?","options":["It decreases, as the equilibrium shifts toward reactants","It increases, as the equilibrium shifts toward products","It stays exactly the same","Ammonia yield only depends on pressure, never temperature"],"answer":0,"explain":"Raising temperature always shifts an exothermic equilibrium's Kc downward, favouring reactants and lowering the equilibrium yield of ammonia."},{"kind":"numeric","prompt":"The [H+] concentration in a sample of soft drink is 3.8x10^-3 M. What is its pH (to 2 decimal places)?","answer":2.42,"explain":"pH = -log(3.8x10^-3) = 2.42."},{"kind":"mcq","prompt":"Which theory of acids and bases defines an acid as a species that ACCEPTS an electron pair, even without needing a proton?","options":["The Lewis concept","The Arrhenius concept","The Bronsted-Lowry concept","None of these theories allow proton-free acids"],"answer":0,"explain":"The Lewis concept is the broadest: an acid is any electron-pair acceptor, which is why species like BF3 (with no proton) can still be classified as acids."},{"kind":"numeric","prompt":"3.00 mol/L of PCl5 reaches equilibrium at 380 K for PCl5(g) <=> PCl3(g) + Cl2(g), with Kc = 1.80. Solving the quadratic x^2 + 1.8x - 5.4 = 0 gives x = 1.59. What is [PCl3] at equilibrium, in M?","answer":1.59,"explain":"[PCl3] = [Cl2] = x = 1.59 M, the positive root of the quadratic that makes chemical sense."},{"kind":"mcq","prompt":"A catalyst added to a reaction at equilibrium does which of the following?","options":["Speeds up the forward and reverse reactions equally, without shifting the equilibrium position","Shifts the equilibrium toward products only","Shifts the equilibrium toward reactants only","Increases the numerical value of the equilibrium constant"],"answer":0,"explain":"A catalyst provides a new lower-energy pathway for both directions equally, so it changes only how fast equilibrium is reached, never the equilibrium composition itself."}]}]},{"id":"redoxreactions11","index":6,"name":"Redox Reactions","subtitle":"Chemistry Chapter 7","emoji":"🔋","palette":{"from":"#818cf8","to":"#312e81"},"lessons":[{"id":"rx1","title":"The Alchemist's Old Rulebook","icon":"🔥","kind":"lesson","story":"You step into a crumbling alchemy hall where magnesium ribbons flare into blinding white light and mercury oxide bubbles when heated. The old Alchemist hands you his rulebook and dares you to name what's really happening: is it oxidation, or reduction?","teach":[{"title":"Oxidation, the classical way","body":"Originally oxidation meant the addition of oxygen to a substance, as in 2Mg(s) + O2(g) -> 2MgO(s) or S(s) + O2(g) -> SO2(g). The idea was later widened to include addition of any electronegative element, or removal of hydrogen or an electropositive element.","visual":"🔥"},{"title":"Reduction, the classical way","body":"Reduction was first seen as removal of oxygen, as in 2HgO(s) -> 2Hg(l) + O2(g). The definition grew to include removal of an electronegative element, or addition of hydrogen or an electropositive element, as when CH2=CH2(g) + H2(g) -> H3C-CH3(g).","visual":"🪨"},{"title":"They never travel alone","body":"In 2HgCl2(aq) + SnCl2(aq) -> Hg2Cl2(s) + SnCl4(aq), mercury is reduced while tin is oxidised at the very same time. Chemists realised oxidation and reduction always occur together, which is why the combined process is called a redox reaction.","visual":"⚖️"}],"questions":[{"kind":"mcq","prompt":"In 2Mg(s) + O2(g) -> 2MgO(s), magnesium undergoes:","options":["oxidation (addition of oxygen)","reduction","disproportionation","no change at all"],"answer":0,"explain":"Oxygen is added to magnesium, which is the classical definition of oxidation."},{"kind":"mcq","prompt":"By the classical definition, reduction is best described as:","options":["removal of oxygen, or addition of hydrogen","addition of oxygen only","loss of an electropositive element only","a reaction that releases heat"],"answer":0,"explain":"Reduction classically means removal of oxygen/electronegative element or addition of hydrogen/electropositive element, e.g. 2HgO -> 2Hg + O2."},{"kind":"mcq","prompt":"In H2S(g) + Cl2(g) -> 2HCl(g) + S(s), which species is oxidised?","options":["H2S","Cl2","HCl","S"],"answer":0,"explain":"H2S is oxidised because the more electronegative chlorine is added to hydrogen (equivalently, the electropositive hydrogen is removed from sulphur)."}]},{"id":"rx2","title":"Duel of the Electrons","icon":"⚡","kind":"lesson","story":"A strip of zinc drops into a beaker of blue copper nitrate solution. An hour later the strip wears a coat of reddish copper and the blue has vanished. Something invisible has changed hands here, follow the electrons to find out what.","teach":[{"title":"Electron transfer defines redox","body":"2Na(s) + Cl2(g) -> 2NaCl(s) can be split into two half reactions: 2Na(s) -> 2Na+(g) + 2e- (loss of electrons) and Cl2(g) + 2e- -> 2Cl-(g) (gain of electrons). Oxidation is loss of electrons; reduction is gain of electrons.","visual":"⚔️"},{"title":"Oxidising and reducing agents","body":"An oxidising agent is an acceptor of electrons and a reducing agent is a donor of electrons. Since sodium donates electrons to chlorine, sodium is the reducing agent and chlorine, which accepts them, is the oxidising agent.","visual":"🤝"},{"title":"The competition for electrons","body":"Zn(s) + Cu2+(aq) -> Zn2+(aq) + Cu(s) shows zinc losing electrons to copper ions. Extending this comparison with Cu(s) + 2Ag+(aq) -> Cu2+(aq) + 2Ag(s) gives an electron-releasing order of Zn > Cu > Ag, the seed of the metal activity series.","visual":"🥇"}],"questions":[{"kind":"mcq","prompt":"In the half reaction 2Na(s) -> 2Na+(g) + 2e-, sodium is:","options":["oxidised, because it loses electrons","reduced, because it loses electrons","oxidised, because it gains electrons","unchanged"],"answer":0,"explain":"Oxidation is defined as loss of electrons by a species."},{"kind":"mcq","prompt":"In Zn(s) + Cu2+(aq) -> Zn2+(aq) + Cu(s), zinc acts as the:","options":["reducing agent, since it donates electrons","oxidising agent, since it donates electrons","reducing agent, since it accepts electrons","oxidising agent, since it accepts electrons"],"answer":0,"explain":"Zinc loses electrons to Cu2+, so it is the electron donor, the reducing agent."},{"kind":"mcq","prompt":"Given the order Zn > Cu > Ag for releasing electrons, what happens when a copper strip is placed in silver nitrate solution?","options":["Cu is oxidised to Cu2+ and Ag+ is reduced to Ag(s)","no reaction occurs at all","Ag is oxidised to Ag+ and Cu2+ is reduced to Cu(s)","both metals stay unchanged"],"answer":0,"explain":"Copper releases electrons more readily than silver, so Cu(s) + 2Ag+(aq) -> Cu2+(aq) + 2Ag(s) occurs, turning the solution blue."}]},{"id":"rx3","title":"The Oracle of Oxidation Numbers","icon":"🔮","kind":"lesson","story":"An oracle blocks the passage, speaking only in numbers assigned to atoms. To pass, you must learn her rulebook for oxidation numbers, the bookkeeping trick chemists use to track electron shifts even in covalent compounds.","teach":[{"title":"The basic rules","body":"Every atom of a free element has oxidation number zero (as in H2, O2, Na). For a monatomic ion, the oxidation number equals the ion's charge, so Na+ is +1 and Cl- is -1. The algebraic sum of oxidation numbers in a neutral compound is zero, and in a polyatomic ion it equals the ion's charge.","visual":"📏"},{"title":"Oxygen and hydrogen, with exceptions","body":"Oxygen is usually -2, but it is -1 in peroxides like H2O2, -1/2 in superoxides like KO2, and positive (+2 or +1) when bonded to fluorine. Hydrogen is usually +1, except it is -1 in metal hydrides like NaH and CaH2.","visual":"💧"},{"title":"Stock notation","body":"The oxidation number of a metal can be written as a Roman numeral in parentheses after its symbol. Applying the rules gives FeO as Fe(II)O, Fe2O3 as Fe2(III)O3, CuO as Cu(II)O, and MnO2 as Mn(IV)O2.","visual":"🪙"}],"questions":[{"kind":"numeric","prompt":"What is the oxidation number of copper in CuO?","answer":2,"explain":"Oxygen is -2, so copper must be +2 to make the compound neutral, matching Cu(II)O in Stock notation."},{"kind":"numeric","prompt":"What is the oxidation number of manganese in MnO2?","answer":4,"explain":"With two oxygens at -2 each (total -4), manganese must be +4 for the compound to be neutral, giving Mn(IV)O2."},{"kind":"mcq","prompt":"The oxidation number of oxygen in the peroxide H2O2 is:","options":["-1","-2","0","+2"],"answer":0,"explain":"In peroxides like H2O2 and Na2O2, each oxygen atom is assigned an oxidation number of -1."},{"kind":"mcq","prompt":"The oxidation number of hydrogen in NaH is:","options":["-1","+1","0","+2"],"answer":0,"explain":"Hydrogen is +1 everywhere except in binary metal hydrides such as NaH and CaH2, where it is -1."}]},{"id":"rx4","title":"Forge and Furnace","icon":"🏭","kind":"lesson","story":"Two chambers stand before you: a Forge where elements fuse into compounds, and a Furnace where compounds break apart again. Not every reaction inside is a redox reaction, only the Oracle's oxidation numbers can tell you which.","teach":[{"title":"Combination reactions","body":"A combination reaction has the form A + B -> C. It is a redox reaction only if at least one of A or B is in the elemental form, as in C(s) + O2(g) -> CO2(g), where carbon's oxidation number rises from 0 to +4.","visual":"🔥"},{"title":"Decomposition reactions","body":"A decomposition reaction breaks a compound into two or more parts, and is a redox reaction only if at least one product is elemental, as in 2H2O(l) -> 2H2(g) + O2(g) or 2KClO3(s) -> 2KCl(s) + 3O2(g).","visual":"💥"},{"title":"Not every decomposition is redox","body":"In CaCO3(s) -> CaO(s) + CO2(g), no element changes oxidation number (Ca stays +2, C stays +4, O stays -2), so this decomposition is not a redox reaction at all.","visual":"❌"}],"questions":[{"kind":"mcq","prompt":"Which of these decomposition reactions is NOT a redox reaction?","options":["CaCO3(s) -> CaO(s) + CO2(g)","2H2O(l) -> 2H2(g) + O2(g)","2KClO3(s) -> 2KCl(s) + 3O2(g)","2NaH(s) -> 2Na(s) + H2(g)"],"answer":0,"explain":"No oxidation numbers change in the decomposition of calcium carbonate, so it is not a redox reaction."},{"kind":"mcq","prompt":"For a combination reaction A + B -> C to count as a redox reaction, what must be true?","options":["At least one of A or B must be in elemental form","Both A and B must already be compounds","The reaction must be exothermic","C must be an ionic solid"],"answer":0,"explain":"Only when at least one reactant is an uncombined element can electron transfer (a change in oxidation number) occur."},{"kind":"mcq","prompt":"In 2KClO3(s) -> 2KCl(s) + 3O2(g), which element's oxidation number stays the same throughout?","options":["Potassium (K)","Chlorine (Cl)","Oxygen (O)","All three change"],"answer":0,"explain":"Potassium remains +1 on both sides; chlorine falls from +5 to -1 and oxygen rises from -2 to 0."},{"kind":"numeric","prompt":"In C(s) + O2(g) -> CO2(g), what is the oxidation number of carbon in the product CO2?","answer":4,"explain":"Carbon starts at 0 in the free element and rises to +4 in CO2, since oxygen is -2 and the molecule is neutral."}]},{"id":"rx5","title":"The Displacement Arena","icon":"⚔️","kind":"lesson","story":"In the arena, a more reactive metal steps in to shove a weaker one out of its own salt solution. But watch closely, sometimes a single element fights itself, some of it rising in oxidation state while the rest falls, a trick called disproportionation.","teach":[{"title":"Metal displacement","body":"A more reactive metal displaces a less reactive one from its compound, as in CuSO4(aq) + Zn(s) -> Cu(s) + ZnSO4(aq). This is how pure metals like titanium and chromium are extracted from their ores, e.g. Cr2O3(s) + 2Al(s) -> Al2O3(s) + 2Cr(s).","visual":"🪙"},{"title":"Non-metal displacement","body":"Reactive metals displace hydrogen from water, as in 2Na(s) + 2H2O(l) -> 2NaOH(aq) + H2(g). Among halogens, chlorine displaces bromide and iodide from solution, e.g. Cl2(g) + 2KI(aq) -> 2KCl(aq) + I2(s), the basis of the 'layer test'. Fluorine is too reactive to use this way, it attacks water instead.","visual":"💧"},{"title":"Disproportionation","body":"In a disproportionation reaction, one element in an intermediate oxidation state is simultaneously oxidised and reduced. In 2H2O2(aq) -> 2H2O(l) + O2(g), oxygen goes from -1 in peroxide to 0 in O2 and to -2 in water, all at once.","visual":"🔀"}],"questions":[{"kind":"mcq","prompt":"CuSO4(aq) + Zn(s) -> ZnSO4(aq) + Cu(s) is classified as a:","options":["displacement (metal displacement) reaction","combination reaction","disproportionation reaction","decomposition reaction"],"answer":0,"explain":"Zinc, in the uncombined state, displaces copper from its compound, a classic metal displacement reaction."},{"kind":"mcq","prompt":"In 2H2O2(aq) -> 2H2O(l) + O2(g), oxygen simultaneously goes to a higher and a lower oxidation state. This makes the reaction a:","options":["disproportionation reaction","combination reaction","simple displacement reaction","non-redox decomposition"],"answer":0,"explain":"One element (oxygen, starting at -1) is both oxidised (to 0) and reduced (to -2) at once, the signature of disproportionation."},{"kind":"mcq","prompt":"Which halogen cannot be used to displace other halide ions from aqueous solution, because it instead attacks water?","options":["Fluorine","Chlorine","Bromine","Iodine"],"answer":0,"explain":"Fluorine is so reactive it displaces oxygen from water (2H2O + 2F2 -> 4HF + O2) rather than being used in aqueous halide displacement."},{"kind":"numeric","prompt":"For an element to undergo disproportionation, it must be able to exist in at least how many different oxidation states?","answer":3,"explain":"The reactant is in an intermediate oxidation state, and both a higher and a lower state must be reachable, requiring at least three states in total."}]},{"id":"rx6","title":"The Equation Locksmith","icon":"🔐","kind":"lesson","story":"A sealed door only opens to a perfectly balanced redox equation. Two locksmith methods are on the table: tracking oxidation numbers, or splitting the reaction into two half reactions. Master both before the door swings shut.","teach":[{"title":"Oxidation number method","body":"Assign oxidation numbers, find how much they increase or decrease per atom, and multiply species so the total increase equals the total decrease. For Cr2O7^2-(aq) + SO3^2-(aq) -> Cr3+(aq) + SO4^2-(aq), chromium falls by 3 per atom while sulphur rises by 2, so 2 Cr and 3 S balance the electron count, giving Cr2O7^2- + 3SO3^2- + 8H+ -> 2Cr3+ + 3SO4^2- + 4H2O.","visual":"🔢"},{"title":"Half-reaction (ion-electron) method","body":"Split the equation into an oxidation half and a reduction half, balance atoms other than O and H, then balance O with water and H with H+ (in acid). Add electrons to balance charge, and scale each half so electron counts match before adding them together.","visual":"⚖️"},{"title":"A worked example","body":"Oxidising Fe2+ with dichromate: the oxidation half Fe2+ -> Fe3+ + e- is multiplied by 6, while the reduction half Cr2O7^2- + 14H+ + 6e- -> 2Cr3+ + 7H2O already carries 6 electrons. Adding them gives 6Fe2+ + Cr2O7^2- + 14H+ -> 6Fe3+ + 2Cr3+ + 7H2O.","visual":"🧪"}],"questions":[{"kind":"numeric","prompt":"To balance Cr2O7^2-(aq) + SO3^2-(aq) -> Cr3+(aq) + SO4^2-(aq) in acid, what coefficient is needed for SO3^2- (and for SO4^2-) so the electron change matches chromium's?","answer":3,"explain":"Chromium's oxidation number falls by 3 per atom (x2 atoms = 6), so 3 sulphite ions (each rising by 2) are needed to balance the electron count."},{"kind":"numeric","prompt":"In the balanced equation Cr2O7^2-(aq) + 3SO3^2-(aq) + ? H+(aq) -> 2Cr3+(aq) + 3SO4^2-(aq) + 4H2O(l), how many H+ ions are needed?","answer":8,"explain":"Balancing the ionic charge on both sides of the acidic-medium equation requires adding 8 H+ ions to the left side."},{"kind":"numeric","prompt":"In 6Fe2+(aq) + Cr2O7^2-(aq) + 14H+(aq) -> 6Fe3+(aq) + 2Cr3+(aq) + 7H2O(l), how many electrons are transferred overall from the oxidation half to the reduction half?","answer":6,"explain":"Six Fe2+ ions each lose one electron (6 total), matching the six electrons gained in the dichromate reduction half-reaction."},{"kind":"mcq","prompt":"In the reduction half-reaction Cr2O7^2-(aq) + 14H+(aq) + 6e- -> 2Cr3+(aq) + 7H2O(l), chromium's oxidation number changes from:","options":["+6 to +3","+7 to +2","+3 to +6","+6 to 0"],"answer":0,"explain":"Chromium in dichromate is +6 and is reduced to +3 in Cr3+."}]},{"id":"rx7","title":"Endpoint and the Electron Bridge","icon":"🔋","kind":"lesson","story":"A titration flask waits for its colour to change, and across the room a zinc rod and a copper rod sit in separate beakers, joined only by a salt bridge and a wire. Both scenes run on the same idea: redox reactions doing measurable, usable work.","teach":[{"title":"Redox titrations","body":"In redox titrations, permanganate ion (MnO4-) is intensely coloured and acts as its own indicator: the first lasting tinge of pink signals that all the reductant (Fe2+ or C2O4^2-) has been consumed. Dichromate is not self-indicating but turns diphenylamine blue at the endpoint.","visual":"🧪"},{"title":"Iodometric titrations","body":"Cu2+ oxidises iodide to iodine (2Cu2+ + 4I- -> Cu2I2 + I2), and the liberated iodine gives an intense blue colour with starch. Iodine reacts specifically with thiosulphate, I2 + 2S2O3^2- -> 2I- + S4O6^2-, so the blue colour disappearing marks the endpoint.","visual":"🔵"},{"title":"Redox couples and the Daniell cell","body":"Separating Zn(s) + Cu2+(aq) -> Zn2+(aq) + Cu(s) into two beakers linked by a salt bridge and a wire (a Daniell cell) forces electrons to flow through the external circuit instead of directly. A redox couple, like Zn2+/Zn, is written oxidised form before reduced form.","visual":"🔌"},{"title":"Standard electrode potential","body":"Measured at unit concentration, 1 atm, and 298 K, the standard hydrogen electrode is fixed at 0.00 V by convention. From Table 7.1, F2(g) + 2e- -> 2F- has the highest value at +2.87 V (strongest oxidant), while Li+ + e- -> Li(s) is deeply negative (strongest reductant among common metals).","visual":"🔋"}],"questions":[{"kind":"mcq","prompt":"In a titration using acidic KMnO4 as the oxidant, how is the endpoint usually detected?","options":["MnO4- itself acts as a self-indicator, giving a lasting faint pink colour","starch is added and turns deep blue","the solution becomes permanently colourless","a separate pH indicator is required"],"answer":0,"explain":"Permanganate is intensely coloured, so the first lasting pink tinge after all the reductant is oxidised marks the endpoint."},{"kind":"mcq","prompt":"In an iodometric titration with starch as indicator, the endpoint is signalled by:","options":["the intense blue starch-iodine colour disappearing","the solution turning permanently blue","a red precipitate forming","no colour change occurring at all"],"answer":0,"explain":"As thiosulphate consumes the last of the iodine (I2 + 2S2O3^2- -> 2I- + S4O6^2-), the blue starch-iodine colour vanishes."},{"kind":"mcq","prompt":"A redox couple such as Zn2+/Zn is written with:","options":["the oxidised form before the reduced form","the reduced form before the oxidised form","whichever form has lower concentration first","no fixed convention"],"answer":0,"explain":"By convention the oxidised form is placed before the reduced form, separated by a slash, e.g. Zn2+/Zn."},{"kind":"numeric","prompt":"According to Table 7.1, what is the standard electrode potential (in volts) of F2(g) + 2e- -> 2F-?","answer":2.87,"explain":"Fluorine's reduction couple has the highest listed standard electrode potential, +2.87 V, making it the strongest oxidising agent in the table."},{"kind":"numeric","prompt":"By convention, what is the standard electrode potential (in volts) of the hydrogen electrode 2H+ + 2e- -> H2(g)?","answer":0,"explain":"The standard hydrogen electrode is the reference point and is defined as 0.00 V."}]},{"id":"rx-boss","title":"The Electron Thief's Vault","icon":"👑","kind":"boss","story":"Every rulebook, every half reaction, every colour change you've learned converges here. The Electron Thief guards the vault, daring you to prove that nothing in this chapter, from classical oxygen counting to the Daniell cell, can slip past you.","boss":{"name":"The Electron Thief","taunt":"Your electrons are mine now! Track every shift I make, oxidation number by oxidation number, or lose this chapter to me forever."},"teach":[{"title":"One chapter, three viewpoints","body":"Redox reactions can be understood classically (addition/removal of oxygen, hydrogen or electronegative/electropositive elements), electronically (loss or gain of electrons), or through oxidation numbers (a bookkeeping increase or decrease). All three describe the same underlying change.","visual":"🧩"}],"questions":[{"kind":"mcq","prompt":"In 2Cu2O(s) + Cu2S(s) -> 6Cu(s) + SO2(g), why is this a redox reaction?","options":["Copper's oxidation number falls from +1 to 0 while sulphur's rises from -2 to +4","No oxidation numbers change in the reaction","Only copper's oxidation number changes","It is purely an acid-base reaction"],"answer":0,"explain":"Copper is reduced from +1 to 0 and sulphur is oxidised from -2 to +4, confirming a redox change."},{"kind":"mcq","prompt":"2H2O2(aq) -> 2H2O(l) + O2(g) is classified as a:","options":["disproportionation reaction","combination reaction","metal displacement reaction","non-redox decomposition"],"answer":0,"explain":"Oxygen in peroxide (-1) is simultaneously oxidised to 0 (in O2) and reduced to -2 (in H2O)."},{"kind":"numeric","prompt":"In 6Fe2+(aq) + Cr2O7^2-(aq) + 14H+(aq) -> 6Fe3+(aq) + 2Cr3+(aq) + 7H2O(l), how many water molecules are produced?","answer":7,"explain":"Balancing the 14 H+ ions and 7 oxygen atoms from dichromate produces 7 H2O molecules on the product side."},{"kind":"mcq","prompt":"Cr2O3(s) + 2Al(s) -> Al2O3(s) + 2Cr(s) proceeds because:","options":["aluminium is a better reducing agent (loses electrons more readily) than chromium","aluminium is denser than chromium","chromium(III) oxide is unstable at room temperature on its own","aluminium has a higher oxidation number than chromium"],"answer":0,"explain":"In metal displacement reactions, the reducing metal used must have a greater tendency to lose electrons than the metal being displaced."},{"kind":"numeric","prompt":"According to Table 7.1, what is the standard electrode potential (in volts) of the Cu2+/Cu couple?","answer":0.34,"explain":"The table lists Cu2+ + 2e- -> Cu(s) at +0.34 V."},{"kind":"mcq","prompt":"In a Daniell cell (Zn | ZnSO4 || CuSO4 | Cu), electrons in the external wire flow from:","options":["the zinc electrode (anode) to the copper electrode (cathode)","the copper electrode to the zinc electrode","directly through the salt bridge","they do not flow through the wire at all"],"answer":0,"explain":"Zinc is oxidised at the anode, releasing electrons that travel through the external wire to the copper cathode."},{"kind":"mcq","prompt":"Which reagent acts as its own indicator in redox titrations because of its intense colour?","options":["MnO4- (permanganate ion)","Fe2+","C2O4^2- (oxalate ion)","Cr3+"],"answer":0,"explain":"Permanganate's own deep purple colour lets it self-indicate the endpoint of a titration."},{"kind":"numeric","prompt":"What is the oxidation number of manganese in the permanganate ion, MnO4-?","answer":7,"explain":"Four oxygens contribute -8; for the ion's total charge to be -1, manganese must be +7."}]}]},{"id":"organicchemistry11","index":7,"name":"Organic Chemistry: Basic Principles","subtitle":"Chemistry Chapter 8","emoji":"⬡","palette":{"from":"#1f2937","to":"#000000"},"lessons":[{"id":"oc1","title":"The Vital Force Myth","icon":"🧪","kind":"lesson","story":"A spectral barrier blocks your path, chanting that only living things can weave carbon into compounds. Two hundred years ago chemists believed the same thing — until one of them proved it wrong in a flask.","teach":[{"title":"The Vital Force Theory","body":"Around two hundred years ago, chemists began distinguishing organic compounds (obtained from plants and animals) from inorganic compounds (prepared from mineral sources). Berzelius, a Swedish chemist, proposed that a mysterious 'vital force' was responsible for the formation of organic compounds, meaning only living organisms could make them.","visual":"👻"},{"title":"Wohler Breaks the Spell","body":"The vital force notion was rejected when F. Wohler synthesised the organic compound urea, NH2CONH2, simply by heating the inorganic compound ammonium cyanate, NH4CNO. This was the first proof that an organic compound could be made from an inorganic starting material in a laboratory.","visual":"⚗️"},{"title":"More Proof Follows","body":"The pioneering synthesis of acetic acid by Kolbe and of methane by Berthelot showed conclusively that organic compounds could be synthesised from inorganic sources in a laboratory, closing the door on the vital force idea for good.","visual":"🔬"},{"title":"What Organic Chemistry Studies","body":"Carbon has the unique property of catenation, forming covalent bonds with other carbon atoms, as well as with hydrogen, oxygen, nitrogen, sulphur, phosphorus and halogens. The resulting compounds are studied under the branch of chemistry called organic chemistry, and its modern shape was ushered in by the electronic theory of covalent bonding.","visual":"⛓️"}],"questions":[{"kind":"mcq","prompt":"Who proposed the 'vital force theory', claiming organic compounds could only be formed by living organisms?","options":["Berzelius","Wohler","Kolbe","Berthelot"],"answer":0,"explain":"Berzelius, a Swedish chemist, proposed the vital force theory before it was disproved."},{"kind":"mcq","prompt":"Which inorganic compound did Wohler convert into the organic compound urea?","options":["Ammonium cyanate","Ammonium sulphate","Ammonium nitrate","Ammonium chloride"],"answer":0,"explain":"Wohler heated ammonium cyanate (NH4CNO) to obtain urea (NH2CONH2), disproving the vital force theory."},{"kind":"mcq","prompt":"What unique property of carbon lets it form long chains by bonding with other carbon atoms?","options":["Catenation","Isomerism","Hybridisation","Resonance"],"answer":0,"explain":"Catenation is carbon's ability to form covalent bonds with other carbon atoms, building chains and rings."},{"kind":"mcq","prompt":"The lab synthesis of urea, acetic acid, and methane from inorganic starting materials proved that organic compounds:","options":["Can be synthesised from inorganic compounds in a laboratory","Require a vital force from living organisms","Occur only in plants and animals","Cannot be studied using chemical methods"],"answer":0,"explain":"These syntheses conclusively disproved the vital force theory."}]},{"id":"oc2","title":"Tetravalence of Carbon","icon":"🔺","kind":"lesson","story":"The chamber ahead reshapes itself around you — sometimes a tetrahedron, sometimes flat as a blade, sometimes a straight spear. To pass, you must recognise which hybrid orbitals carbon is wearing.","teach":[{"title":"sp3 Carbon: Methane","body":"The tetrahedral shape of methane (CH4), the planar shape of ethene (CH2=CH2), and the linear shape of ethyne (HC≡CH) are explained by carbon using sp3, sp2 and sp hybrid orbitals respectively. An sp3 hybrid orbital overlaps with the s orbital of hydrogen to give a carbon-hydrogen sigma (σ) bond.","visual":"🔷"},{"title":"sp2 and sp Carbons","body":"Overlap of an sp3 orbital of one carbon with an sp3 orbital of another carbon gives a carbon-carbon sigma bond. The unhybridised p orbitals on two adjacent carbons can undergo lateral, side-by-side overlap to give a pi (π) bond, as seen in ethene (sp2) and ethyne (sp, which has two pi bonds).","visual":"🔶"},{"title":"s-Character and Electronegativity","body":"The sp hybrid orbital has more s-character than sp2, and sp2 has more than sp3; greater s-character means the orbital is closer to the nucleus and forms shorter, stronger bonds. Greater s-character also means greater electronegativity, so an sp-hybridised carbon is more electronegative than an sp2 or sp3 carbon.","visual":"⚡"},{"title":"Reactive Pi Bonds","body":"In pi bond formation, the two p orbitals on adjacent atoms must be parallel for proper sideways overlap, which restricts rotation about a carbon-carbon double bond. The electron charge cloud of the pi bond lies above and below the plane of the bonded atoms, making those electrons easily available to attacking reagents — so pi bonds provide the most reactive centres in molecules with multiple bonds.","visual":"🌀"}],"questions":[{"kind":"mcq","prompt":"What is the shape of the methane (CH4) molecule?","options":["Tetrahedral","Trigonal planar","Linear","Pyramidal"],"answer":0,"explain":"Methane's carbon is sp3-hybridised, giving it a tetrahedral shape."},{"kind":"mcq","prompt":"In ethyne, HC≡CH, what hybridisation does each carbon atom show?","options":["sp","sp2","sp3","sp3d"],"answer":0,"explain":"Ethyne's linear shape comes from sp hybridisation of each carbon."},{"kind":"mcq","prompt":"Why is an sp-hybridised carbon atom more electronegative than an sp3-hybridised carbon atom?","options":["It has greater s-character in its hybrid orbital","It has fewer electrons overall","It always carries a formal positive charge","It is physically larger"],"answer":0,"explain":"Greater s-character pulls the hybrid orbital closer to the nucleus, increasing electronegativity."},{"kind":"numeric","prompt":"How many sigma (σ) bonds does the tetravalent carbon atom form with hydrogen in one methane (CH4) molecule?","answer":4,"explain":"Carbon's four sp3 hybrid orbitals each overlap with a hydrogen s orbital, forming four C-H sigma bonds."}]},{"id":"oc3","title":"Cipher of Structures","icon":"🧩","kind":"lesson","story":"An old archive stores every molecule as a coded scroll — full dot structures, condensed shorthand, and bare zig-zag lines. Learn to read all three, or the archive's secrets stay locked.","teach":[{"title":"Complete Structural Formulas","body":"A Lewis (dot) structure can be simplified by representing the two-electron covalent bond with a dash: a single dash for a single bond, a double dash for a double bond, and a triple dash for a triple bond. Ethane, ethene, ethyne and methanol drawn this way are called complete structural formulas.","visual":"✏️"},{"title":"Condensed Formulas","body":"Structural formulas can be abbreviated further by omitting some or all bond dashes and using a subscript to show the number of identical groups attached to an atom. For example, a long chain like CH3CH2CH2CH2CH2CH2CH2CH3 can be condensed to CH3(CH2)6CH3.","visual":"📝"},{"title":"Bond-Line Formulas","body":"In bond-line structural formulas, carbon and hydrogen atoms are not shown, and carbon-carbon bonds are drawn as zig-zag lines. Only atoms like oxygen, chlorine and nitrogen are written explicitly; line terminals denote methyl (-CH3) groups unless marked otherwise, and line junctions denote carbon atoms bonded to enough hydrogens to satisfy their valency. Cyclic compounds like cyclopropane and cyclopentane are drawn as simple polygons.","visual":"📐"},{"title":"Wedge-and-Dash: Seeing in 3D","body":"The three-dimensional structure of organic molecules can be shown on paper using solid-wedge and dashed-wedge formulas. A solid wedge shows a bond projecting out of the plane of the paper toward the observer; a dashed wedge shows a bond projecting away from the observer; a normal line shows a bond lying in the plane of the paper.","visual":"📄"},{"title":"Molecular Models","body":"Physical molecular models help visualise 3D shapes: the framework model shows only the bonds (not the atoms); the ball-and-stick model shows both atoms (balls) and bonds (sticks), using springs instead of sticks for double bonds like in ethene (ball-and-spring model); the space-filling model emphasises each atom's relative size based on its van der Waals radius, and does not show bonds at all.","visual":"⚛️"}],"questions":[{"kind":"mcq","prompt":"In a bond-line structural formula, what do unlabeled line junctions (corners) represent?","options":["Carbon atoms bonded to enough hydrogens to satisfy their valency","Oxygen atoms","Empty space with no atom","Hydrogen atoms"],"answer":0,"explain":"Line junctions in bond-line formulas stand for carbon atoms carrying the hydrogens needed to complete their valency."},{"kind":"mcq","prompt":"In wedge-and-dash notation, what does a dashed wedge represent?","options":["A bond projecting away from the observer, behind the plane of paper","A bond in the plane of the paper","A bond projecting toward the observer, out of the plane of paper","A double bond"],"answer":0,"explain":"The dashed wedge depicts a bond going into the page, away from the viewer."},{"kind":"mcq","prompt":"Which molecular model shows the relative size of atoms using their van der Waals radius, without showing bonds?","options":["Space-filling model","Framework model","Ball-and-stick model","Wedge-dash formula"],"answer":0,"explain":"The space-filling model conveys the volume occupied by each atom but leaves out the bonds."},{"kind":"mcq","prompt":"In a bond-line formula, what do the terminal points (ends) of lines usually represent, unless marked otherwise by a functional group?","options":["A methyl (-CH3) group","A hydroxyl (-OH) group","A single hydrogen atom","A carboxylic acid group"],"answer":0,"explain":"Line terminals in bond-line formulas denote -CH3 groups unless a functional group is explicitly shown."}]},{"id":"oc4","title":"Sorting the Compound Realms","icon":"🗂️","kind":"lesson","story":"The realm splits into open roads and closed loops. Some loops even hide a stranger inside their ring of carbons. Sort every traveller correctly to earn passage.","teach":[{"title":"Acyclic (Aliphatic) Compounds","body":"Acyclic or open-chain compounds, also called aliphatic compounds, consist of straight or branched chains, for example ethane, isobutane, and acetic acid.","visual":"➖"},{"title":"Alicyclic and Heterocyclic Rings","body":"Cyclic or closed-chain compounds include alicyclic (aliphatic cyclic) compounds, which contain carbon atoms joined in a ring, such as cyclopropane, cyclohexane and cyclohexene, and exhibit properties similar to aliphatic compounds. Sometimes atoms other than carbon are present in the ring; such compounds, like tetrahydrofuran, are called heterocyclic.","visual":"⭕"},{"title":"Aromatic Compounds","body":"Aromatic compounds are special ring compounds including benzene and other related benzenoid ring compounds like aniline and naphthalene, plus non-benzenoid aromatic compounds like tropone. Aromatic compounds may also have a heteroatom in the ring, in which case they are called heterocyclic aromatic compounds, such as furan, thiophene and pyridine.","visual":"💍"},{"title":"Functional Groups","body":"A functional group is an atom or group of atoms joined to the carbon chain that is responsible for the characteristic chemical properties of the organic compound, such as the hydroxyl group (-OH), the aldehyde group (-CHO), and the carboxylic acid group (-COOH).","visual":"🔑"},{"title":"Homologous Series","body":"A group or series of organic compounds, each containing the same characteristic functional group, forms a homologous series, and its members are called homologues; successive members differ from each other by a -CH2- unit. Examples include alkanes, alkenes, alkynes, haloalkanes, alkanols, alkanals, alkanones, alkanoic acids, and amines. A compound may also contain two or more identical or different functional groups, giving rise to polyfunctional compounds.","visual":"📶"}],"questions":[{"kind":"mcq","prompt":"Compounds like cyclohexane and cyclopropane, where carbon atoms are joined to form a ring, are classified as:","options":["Alicyclic compounds","Aromatic compounds","Acyclic compounds","Heterocyclic aromatic compounds"],"answer":0,"explain":"Alicyclic (aliphatic cyclic) compounds contain carbon atoms joined in a ring and behave much like aliphatic compounds."},{"kind":"mcq","prompt":"Tetrahydrofuran, which contains an oxygen atom within its ring, is an example of a:","options":["Heterocyclic compound","Benzenoid aromatic compound","Purely acyclic compound","Non-benzenoid aromatic compound"],"answer":0,"explain":"When atoms other than carbon appear in the ring, the compound is called heterocyclic."},{"kind":"mcq","prompt":"What is a series of compounds called if each member contains the same functional group and successive members differ by a -CH2- unit?","options":["A homologous series","An isomeric series","A resonance series","A catenated series"],"answer":0,"explain":"Such a series is a homologous series, and its members are called homologues."},{"kind":"mcq","prompt":"Pyridine and thiophene, which contain a heteroatom inside an aromatic ring, are classified as:","options":["Heterocyclic aromatic compounds","Alicyclic compounds","Benzenoid aromatic compounds","Acyclic aliphatic compounds"],"answer":0,"explain":"Aromatic rings containing a heteroatom, like pyridine and thiophene, are heterocyclic aromatic compounds."}]},{"id":"oc5","title":"The Naming Council: Alkanes","icon":"📛","kind":"lesson","story":"An ancient council of IUPAC scribes demands every alkane be named exactly by their rules, or the door to the next hall stays sealed. Learn their laws of chains, branches, and lowest numbers.","teach":[{"title":"Straight-Chain Alkanes","body":"Saturated hydrocarbons containing only carbon-carbon single bonds are called alkanes. Straight-chain alkane names end with the suffix '-ane' and carry a prefix showing the number of carbon atoms in the chain, except from CH4 to C4H10, where the prefixes come from trivial names.","visual":"🔗"},{"title":"Alkyl Groups","body":"An alkyl group is derived from a saturated hydrocarbon by removing one hydrogen atom from a carbon, and is named by substituting '-yl' for '-ane' in the corresponding alkane name; for example, CH4 becomes -CH3, the methyl group. Branched alkyl groups have their own trivial names too, such as isopropyl, sec-butyl, isobutyl, tert-butyl, and neopentyl (-CH2C(CH3)3).","visual":"🌿"},{"title":"Finding the Parent Chain","body":"To name a branched alkane, first identify the longest carbon chain in the molecule; this becomes the parent or root chain. If two chains of equal length are found, the chain with more side chains is selected as the parent.","visual":"🌳"},{"title":"Lowest Locants Rule","body":"The carbon atoms of the parent chain are numbered so that the branched carbon atoms get the lowest possible numbers. Where two substituents sit in equivalent positions, the lower number is given to the one that comes first in alphabetical order. When naming a branched alkyl group itself, the carbon atom that attaches to the root chain is always numbered 1.","visual":"🔢"},{"title":"Multiple Identical Groups","body":"If two or more identical substituent groups are present, their number is shown using prefixes di- (2), tri- (3), tetra- (4), penta- (5), hexa- (6), and so on, with locants separated by commas; but these multiplying prefixes are ignored when arranging substituent names alphabetically. The prefixes iso- and neo- ARE considered part of the alkyl group's fundamental name for alphabetisation, while sec- and tert- are NOT.","visual":"✖️"}],"questions":[{"kind":"mcq","prompt":"The branched carbon unit -CH2C(CH3)3 is given the trivial name:","options":["Neopentyl group","Isopropyl group","sec-Butyl group","tert-Butyl group"],"answer":0,"explain":"This structural unit, -CH2C(CH3)3, is specifically called the neopentyl group."},{"kind":"mcq","prompt":"When numbering a branched alkane's parent chain, the numbering is chosen so that:","options":["The branched carbon atoms get the lowest possible numbers","The longest branch is always numbered 1","Numbering always proceeds left to right","Substituents are ignored while numbering"],"answer":0,"explain":"IUPAC rules require numbering that gives branch points the lowest possible locants."},{"kind":"mcq","prompt":"If two substituents sit in equivalent positions on a chain, which one is given the lower locant number?","options":["The one that comes first in alphabetical order","The one with the larger structure","The one that is a halogen","The one named with 'tert-'"],"answer":0,"explain":"When positions are equivalent, alphabetical order decides which substituent gets the lower number."},{"kind":"numeric","prompt":"In the IUPAC name 4-ethyl-2,2-dimethylnonane, how many carbon atoms are in the parent (longest) chain, 'nonane'?","answer":9,"explain":"The prefix 'non-' indicates a 9-carbon parent chain, as used for the longest-chain examples in the chapter."},{"kind":"mcq","prompt":"While alphabetising substituent names in a multi-substituted alkane, which prefix IS treated as part of the alkyl group's fundamental name?","options":["iso-","di-","tri-","sec-"],"answer":0,"explain":"iso- and neo- are considered part of the fundamental alkyl name; sec- and tert- and multiplying prefixes like di-/tri- are not."}]},{"id":"oc6","title":"Priority of the Functional Groups","icon":"👑","kind":"lesson","story":"Several functional groups squabble over which gets to be crowned 'principal group' and lend the compound its suffix. Only the IUPAC priority order can settle the dispute.","teach":[{"title":"Choosing the Principal Functional Group","body":"In polyfunctional compounds, one functional group is chosen as the principal functional group and the compound is named on that basis; the remaining, subordinate functional groups are named as substituents using prefixes. The order of decreasing priority for choosing the principal group is: -COOH > -SO3H > -COOR > -COCl > -CONH2 > -CN > -CHO > >C=O > -OH > -NH2 > >C=C< > -C≡C-.","visual":"🏆"},{"title":"Numbering for the Lowest Locant","body":"First, the functional group present is identified, which determines the suffix; then the longest carbon chain containing that functional group is numbered so the functional group gets the lowest possible number in the chain.","visual":"🎯"},{"title":"Always-Prefix Substituents","body":"Alkyl groups (-R), halogens (F, Cl, Br, I), the nitro group (-NO2), and alkoxy groups (-OR) are always prefix substituents — they are never used as the principal suffix.","visual":"🏷️"},{"title":"A Worked Priority Example","body":"HOCH2CH2CH2CH2COCH3 is named as a hydroxy-substituted heptanone, not as an oxo-substituted heptanol, because the keto group (>C=O) is preferred over the hydroxyl group (-OH) in the priority order.","visual":"🧮"},{"title":"Multiple Groups and Benzene Naming","body":"When more than one functional group of the same type is present, di-, tri- etc. are added before the class suffix, and the full parent alkane name is written first, e.g. ethane-1,2-diol for HOCH2CH2OH; the terminal '-e' of the parent name is dropped before a suffix starting with a vowel, as in buta-1,3-diene. For substituted benzene compounds, the substituent is placed as a prefix to 'benzene'; for disubstituted rings, the trivial prefixes ortho-, meta- and para- indicate the 1,2-, 1,3- and 1,4- relative positions, but these prefixes cannot be used for tri- or higher-substituted benzene derivatives, which instead follow the lowest-locant numbering rule.","visual":"💠"}],"questions":[{"kind":"mcq","prompt":"In a compound containing both an alcohol group and a ketone group, which becomes the principal functional group (suffix)?","options":["The ketone group, because it outranks the hydroxyl group in priority","The alcohol group, because -OH is always preferred","Whichever group is written first, left to right","Both are always named together as one suffix"],"answer":0,"explain":"The ketone (>C=O) group is higher in the IUPAC priority order than -OH, so it becomes the suffix."},{"kind":"mcq","prompt":"Which of these is always used only as a prefix in IUPAC names, never as the principal suffix?","options":["Nitro (-NO2)","Carboxylic acid (-COOH)","Aldehyde (-CHO)","Ketone (>C=O)"],"answer":0,"explain":"The nitro group, like halogens, alkyl groups and alkoxy groups, is always named as a prefix."},{"kind":"mcq","prompt":"The compound 1,2-dibromobenzene is also known by which trivial name?","options":["ortho-dibromobenzene","meta-dibromobenzene","para-dibromobenzene","cyclo-dibromobenzene"],"answer":0,"explain":"The 1,2- relative positions on a disubstituted benzene ring correspond to the trivial prefix ortho- (o-)."},{"kind":"mcq","prompt":"For tri- or higher-substituted benzene derivatives, which naming approach replaces ortho/meta/para?","options":["Numbering substituent positions using the lowest-locant rule","Using only the prefix ortho- for every position","Using only trivial names with no numbers","Using Roman numerals for positions"],"answer":0,"explain":"Ortho/meta/para only work for disubstituted rings; higher-substituted rings are numbered following the lowest locant rule."},{"kind":"mcq","prompt":"HOCH2CH2OH is named by IUPAC nomenclature as:","options":["Ethane-1,2-diol","Ethanediol acid","Dihydroxyethane","Ethanol-2-ol"],"answer":0,"explain":"Two identical -OH groups on ethane are indicated with 'di-' plus locants, giving ethane-1,2-diol."}]},{"id":"oc7","title":"Hall of the Isomer Doubles","icon":"🪞","kind":"lesson","story":"In this mirrored hall, molecules with identical formulas appear as doubles, triples, even quadruples of themselves, yet each behaves differently. Tell them apart to find the true exit.","teach":[{"title":"What Is Isomerism?","body":"The phenomenon of two or more compounds possessing the same molecular formula but different properties is called isomerism, and such compounds are called isomers. Isomers are classified as structural isomers (chain, position, functional group, metamerism) or stereoisomers (geometrical and optical).","visual":"🔀"},{"title":"Chain Isomerism","body":"When two or more compounds have similar molecular formula but different carbon skeletons, they are chain isomers. For example, the molecular formula C5H12 represents three compounds: pentane, isopentane (2-methylbutane), and neopentane (2,2-dimethylpropane).","visual":"⛓️"},{"title":"Position and Functional Group Isomerism","body":"Position isomers differ in the position of a substituent atom or functional group on the same carbon skeleton, e.g. C3H8O represents propan-1-ol and propan-2-ol. Functional group isomers have the same molecular formula but different functional groups altogether, e.g. C3H6O represents both an aldehyde (propanal) and a ketone (propanone).","visual":"📍"},{"title":"Metamerism","body":"Metamerism arises due to different alkyl chains on either side of the functional group in the molecule. For example, C4H10O represents both methoxypropane (CH3-O-CH2CH2CH3) and ethoxyethane (CH3CH2-O-CH2CH3).","visual":"🔁"},{"title":"Stereoisomerism","body":"Compounds that have the same constitution and sequence of covalent bonds but differ in the relative positions of their atoms or groups in space are called stereoisomers; this special type of isomerism is called stereoisomerism and can be classified as geometrical and optical isomerism.","visual":"🌐"}],"questions":[{"kind":"numeric","prompt":"How many chain isomers (pentane, isopentane, and neopentane) does the molecular formula C5H12 represent, as described in the text?","answer":3,"explain":"C5H12 represents three chain isomers: pentane, isopentane (2-methylbutane) and neopentane (2,2-dimethylpropane)."},{"kind":"mcq","prompt":"Propan-1-ol and propan-2-ol, which differ only in the position of the -OH group, are examples of:","options":["Position isomerism","Chain isomerism","Functional group isomerism","Metamerism"],"answer":0,"explain":"They share the same carbon skeleton and functional group, differing only in where the -OH sits, which is position isomerism."},{"kind":"mcq","prompt":"The molecular formula C3H6O representing both propanal (an aldehyde) and propanone (a ketone) is an example of:","options":["Functional group isomerism","Position isomerism","Metamerism","Geometrical isomerism"],"answer":0,"explain":"These compounds share a molecular formula but have entirely different functional groups, which is functional group isomerism."},{"kind":"mcq","prompt":"Metamerism arises specifically due to:","options":["Different alkyl chains on either side of the same functional group","Different functional groups being present","Different positions of the same substituent on a chain","Different spatial arrangement of atoms"],"answer":0,"explain":"Metamers share the same functional group but differ in the alkyl groups attached on either side of it."},{"kind":"mcq","prompt":"Stereoisomers are compounds that have:","options":["The same constitution and bond sequence but differ in spatial arrangement of atoms","Different molecular formulas altogether","Completely different constitutions and bond sequences","No structural relationship to one another"],"answer":0,"explain":"Stereoisomers share constitution and bonding sequence but differ in how atoms are arranged in space."}]},{"id":"oc8","title":"Breaking the Bond","icon":"💥","kind":"lesson","story":"An alchemist offers you a covalent bond to split for passage: take all the shared electrons for yourself, or share the loss equally. Each choice conjures a different kind of reactive spirit.","teach":[{"title":"Heterolytic Cleavage and Carbocations","body":"In heterolytic cleavage, the bond breaks so that the shared pair of electrons stays entirely with one fragment; one atom is left with a sextet of electrons and a positive charge, called a carbocation, while the other gets a valence octet with a negative charge. Carbocations are classified as primary, secondary, or tertiary, and their stability order is tertiary > secondary > primary > methyl cation; they are trigonal planar with an sp2-hybridised positively charged carbon.","visual":"➕"},{"title":"Carbanions","body":"Heterolytic cleavage can also leave carbon with the shared electron pair, forming a carbanion, a carbon species carrying a negative charge. Carbon in a carbanion is generally sp3-hybridised, with a distorted tetrahedral structure. Reactions proceeding through heterolytic cleavage are called ionic, heteropolar, or polar reactions.","visual":"➖"},{"title":"Homolytic Cleavage and Free Radicals","body":"In homolytic cleavage, one electron of the shared pair goes to each bonded atom, shown using a half-headed 'fish-hook' curved arrow; this produces neutral species with an unpaired electron, called free radicals. Alkyl radicals are also classified as primary, secondary, or tertiary, and radical stability increases from primary to tertiary. Reactions proceeding by homolytic fission are called free radical, homopolar, or non-polar reactions.","visual":"🔥"},{"title":"Substrate and Reagent","body":"In an organic reaction, a substrate reacts with an attacking reagent to form intermediates and finally products. The substrate is the reactant whose carbon supplies the new bond, and the other reactant is called the reagent; when both reactants supply carbon to the new bond, the choice of which is 'substrate' is arbitrary.","visual":"🧫"},{"title":"Nucleophiles and Electrophiles","body":"A reagent that brings an electron pair to the reactive site is called a nucleophile (nucleus-seeking), and the reaction is nucleophilic; examples include hydroxide (OH-), cyanide (CN-) ions, carbanions, and neutral molecules with lone pairs. A reagent that takes away an electron pair from the reactive site is an electrophile (electron-seeking); examples include carbocations and neutral molecules with functional groups like carbonyl (>C=O) or alkyl halides (R-X), where the carbon becomes electron deficient due to bond polarity.","visual":"🧲"}],"questions":[{"kind":"mcq","prompt":"What is a carbon species with only a sextet of electrons and a positive charge called?","options":["Carbocation","Carbanion","Free radical","Nucleophile"],"answer":0,"explain":"A carbocation is an electron-deficient, positively charged carbon species formed by heterolytic cleavage."},{"kind":"mcq","prompt":"What is the correct order of carbocation stability?","options":["Tertiary > secondary > primary > methyl","Methyl > primary > secondary > tertiary","Primary > tertiary > secondary > methyl","All carbocations are equally stable"],"answer":0,"explain":"Alkyl groups stabilise carbocations through inductive and hyperconjugation effects, so tertiary cations are most stable."},{"kind":"mcq","prompt":"Homolytic cleavage of a covalent bond produces which reactive species?","options":["Free radicals","Carbocations and carbanions together","Only positively charged ions","Only negatively charged ions"],"answer":0,"explain":"Homolytic cleavage splits the shared electron pair evenly, producing neutral species with an unpaired electron: free radicals."},{"kind":"mcq","prompt":"A reagent that donates an electron pair to the reactive site of a substrate is called a(n):","options":["Nucleophile","Electrophile","Free radical","Carbocation"],"answer":0,"explain":"Nucleophiles are electron pair donors ('nucleus-seeking'), such as OH- and CN-."},{"kind":"mcq","prompt":"In an organic reaction, the reactant whose carbon is involved in forming the new bond is called the:","options":["Substrate","Reagent","Byproduct","Intermediate"],"answer":0,"explain":"The molecule supplying carbon to the new bond is termed the substrate; the other reactant is the reagent."}]},{"id":"oc9","title":"The Polarity Puzzle","icon":"🌀","kind":"lesson","story":"Every door in this wing is locked by shifting delta charges. Read the inductive pull, the resonance hum, and the hyperconjugative glow correctly, and the doors swing open on their own.","teach":[{"title":"The Inductive Effect","body":"When a covalent bond forms between atoms of different electronegativity, the electron density shifts toward the more electronegative atom, creating a polar covalent bond. In chloroethane, the polar C-Cl bond draws electron density from the adjacent C-C bond too; this polarisation of a sigma bond caused by polarisation of an adjacent sigma bond is called the inductive effect. It decreases rapidly and becomes vanishingly small after about three bonds.","visual":"➡️"},{"title":"Electron-Withdrawing vs Donating Groups","body":"Halogens and groups such as nitro (-NO2), cyano (-CN), carboxyl (-COOH), ester (-COOR), and aryloxy (-OAr, e.g. -OCH3) are electron-withdrawing groups. Alkyl groups like methyl (-CH3) and ethyl (-C2H5) are usually considered electron-donating groups relative to hydrogen.","visual":"🧭"},{"title":"Resonance","body":"Some molecules, like benzene, cannot be adequately described by a single Lewis structure. Benzene's experimentally determined C-C bond length is a uniform 139 pm, intermediate between a C-C single bond (154 pm) and a C=C double bond (134 pm), so benzene is described as a resonance hybrid of energetically identical contributing (canonical) structures, which are hypothetical and do not individually represent any real molecule.","visual":"🔄"},{"title":"The Resonance (Mesomeric) Effect","body":"The resonance effect is the polarity produced in a molecule by the interaction between two pi bonds, or between a pi bond and a lone pair on an adjacent atom. In the +R effect, seen in aniline, electron transfer is away from the attached group, increasing electron density at certain ring positions; groups showing +R include halogens, -OH, -OR, -NH2. In the -R effect, seen in nitrobenzene, electron transfer is toward the attached group; groups showing -R include -COOH, -CHO, >C=O, -CN, -NO2.","visual":"🎭"},{"title":"Electromeric Effect and Hyperconjugation","body":"The electromeric effect (E effect) is a temporary effect shown only by compounds with a multiple bond, in the presence of an attacking reagent, involving complete transfer of a shared pi electron pair; it vanishes once the reagent is removed. Hyperconjugation, by contrast, is a permanent, general stabilising effect involving delocalisation of C-H sigma electrons of an alkyl group into an adjacent empty p orbital or unsaturated system; the tert-butyl cation, with nine such C-H bonds, is stabilised far more than the ethyl cation, while the methyl cation has no C-H bonds available for hyperconjugation and is the least stable.","visual":"✨"}],"questions":[{"kind":"mcq","prompt":"The polarisation of a sigma bond caused by the polarisation of an adjacent sigma bond is called the:","options":["Inductive effect","Resonance effect","Electromeric effect","Hyperconjugation"],"answer":0,"explain":"This permanent electron displacement through sigma bonds is the inductive effect, weakening after about three bonds."},{"kind":"mcq","prompt":"Benzene's uniform carbon-carbon bond length of 139 pm, intermediate between a single bond (154 pm) and a double bond (134 pm), is best explained by:","options":["Resonance","The inductive effect","The electromeric effect","Metamerism"],"answer":0,"explain":"Benzene is a resonance hybrid of contributing structures, giving all its C-C bonds an equal, intermediate length."},{"kind":"mcq","prompt":"The electromeric effect is best described as:","options":["A temporary effect shown by a multiple bond only in the presence of an attacking reagent","A permanent effect present even in the ground state of the molecule","An effect only seen in single-bonded saturated molecules","Identical to the inductive effect"],"answer":0,"explain":"The electromeric effect appears only while an attacking reagent is present and disappears once it is removed."},{"kind":"mcq","prompt":"Which effect explains why the tert-butyl cation is more stable than the methyl cation?","options":["Hyperconjugation","The resonance effect","The electromeric effect","Metamerism"],"answer":0,"explain":"The tert-butyl cation has nine C-H bonds available for hyperconjugative stabilisation, while the methyl cation has none."},{"kind":"mcq","prompt":"Which of these groups is generally electron-donating (relative to hydrogen) via the inductive effect?","options":["Methyl group (-CH3)","Nitro group (-NO2)","Cyano group (-CN)","Carboxyl group (-COOH)"],"answer":0,"explain":"Alkyl groups like methyl and ethyl are electron-donating; halogens, -NO2, -CN and -COOH are electron-withdrawing."}]},{"id":"oc10","title":"The Forensic Alchemist's Lab","icon":"🔍","kind":"lesson","story":"Before any compound's secrets can be trusted, it must be purified, tested, and weighed. Step into the lab and run the classic tests that reveal exactly what a mystery compound is made of.","teach":[{"title":"Sublimation and Crystallisation","body":"Sublimation separates sublimable compounds from non-sublimable impurities using the direct solid-to-vapour transition. Crystallisation, the most common technique for purifying solid organic compounds, relies on the compound being far more soluble in a solvent at high temperature than at room temperature; the impure solid is dissolved, the solution concentrated to near-saturation, and pure compound crystallises out on cooling and is removed by filtration.","visual":"❄️"},{"title":"The Distillation Family","body":"Simple distillation separates volatile liquids from non-volatile impurities, or liquids with sufficiently different boiling points, such as chloroform (bp 334 K) and aniline (bp 457 K). Fractional distillation, using a fractionating column, separates liquids with close boiling points and is used industrially to separate crude oil into fractions in the petroleum industry. Distillation under reduced pressure lowers the boiling point for high-boiling or heat-sensitive liquids, such as separating glycerol from spent-lye in the soap industry. Steam distillation separates steam-volatile, water-immiscible substances, such as aniline from an aniline-water mixture, boiling the mixture below 373 K.","visual":"🌡️"},{"title":"Extraction and Chromatography","body":"Differential extraction, using a separating funnel, removes a compound from an aqueous medium into an organic solvent in which it is more soluble. Chromatography separates a mixture over a stationary phase while a mobile phase moves over it; adsorption chromatography (column and thin-layer chromatography, using adsorbents like silica gel or alumina) and partition chromatography (such as paper chromatography, where trapped water acts as the stationary phase) are its two main types. A component's movement is expressed as its retardation factor, Rf = distance moved by the substance from the baseline / distance moved by the solvent from the baseline.","visual":"🧪"},{"title":"Lassaigne's Test","body":"Nitrogen, sulphur, halogens and phosphorus are detected using Lassaigne's test, which fuses the compound with sodium metal to convert these elements into ionic sodium cyanide, sulphide and halide, extracted as the 'sodium fusion extract'. A Prussian blue colour (from iron(III) hexacyanidoferrate(II)) with FeSO4 and H2SO4 confirms nitrogen; a black lead sulphide precipitate, or a violet colour with sodium nitroprusside, confirms sulphur; and white (chlorine), yellowish (bromine) or yellow (iodine) silver halide precipitates, with differing solubility in ammonium hydroxide, confirm the halogen present.","visual":"🎨"},{"title":"Quantitative Estimation","body":"Carbon and hydrogen are estimated together by burning a known mass of compound with excess CuO, absorbing the water formed in anhydrous CaCl2 and the CO2 in KOH solution. Nitrogen is estimated by the Dumas method (measuring the volume of N2 gas collected) or Kjeldahl's method (converting nitrogen to ammonium sulphate, then titrating the ammonia against standard acid) — though Kjeldahl's method fails for nitro, azo, and ring-nitrogen compounds like pyridine. Halogens are estimated by the Carius method (precipitating AgX), sulphur by precipitating BaSO4, and phosphorus by precipitating ammonium phosphomolybdate; oxygen is usually found by difference from 100%.","visual":"⚖️"}],"questions":[{"kind":"numeric","prompt":"On complete combustion, 0.246 g of an organic compound gave 0.198 g of CO2. What is the percentage of carbon in the compound (to two decimal places)?","answer":21.95,"explain":"Percentage of carbon = (12 x mass of CO2 x 100) / (44 x mass of compound) = (12 x 0.198 x 100)/(44 x 0.246) = 21.95%."},{"kind":"numeric","prompt":"In Kjeldahl's method, the ammonia evolved from 0.5 g of an organic compound neutralised 10 mL of 1 M H2SO4. What is the percentage of nitrogen in the compound?","answer":56.0,"explain":"10 mL of 1M H2SO4 equals 20 mL of 1M NH3; percentage of nitrogen = (14 x 20 x 100)/(1000 x 0.5) = 56.0%."},{"kind":"numeric","prompt":"In the Carius method for halogen estimation, 0.15 g of an organic compound gave 0.12 g of AgBr (molar mass 188 g/mol). What is the percentage of bromine in the compound (to two decimal places)?","answer":34.04,"explain":"Percentage of bromine = (80 x 0.12 x 100)/(188 x 0.15) = 34.04%."},{"kind":"mcq","prompt":"In Lassaigne's test, the formation of a Prussian blue colour confirms the presence of:","options":["Nitrogen","Sulphur","A halogen","Phosphorus"],"answer":0,"explain":"Prussian blue (iron(III) hexacyanidoferrate(II)) forms from the sodium cyanide produced when nitrogen is present."},{"kind":"mcq","prompt":"Which technique separates chloroform (bp 334 K) from aniline (bp 457 K)?","options":["Simple distillation","Sublimation","Chromatography","Crystallisation"],"answer":0,"explain":"Liquids with a large difference in boiling points, like chloroform and aniline, are easily separated by simple distillation."}]},{"id":"oc-boss","title":"The Vitalist Specter","icon":"👑","kind":"boss","story":"At the heart of the archive waits the Vitalist Specter, a ghost of the old theory that only life could create organic matter. It will not fade until you answer for everything you have learned about carbon's world.","boss":{"name":"The Vitalist Specter","taunt":"Life alone can weave carbon's magic — prove me wrong, apprentice, or remain forever inorganic!"},"teach":[{"title":"One Last Review","body":"From the vital force theory's fall, to carbon's tetrahedral, planar and linear shapes, to naming rules, isomers, reaction mechanisms, electron effects, and the purification and analysis of compounds — everything you've learned about organic chemistry's foundations is tested here.","visual":"📖"}],"questions":[{"kind":"mcq","prompt":"Who disproved the vital force theory by synthesising urea from ammonium cyanate?","options":["Friedrich Wohler","Berzelius","Kolbe","Berthelot"],"answer":0,"explain":"Wohler's synthesis of urea from an inorganic compound overturned the vital force theory."},{"kind":"mcq","prompt":"What shape does the sp3-hybridised carbon atom in methane adopt?","options":["Tetrahedral","Trigonal planar","Linear","Octahedral"],"answer":0,"explain":"Methane's four sp3 hybrid orbitals point toward the corners of a tetrahedron."},{"kind":"mcq","prompt":"In a bond-line formula, an unlabeled line junction represents:","options":["A carbon atom with enough hydrogens to complete its valency","An oxygen atom","A nitrogen atom","Nothing at all"],"answer":0,"explain":"Bond-line formulas omit carbon and hydrogen atoms, with junctions implying carbon plus the needed hydrogens."},{"kind":"mcq","prompt":"C5H12 represents three isomers (pentane, isopentane, neopentane) that differ only in their carbon skeleton. This is called:","options":["Chain isomerism","Position isomerism","Functional group isomerism","Metamerism"],"answer":0,"explain":"Isomers differing only in how the carbon skeleton is arranged are chain isomers."},{"kind":"mcq","prompt":"A carbon species formed by heterolytic cleavage, with only six valence electrons and a positive charge, is called a:","options":["Carbocation","Carbanion","Free radical","Nucleophile"],"answer":0,"explain":"Carbocations are electron-deficient, positively charged carbon intermediates formed by heterolytic bond cleavage."},{"kind":"mcq","prompt":"Benzene's uniform, intermediate carbon-carbon bond length (139 pm) is best explained by:","options":["Resonance","The inductive effect","Hyperconjugation","Metamerism"],"answer":0,"explain":"Benzene is a resonance hybrid of equivalent canonical structures, giving it one uniform bond length."},{"kind":"numeric","prompt":"In Kjeldahl's method, ammonia evolved from 0.5 g of a compound neutralised 10 mL of 1 M H2SO4. What is the percentage of nitrogen?","answer":56.0,"explain":"Percentage of nitrogen = (14 x 20 x 100)/(1000 x 0.5) = 56.0%, since 10 mL of 1M H2SO4 equals 20 mL of 1M NH3."},{"kind":"mcq","prompt":"Among -COOH, -OH, -NH2 and >C=O, which ranks highest in the IUPAC principal-group priority order?","options":["-COOH","-OH","-NH2",">C=O"],"answer":0,"explain":"The carboxylic acid group (-COOH) sits at the top of the IUPAC priority order given in the chapter."}]}]},{"id":"hydrocarbons11","index":8,"name":"Hydrocarbons","subtitle":"Chemistry Chapter 9","emoji":"🔥","palette":{"from":"#ec4899","to":"#831843"},"lessons":[{"id":"hc1","title":"Three Kingdoms of Carbon","icon":"🔗","kind":"lesson","story":"The Study Atlas splits into a realm of pure carbon and hydrogen -- pumps of LPG hiss, a CNG flame flickers blue, and three glowing gates stand before you, labelled Saturated, Unsaturated, and Aromatic. A guide whispers: 'Every hydrocarbon you will ever meet belongs to one of these three kingdoms. Learn to sort them, and the gates will open.'","teach":[{"title":"Hydrocarbons: carbon and hydrogen only","body":"Hydrocarbons are compounds of carbon and hydrogen only. LPG, CNG, petrol, diesel and kerosene are all mixtures of hydrocarbons used as fuels, and hydrocarbons are also the starting materials for polymers like polythene and polypropene, solvents, dyes and drugs.","visual":"⛽"},{"title":"Three main classes","body":"Based on the type of carbon-carbon bonds, hydrocarbons are classified as saturated (alkanes -- open chains with only C-C and C-H single bonds; cycloalkanes -- closed rings of single bonds), unsaturated (alkenes and alkynes, containing C=C double bonds, C-C triple bonds, or both), and aromatic (a special class of cyclic compounds).","visual":"🧪"},{"title":"Methane: the first alkane","body":"Alkanes are saturated open-chain hydrocarbons with general formula CnH2n+2. Methane, CH4, the first member, has a tetrahedral structure with carbon at the centre and four hydrogens at the corners, with every H-C-H bond angle equal to 109.5 degrees, exactly as predicted by VSEPR theory.","visual":"🔺"},{"title":"Bonds built from sp3 orbitals","body":"In alkanes, C-C and C-H bonds form by head-on overlap of sp3 hybrid orbitals of carbon with sp3 orbitals (for C-C) or the 1s orbital of hydrogen (for C-H). The C-C bond length is 154 pm and the C-H bond length is 112 pm. Because alkanes barely react with acids, bases or other reagents under normal conditions, they were once called paraffins, from the Latin parum (little) and affinis (affinity).","visual":"⚛️"}],"questions":[{"kind":"mcq","prompt":"What is the general formula for the alkane homologous series?","options":["CnH2n+2","CnH2n","CnH2n-2","CnHn"],"answer":0,"explain":"Alkanes are saturated open-chain hydrocarbons with the general formula CnH2n+2."},{"kind":"numeric","prompt":"What is the H-C-H bond angle in methane, in degrees?","answer":109.5,"explain":"Methane has a tetrahedral structure (VSEPR theory) with all H-C-H bond angles equal to 109.5 degrees."},{"kind":"numeric","prompt":"What is the C-C bond length in alkanes, in picometres (pm)?","answer":154,"explain":"In alkanes, the C-C bond length is 154 pm and the C-H bond length is 112 pm."},{"kind":"mcq","prompt":"Why were alkanes historically called 'paraffins'?","options":["Because they are inert under normal conditions and do not react with acids, bases or other reagents","Because they were first extracted from paraffin wax candles","Because they contain a triple bond that resists all reactions","Because they were named after the chemist Paraffin"],"answer":0,"explain":"'Paraffin' comes from Latin parum (little) and affinis (affinity), reflecting the low reactivity of alkanes under normal conditions."}]},{"id":"hc2","title":"The Isomer Labyrinth","icon":"🌀","kind":"lesson","story":"The saturated gate opens into a labyrinth where identical formulas twist into different corridors. A sign reads: 'C6H14 has five doors. C10H22 has seventy-five. Name each passage correctly by IUPAC rules, or wander forever.'","teach":[{"title":"Chain isomers multiply fast","body":"C4H10 can be arranged as n-butane (b.p. 273 K) or 2-methylpropane/isobutane (b.p. 261 K) -- two chain isomers. C5H12 has three (n-pentane, 2-methylbutane, 2,2-dimethylpropane). This growth accelerates: C6H14 has five isomers, C7H16 has nine, and C10H22 has as many as 75 possible isomers.","visual":"🔀"},{"title":"Naming the correct way","body":"To name a structure by IUPAC rules: find the longest continuous carbon chain (the parent alkane), number it from the end that gives the lowest locants to substituents, attach substituent names in alphabetical order, and satisfy every carbon's valence with hydrogens. Getting the longest chain wrong gives an invalid name.","visual":"📏"},{"title":"Primary, secondary, tertiary, quaternary carbons","body":"A carbon attached to only one other carbon (or none, as in methane) is primary; attached to two carbons it is secondary; to three, tertiary; and to four other carbons, quaternary (or neo). Terminal carbons of a chain are always primary.","visual":"🧮"},{"title":"Alkyl groups","body":"Removing one hydrogen atom from an alkane gives an alkyl group, such as -CH3 (methyl) or -C2H5 (ethyl), with general formula CnH2n+1. These groups act as substituents when naming branched alkanes and other classes of organic compounds.","visual":"🪢"}],"questions":[{"kind":"numeric","prompt":"How many chain isomers does the alkane C6H14 have?","answer":5,"explain":"C6H14 (hexane) has five chain isomers: n-hexane, 2-methylpentane, 3-methylpentane, 2,3-dimethylbutane and 2,2-dimethylbutane."},{"kind":"numeric","prompt":"How many isomers are possible for the alkane C10H22?","answer":75,"explain":"As the chain gets longer, isomer count grows rapidly -- C10H22 has as many as 75 possible isomers."},{"kind":"mcq","prompt":"What is a carbon atom called when it is attached to four other carbon atoms?","options":["Quaternary carbon","Primary carbon","Secondary carbon","Tertiary carbon"],"answer":0,"explain":"A carbon bonded to four other carbon atoms is called quaternary (or neo) carbon."},{"kind":"mcq","prompt":"The name '2-Ethylpentane' for CH3-CH(C2H5)-CH2-CH2-CH3 is actually incorrect. Why, and what is the correct IUPAC name?","options":["The longest chain is actually six carbons, not five, so the correct name is 3-Methylhexane","Ethyl substituents are never allowed in IUPAC names","The locant should be 3, not 2, giving 3-Ethylpentane","The compound should be named as a cyclohexane derivative"],"answer":0,"explain":"Picking the ethyl-bearing carbon as branch instead of chain member hides a six-carbon chain; the correct IUPAC name is 3-Methylhexane."},{"kind":"mcq","prompt":"What is the general formula for an alkyl group derived from an alkane?","options":["CnH2n+1","CnH2n+2","CnH2n","CnH2n-1"],"answer":0,"explain":"Removing one hydrogen atom from CnH2n+2 gives an alkyl group with general formula CnH2n+1."}]},{"id":"hc3","title":"Forging Alkanes","icon":"⚒️","kind":"lesson","story":"Beyond the labyrinth lies a forge with four glowing anvils, each representing a different route to build an alkane -- from unsaturated cousins, from alkyl halides, and from carboxylic acid salts. The forge master hands you a hammer: 'Choose the right reagent for each anvil, or the metal cools to nothing.'","teach":[{"title":"Hydrogenation of unsaturated hydrocarbons","body":"Alkenes and alkynes add dihydrogen gas in the presence of finely divided Pt, Pd or Ni to form alkanes. Platinum and palladium catalyse this at room temperature, while nickel needs higher temperature and pressure. This process is called hydrogenation.","visual":"⚗️"},{"title":"Wurtz reaction","body":"Alkyl halides treated with sodium metal in dry ether give higher alkanes containing an even number of carbon atoms -- for example, two molecules of bromoethane give n-butane plus NaBr. This is the Wurtz reaction, useful for symmetrical higher alkanes.","visual":"🧲"},{"title":"Decarboxylation of carboxylic acid salts","body":"Sodium salts of carboxylic acids, heated with soda lime (NaOH + CaO), lose CO2 to give an alkane with one carbon fewer than the acid, plus sodium carbonate. For example, sodium propanoate would not give propane -- you need sodium butanoate (from butanoic acid) to obtain propane by this route.","visual":"🔥"},{"title":"Kolbe's electrolytic method","body":"Electrolysis of an aqueous solution of the sodium or potassium salt of a carboxylic acid gives an alkane with an even number of carbon atoms at the anode (via a free-radical pathway: acetate ion loses an electron, decarboxylates to a methyl radical, and two methyl radicals combine), with H2 liberated at the cathode. Methane cannot be prepared this way.","visual":"⚡"}],"questions":[{"kind":"mcq","prompt":"What reagent converts an alkyl halide into a higher alkane in the Wurtz reaction?","options":["Sodium metal in dry ether","Zinc and dilute HCl","Soda lime on heating","Aqueous KOH"],"answer":0,"explain":"Alkyl halides on treatment with sodium metal in dry (moisture-free) ether undergo the Wurtz reaction to form higher alkanes with an even number of carbon atoms."},{"kind":"mcq","prompt":"To prepare propane by decarboxylation of a sodium carboxylate salt with soda lime, which acid's sodium salt is needed?","options":["Butanoic acid","Propanoic acid","Ethanoic acid","Pentanoic acid"],"answer":0,"explain":"Decarboxylation removes one carbon as CO2, so a four-carbon acid (butanoic acid) is needed to obtain the three-carbon alkane propane."},{"kind":"mcq","prompt":"In Kolbe's electrolytic method, what kind of alkane is obtained at the anode?","options":["An alkane containing an even number of carbon atoms","An alkane containing an odd number of carbon atoms","Only methane","A mixture of alkenes and alkynes"],"answer":0,"explain":"Electrolysis of an aqueous sodium or potassium carboxylate gives an alkane with an even number of carbon atoms at the anode, since two alkyl radicals combine."},{"kind":"mcq","prompt":"Which catalysts allow hydrogenation of alkenes/alkynes to proceed at room temperature, unlike nickel which needs higher temperature and pressure?","options":["Platinum and palladium","Iron and copper","Zinc and magnesium","Aluminium and sodium"],"answer":0,"explain":"Pt and Pd catalyse hydrogenation at room temperature, while Ni requires relatively higher temperature and pressure."}]},{"id":"hc4","title":"Fire and Chains","icon":"🔥","kind":"lesson","story":"A trial chamber ignites around you -- flames test which alkanes burn cleanest, and a chain of ghostly chlorine radicals races between molecules of methane. 'Alkanes look lazy,' the flame hisses, 'but under fire and light, they transform. Show me you understand how.'","teach":[{"title":"Physical properties and branching","body":"Alkanes are almost non-polar, held together only by weak van der Waals forces, so C1-C4 are gases, C5-C17 are liquids, and C18+ are solids at 298 K. Branching lowers boiling point by shrinking surface area for contact: n-pentane (309 K) boils higher than 2-methylbutane (301 K), which boils higher than 2,2-dimethylpropane (282.5 K).","visual":"🌡️"},{"title":"Combustion","body":"Alkanes burn completely in excess air or oxygen to CO2 and water, releasing large amounts of heat -- methane's combustion releases about 890 kJ/mol. With insufficient air, incomplete combustion produces carbon black (soot), used in inks and pigments.","visual":"🔥"},{"title":"Halogenation: a free-radical chain reaction","body":"Under sunlight/UV light or heat, halogens substitute hydrogens of alkanes. Reactivity follows F2 > Cl2 > Br2 > I2, and ease of hydrogen replacement follows 3 degree > 2 degree > 1 degree. The mechanism has three stages: initiation (Cl2 splits homolytically into Cl radicals under light), propagation (two repeating steps that generate the main product), and termination (radicals combine and the chain stops).","visual":"⛓️"},{"title":"Why ethane appears as a byproduct","body":"During chlorination of methane, a termination step where two methyl radicals combine (CH3 + CH3 -> CH3-CH3) explains why ethane is found as a byproduct alongside the main product, chloromethane.","visual":"🧪"}],"questions":[{"kind":"mcq","prompt":"Among the three isomers of pentane (C5H12), which has the highest boiling point, and why?","options":["n-Pentane (309 K), because its continuous chain has the largest surface area for van der Waals contact","2,2-Dimethylpropane (neopentane), because it is the most spherical isomer","2-Methylbutane, because branching always raises boiling point","All three isomers boil at exactly the same temperature"],"answer":0,"explain":"n-Pentane's continuous chain gives more surface contact and stronger van der Waals forces than the branched, more spherical isomers."},{"kind":"numeric","prompt":"About how much heat (in kJ/mol, as a positive magnitude) is released when one mole of methane undergoes complete combustion?","answer":890,"explain":"CH4(g) + 2O2(g) -> CO2(g) + 2H2O(l), with an enthalpy change of about -890 kJ/mol."},{"kind":"mcq","prompt":"What is the correct order of reactivity of halogens toward alkanes?","options":["F2 > Cl2 > Br2 > I2","I2 > Br2 > Cl2 > F2","Cl2 > F2 > Br2 > I2","Br2 > Cl2 > I2 > F2"],"answer":0,"explain":"Fluorine reacts most violently and iodine most slowly/reversibly; the reactivity order is F2 > Cl2 > Br2 > I2."},{"kind":"mcq","prompt":"What happens in the initiation step of the free-radical halogenation mechanism?","options":["The halogen molecule (e.g. Cl2) splits homolytically into two halogen radicals under light or heat","A halogen radical directly forms the final alkyl halide product","Two alkane molecules combine to form a longer chain","The alkane loses a proton to form a carbanion"],"answer":0,"explain":"Initiation is the homolysis of the weaker Cl-Cl bond (weaker than C-C or C-H) under light or heat, producing chlorine free radicals."},{"kind":"mcq","prompt":"Why is ethane found as a byproduct when methane is chlorinated?","options":["A termination step where two methyl free radicals combine directly forms ethane","Methane spontaneously dimerises before any radicals form","Chlorine gas is contaminated with ethane","Ethane is formed only when the reaction is run in the dark"],"answer":0,"explain":"One possible chain-terminating step is CH3 + CH3 -> CH3-CH3, explaining ethane as a minor byproduct."}]},{"id":"hc5","title":"The Rotating Chamber","icon":"⚙️","kind":"lesson","story":"A cylindrical chamber locks around a single ethane molecule, and the far half begins to spin freely around the C-C axis. 'Watch closely,' says the mechanism etched on the wall. 'Some arrangements cost more energy than others -- find the one the molecule prefers.'","teach":[{"title":"Free rotation, infinite conformations","body":"Because the sigma bond's electron cloud is symmetrical around the C-C axis, rotation about a C-C single bond is essentially unhindered. Each spatial arrangement reached this way is called a conformation, conformer, or rotamer, and alkanes can have an infinite number of them.","visual":"🔄"},{"title":"Staggered vs eclipsed","body":"In ethane, the conformation where hydrogens on the two carbons are as far apart as possible is staggered; the one where they line up as closely as possible is eclipsed; anything in between is a skew conformation. These can be drawn as Sawhorse projections (viewed along the molecular axis) or Newman projections (viewed head-on down the C-C bond).","visual":"🪛"},{"title":"Torsional strain and stability","body":"The small repulsive interaction between electron clouds of C-H bonds on adjacent carbons is called torsional strain, and it is worth roughly 1-20 kJ/mol depending on the angle of rotation (the dihedral or torsional angle). Staggered ethane has minimum torsional strain and is the most stable conformation; eclipsed ethane has maximum torsional strain.","visual":"⛓️"},{"title":"A barrier too small to trap","body":"The energy difference between staggered and eclipsed ethane is only about 12.5 kJ/mol. Molecules easily gain this much thermal energy from intermolecular collisions even at ordinary temperatures, so rotation about the C-C bond in ethane is almost free -- no single conformer of ethane has ever been isolated.","visual":"🌡️"}],"questions":[{"kind":"numeric","prompt":"What is the approximate energy difference, in kJ/mol, between the staggered and eclipsed conformations of ethane?","answer":12.5,"explain":"The energy difference between the two extreme conformations of ethane is of the order of 12.5 kJ/mol -- small enough to overcome by thermal collisions."},{"kind":"mcq","prompt":"Which conformation of ethane is more stable, and why?","options":["Staggered, because the C-H bonds on adjacent carbons are as far apart as possible, minimising torsional strain","Eclipsed, because the C-H bonds overlap and stabilise each other","Both are equally stable at all temperatures","Skew, because it balances repulsion perfectly"],"answer":0,"explain":"In the staggered form, C-H electron clouds are maximally separated, giving minimum torsional strain and maximum stability."},{"kind":"mcq","prompt":"What name is given to the repulsive interaction between electron clouds of C-H bonds on adjacent carbons during rotation about a C-C bond?","options":["Torsional strain","Steric hindrance","Ionic repulsion","Resonance strain"],"answer":0,"explain":"This repulsive interaction, which depends on the angle of rotation, is called torsional strain."},{"kind":"mcq","prompt":"Which two projections are used to represent the eclipsed and staggered conformations of ethane?","options":["Sawhorse projection and Newman projection","Fischer projection and Haworth projection","Wedge-dash projection and Line-angle projection","Lewis structure and VSEPR diagram"],"answer":0,"explain":"Sawhorse projections view the molecule along its axis; Newman projections view it head-on down the C-C bond, front carbon as a point and rear carbon as a circle."}]},{"id":"hc6","title":"Twin Mirrors of the Double Bond","icon":"🪞","kind":"lesson","story":"Past the rotating chamber stands a hall of mirrors, but the reflections refuse to line up -- one twin has its methyl groups on the same side, the other across. 'The double bond does not rotate,' warns an inscription. 'Cis and trans are not the same molecule -- prove you can tell them apart.'","teach":[{"title":"The double bond: one sigma, one pi","body":"Alkenes have general formula CnH2n. Each carbon-carbon double bond is one strong sigma bond (about 397 kJ/mol, from head-on overlap of sp2 orbitals) plus one weaker pi bond (about 284 kJ/mol, from sideways overlap of 2p orbitals). The total C=C bond enthalpy (681 kJ/mol) exceeds the C-C single bond enthalpy in ethane (348 kJ/mol), and C=C (133 pm) is shorter than C-C (154 pm).","visual":"⚖️"},{"title":"Naming alkenes","body":"The longest chain containing the double bond is chosen, numbered from the end nearer the double bond, and the suffix '-ene' replaces '-ane'. Ethene (C2H4) is the first stable member; the hypothetical CH2, 'methene', does not persist.","visual":"🏷️"},{"title":"Structural isomers of C4H8","body":"Butene's molecular formula C4H8 gives three structures: but-1-ene and but-2-ene (position isomers of each other), and 2-methylprop-1-ene (a chain isomer of both). Higher alkenes show similarly rich isomerism.","visual":"🔀"},{"title":"Cis-trans (geometrical) isomerism","body":"Rotation about C=C is restricted, so if each doubly-bonded carbon carries two different groups, two distinct spatial arrangements exist: cis (identical groups on the same side) and trans (identical groups on opposite sides). Cis-but-2-ene has a dipole moment of 0.33 D, while trans-but-2-ene is essentially non-polar (about 0 D) because the two C-CH3 bond dipoles cancel across the double bond.","visual":"🪞"}],"questions":[{"kind":"numeric","prompt":"What is the approximate bond enthalpy of a carbon-carbon double bond (C=C), in kJ/mol?","answer":681,"explain":"The C=C double bond (one sigma + one pi bond) has a bond enthalpy of about 681 kJ/mol, greater than the 348 kJ/mol of a C-C single bond in ethane."},{"kind":"numeric","prompt":"What is the dipole moment of cis-but-2-ene, in Debye?","answer":0.33,"explain":"Cis-but-2-ene has a dipole moment of 0.33 D, while trans-but-2-ene is almost non-polar since its bond dipoles cancel."},{"kind":"mcq","prompt":"How is the pi bond of a C=C double bond formed?","options":["By sideways (lateral) overlap of two unhybridised 2p orbitals","By head-on overlap of two sp3 orbitals","By head-on overlap of two 1s orbitals","By sideways overlap of two sp2 orbitals"],"answer":0,"explain":"The pi bond forms from lateral (sideways) overlap of the two carbons' unhybridised 2p orbitals, making it weaker than the sigma bond."},{"kind":"mcq","prompt":"Why is trans-but-2-ene essentially non-polar even though it contains polar C-CH3 bonds?","options":["The two C-CH3 bond dipoles point in opposite directions and cancel each other","Trans alkenes never have any polar bonds","The double bond itself has no dipole in any alkene","Rotation around the double bond averages the dipoles to zero"],"answer":0,"explain":"In trans-but-2-ene, the two methyl groups are on opposite sides, so their bond dipoles cancel, giving a net dipole moment near zero."},{"kind":"mcq","prompt":"The molecular formula C4H8 corresponds to which set of alkene structures?","options":["But-1-ene, but-2-ene, and 2-methylprop-1-ene","Only but-1-ene","Propene and but-1-ene","But-1-yne and but-2-yne"],"answer":0,"explain":"C4H8 can be but-1-ene, but-2-ene (position isomers), or 2-methylprop-1-ene (a chain isomer of both)."}]},{"id":"hc7","title":"The Markovnikov Trials","icon":"🧪","kind":"lesson","story":"A judge made of shifting electron clouds presents propene and a vial of HBr. 'Two products are possible,' she says. 'Choose the major one -- and then choose again, once I add a pinch of peroxide. The rules change; your reasoning must not.'","teach":[{"title":"Making alkenes","body":"Alkenes can be made from alkynes (Lindlar's catalyst -- palladised charcoal partly deactivated with sulphur compounds or quinoline -- gives cis-alkenes; Na in liquid ammonia gives trans-alkenes), from alkyl halides (dehydrohalogenation with alcoholic KOH), from vicinal dihalides (dehalogenation with zinc), and from alcohols (acidic dehydration with conc. H2SO4).","visual":"⚗️"},{"title":"Markovnikov's rule","body":"In 1869, Markovnikov generalised that when HX adds across an unsymmetrical alkene, the negative part of the addendum attaches to the carbon bearing fewer hydrogen atoms. So propene + HBr gives mainly 2-bromopropane. Mechanistically, H+ attacks first, forming the more stable secondary carbocation (rather than the less stable primary one) faster, and Br- then adds to it.","visual":"🎯"},{"title":"The peroxide (Kharash) effect","body":"In 1933, Kharash and Mayo found that HBr alone -- in the presence of peroxides -- adds to unsymmetrical alkenes anti to Markovnikov's rule, giving 1-bromopropane from propene as the major product, via a free-radical chain mechanism through the more stable secondary radical. This effect appears only with HBr: the H-Cl bond (430.5 kJ/mol) is too strong to be cleaved by the radical, while H-I (296.8 kJ/mol) is cleaved but the resulting iodine radicals recombine to I2 instead of adding.","visual":"⚡"},{"title":"Other addition and cleavage reactions","body":"Cold, dilute aqueous KMnO4 (Baeyer's reagent) adds across C=C to give a vicinal glycol, decolourising as a test for unsaturation; hot acidic KMnO4 or K2Cr2O7 cleaves the alkene to ketones and/or acids. Ozonolysis (O3 addition, then Zn/H2O cleavage) splits the alkene into carbonyl fragments and is used to locate the double bond's position. Alkenes also polymerise -- ethene into polythene, propene into polypropene.","visual":"🔬"}],"questions":[{"kind":"mcq","prompt":"According to Markovnikov's rule, when HBr adds to an unsymmetrical alkene, where does the bromine (negative part) attach?","options":["To the carbon atom bearing the lesser number of hydrogen atoms","To the carbon atom bearing the greater number of hydrogen atoms","Always to the terminal carbon atom","Randomly, with no preference"],"answer":0,"explain":"Markovnikov's rule: the negative part of the addendum attaches to the carbon atom with fewer hydrogen atoms."},{"kind":"mcq","prompt":"What is the major product when propene reacts with HBr in the absence of peroxide?","options":["2-Bromopropane","1-Bromopropane","1,2-Dibromopropane","Propyl alcohol"],"answer":0,"explain":"H+ attacks first, forming the more stable secondary carbocation, which Br- then attacks to give 2-bromopropane (Markovnikov addition)."},{"kind":"mcq","prompt":"What is the major product when propene reacts with HBr in the presence of peroxide (the Kharash/peroxide effect)?","options":["1-Bromopropane","2-Bromopropane","1,1-Dibromopropane","Propyne"],"answer":0,"explain":"Peroxides trigger a free-radical mechanism through the more stable secondary radical, giving 1-bromopropane -- anti to Markovnikov's rule."},{"kind":"mcq","prompt":"Which catalyst, when used to partially reduce an alkyne with dihydrogen, gives an alkene with cis geometry?","options":["Lindlar's catalyst (palladised charcoal partly deactivated with sulphur compounds/quinoline)","Raney nickel","Plain platinum black with no poison","Sodium in liquid ammonia"],"answer":0,"explain":"Lindlar's catalyst partially deactivates palladised charcoal, stopping reduction at the cis-alkene stage; Na/liquid NH3 instead gives the trans-alkene."},{"kind":"numeric","prompt":"What is the approximate bond dissociation enthalpy of the H-Br bond, in kJ/mol?","answer":363.7,"explain":"H-Br bond enthalpy (363.7 kJ/mol) is weak enough for a free radical to cleave it, unlike the stronger H-Cl bond (430.5 kJ/mol) -- this is why the peroxide effect works only with HBr."}]},{"id":"hc8","title":"Triple Threat","icon":"⚔️","kind":"lesson","story":"A welder's torch roars to life, fed by acetylene gas -- the linear, triple-bonded ancestor of every alkyne. 'One sigma, two pi, one straight line,' the torch crackles. 'Prove you understand the strongest, shortest bond in this Atlas.'","teach":[{"title":"Ethyne: the first alkyne","body":"Alkynes have general formula CnH2n-2 and contain at least one C-C triple bond. Ethyne (acetylene), C2H2, is the first stable member, burned with oxygen as the oxyacetylene flame used in welding.","visual":"🔥"},{"title":"Structure of the triple bond","body":"Each carbon in ethyne is sp hybridised: one sp-sp sigma bond joins the carbons, two sp-1s sigma bonds join each carbon to hydrogen (giving a linear H-C-C angle of 180 degrees), and the two remaining unhybridised p orbitals on each carbon overlap sideways to form two pi bonds. The C-C triple bond is the shortest (120 pm, vs 133 pm for C=C and 154 pm for C-C) and strongest (823 kJ/mol) of the three.","visual":"📐"},{"title":"Industrial preparation","body":"Limestone is heated to quicklime (CaCO3 -> CaO + CO2), quicklime heated with coke gives calcium carbide (CaO + 3C -> CaC2 + CO), and calcium carbide reacts with water to give ethyne industrially: CaC2 + 2H2O -> Ca(OH)2 + C2H2.","visual":"⛏️"},{"title":"Acidic terminal hydrogens","body":"Because sp carbons have 50% s-character (the highest electronegativity among sp3, sp2 and sp carbons), they pull the shared C-H electron pair strongly, making the hydrogen on a terminal triple bond mildly acidic. Ethyne reacts with sodium or sodamide to give sodium/disodium acetylide and H2 -- a reaction alkenes and alkanes do not show, so it distinguishes terminal alkynes from them.","visual":"⚛️"}],"questions":[{"kind":"mcq","prompt":"What is the hybridisation of each carbon atom in ethyne (C2H2)?","options":["sp","sp2","sp3","sp3d"],"answer":0,"explain":"Each carbon in ethyne is sp hybridised, giving a linear molecule with 180 degree H-C-C bond angles."},{"kind":"numeric","prompt":"What is the C-C triple bond length in ethyne, in picometres (pm)?","answer":120,"explain":"The C≡C bond length (120 pm) is shorter than C=C (133 pm) and C-C (154 pm), and it is also the strongest of the three (823 kJ/mol)."},{"kind":"mcq","prompt":"What is the industrial method for preparing ethyne (acetylene)?","options":["Treating calcium carbide with water: CaC2 + 2H2O -> Ca(OH)2 + C2H2","Heating sodium acetate with soda lime","Electrolysing aqueous sodium acetate (Kolbe's method)","Passing methane over a nickel catalyst at high pressure"],"answer":0,"explain":"On an industrial scale, ethyne is prepared by treating calcium carbide (itself made from quicklime and coke) with water."},{"kind":"mcq","prompt":"Why is the hydrogen on a triply-bonded carbon in a terminal alkyne mildly acidic, unlike hydrogens in alkenes or alkanes?","options":["The sp-hybridised carbon has the highest electronegativity (50% s-character), pulling the C-H bond's electron pair strongly","Triple bonds are inherently unstable and release protons spontaneously","Alkynes contain an -OH group that ionises","The carbon-hydrogen bond in alkynes is longer, making it weaker"],"answer":0,"explain":"The sp carbon's high s-character (50%) gives it the greatest electronegativity of the three hybridisations, letting terminal alkyne hydrogens be liberated as protons more readily."},{"kind":"mcq","prompt":"Three molecules of ethyne, passed through a red-hot iron tube at 873 K, undergo cyclic polymerisation to form which compound?","options":["Benzene","Cyclohexane","Toluene","Naphthalene"],"answer":0,"explain":"This cyclic trimerisation of ethyne is the classic laboratory route from aliphatic to aromatic compounds, producing benzene."}]},{"id":"hc9","title":"Kekule's Dream","icon":"🐍","kind":"lesson","story":"You doze by a fire, and the atoms around you gambol into long twisting rows -- until one serpent seizes its own tail and whirls before your eyes. You wake with the answer Kekule once dreamed: benzene is a ring, and the ring holds a secret deeper than alternating bonds.","teach":[{"title":"A stable, puzzling ring","body":"Michael Faraday isolated benzene in 1825. Its formula, C6H6, suggested high unsaturation, yet benzene was unusually stable, formed a triozonide (implying three double bonds), and gave only one monosubstituted product -- proving all six carbons and hydrogens were equivalent. In 1865, August Kekule proposed a six-membered ring with alternating single and double bonds to explain this.","visual":"⬡"},{"title":"The ortho-disubstitution problem, solved by resonance","body":"A strict alternating-bond ring predicts two different 1,2-disubstituted benzenes, but only one is ever found. Kekule patched this with 'oscillating' double bonds; modern valence bond theory explains it properly as resonance -- benzene is a hybrid of the two Kekule structures, with the true structure represented by a circle inside the hexagon.","visual":"♻️"},{"title":"Delocalised pi electrons","body":"All six benzene carbons are sp2 hybridised: their sp2 orbitals form six C-C and six C-H sigma bonds in the ring's plane, while the remaining unhybridised p orbital on each carbon overlaps sideways with its neighbours, forming two doughnut-shaped clouds of six delocalised pi electrons above and below the ring. X-ray diffraction confirms benzene is planar with every C-C bond the same length, 139 pm -- between a single bond (154 pm) and a double bond (133 pm) -- which is exactly why benzene resists addition reactions and favours substitution instead.","visual":"🍩"},{"title":"Aromaticity and Huckel's rule","body":"A ring system is aromatic if it is (i) planar, (ii) has complete delocalisation of pi electrons around the ring, and (iii) contains (4n + 2) pi electrons for some integer n (Huckel's Rule). Benzene fits with n = 1, giving 6 delocalised pi electrons. Benzene can be made by cyclic polymerisation of ethyne, by decarboxylating sodium benzoate with soda lime, or by reducing phenol vapour over heated zinc dust.","visual":"💎"}],"questions":[{"kind":"numeric","prompt":"What is the C-C bond length in benzene, in picometres (pm)?","answer":139,"explain":"X-ray diffraction shows all six C-C bonds in benzene are 139 pm, intermediate between a single bond (154 pm) and double bond (133 pm)."},{"kind":"mcq","prompt":"Who first isolated benzene, in 1825?","options":["Michael Faraday","August Kekule","John Dalton","Antoine Lavoisier"],"answer":0,"explain":"Michael Faraday isolated benzene in 1825, decades before Kekule proposed its cyclic structure in 1865."},{"kind":"mcq","prompt":"Who proposed the cyclic structure of benzene with alternating single and double bonds, in 1865?","options":["August Kekule","Michael Faraday","Linus Pauling","Robert Mulliken"],"answer":0,"explain":"August Kekule proposed the hexagonal ring of six carbons with alternating single/double bonds in 1865."},{"kind":"numeric","prompt":"By Huckel's Rule, how many pi electrons does benzene's aromatic ring contain (4n + 2, with n = 1)?","answer":6,"explain":"With n = 1, (4n + 2) = 6, matching benzene's six delocalised pi electrons from the ring's three formal double bonds."},{"kind":"mcq","prompt":"Which of the following is one of the three necessary conditions for a ring system to be aromatic, per Huckel's Rule?","options":["The ring must be planar with complete delocalisation of pi electrons and contain (4n + 2) pi electrons","The ring must contain at least one nitrogen atom","The ring must have an odd number of carbon atoms","The ring must be non-planar to allow free rotation"],"answer":0,"explain":"Aromaticity requires planarity, complete pi-electron delocalisation around the ring, and (4n + 2) pi electrons (n = 0, 1, 2, ...)."}]},{"id":"hc10","title":"The Substitution Gauntlet","icon":"🛡️","kind":"lesson","story":"Five electrophiles line up to attack the aromatic ring -- a nitronium ion, a chlorine cation, a sulphur trioxide, an alkyl cation, an acylium ion. The ring's delocalised electrons brace themselves. 'We do not add,' the ring declares. 'We substitute, and we recover our aromaticity every time.'","teach":[{"title":"Five classic electrophilic substitutions","body":"Benzene reacts with conc. HNO3 + conc. H2SO4 (nitrating mixture) to give nitrobenzene; with X2 and a Lewis acid (FeCl3, FeBr3 or AlCl3) to give a haloarene; with fuming H2SO4 (oleum) to give benzenesulphonic acid (sulphonation); and, via Friedel-Crafts reactions with anhydrous AlCl3, with an alkyl halide to give an alkylbenzene, or with an acyl halide/anhydride to give an acylbenzene.","visual":"⚔️"},{"title":"The three-step mechanism","body":"Every electrophilic substitution (SE) proceeds by: (a) generating the electrophile -- e.g. the nitronium ion NO2+ forms when H2SO4 protonates HNO3 (H2SO4 acting as the acid, HNO3 as the base); (b) the electrophile attacks to form a resonance-stabilised sigma complex (arenium ion), where one ring carbon becomes sp3 hybridised and aromaticity is temporarily lost; (c) a base (AlCl4- or HSO4-) removes the proton from that sp3 carbon, restoring the aromatic sextet.","visual":"🔁"},{"title":"Directive influence of substituents","body":"Groups like -OH, -NH2, -OCH3, -CH3 and -C2H5 donate electron density into the ring (mainly by resonance) and direct incoming electrophiles to the ortho and para positions, activating the ring toward further substitution. Halogens are also ortho/para directors through resonance donation of a lone pair, but their strong -I effect makes them net deactivating overall.","visual":"🧭"},{"title":"Meta directors and carcinogenicity","body":"Groups like -NO2, -CN, -CHO, -COR, -COOH and -SO3H withdraw electron density strongly by resonance (-M effect), leaving the meta position comparatively electron-richer, so they direct incoming electrophiles meta and deactivate the ring. Separately, benzene and polynuclear hydrocarbons with more than two fused benzene rings -- formed by incomplete combustion of tobacco, coal or petroleum -- are toxic and carcinogenic, damaging DNA after entering the body.","visual":"☠️"}],"questions":[{"kind":"mcq","prompt":"What reagent mixture is used to nitrate benzene into nitrobenzene?","options":["Concentrated nitric acid and concentrated sulphuric acid (nitrating mixture)","Dilute nitric acid alone","Fuming sulphuric acid (oleum) alone","Sodium nitrite and hydrochloric acid"],"answer":0,"explain":"Heating benzene with a nitrating mixture of conc. HNO3 and conc. H2SO4 introduces a nitro group to give nitrobenzene."},{"kind":"mcq","prompt":"What electrophile actually attacks the benzene ring during nitration, and how is it formed?","options":["The nitronium ion, NO2+, formed when sulphuric acid protonates nitric acid","The nitrate ion, NO3-, formed by dissociation of nitric acid","Elemental nitrogen, N2, generated by reduction of HNO3","Ammonium ion, NH4+, from partial reduction of HNO3"],"answer":0,"explain":"H2SO4 protonates HNO3 in an acid-base equilibrium, generating the electrophilic nitronium ion, NO2+."},{"kind":"mcq","prompt":"What kind of directing and activating behaviour does a -OH group show on a benzene ring?","options":["Ortho/para-directing and ring-activating, since resonance increases electron density at o- and p- positions","Meta-directing and ring-deactivating","Ortho/para-directing but ring-deactivating","It has no directing effect at all"],"answer":0,"explain":"Resonance donation from -OH raises electron density mainly at ortho and para positions, activating the ring and directing incoming electrophiles there."},{"kind":"mcq","prompt":"What kind of directing and activating behaviour does a -NO2 group show on a benzene ring?","options":["Meta-directing and ring-deactivating, due to its strong -M (electron-withdrawing) effect","Ortho/para-directing and ring-activating","Meta-directing but ring-activating","It blocks all further substitution entirely"],"answer":0,"explain":"The nitro group withdraws electron density strongly by resonance, leaving the meta position relatively electron-rich, so it directs meta and deactivates the ring."},{"kind":"mcq","prompt":"Polynuclear aromatic hydrocarbons formed by incomplete combustion of tobacco, coal or petroleum are associated with which health effect?","options":["They are toxic and carcinogenic, damaging DNA after entering the body","They are harmless and simply pass through the body unchanged","They only cause mild skin irritation with no internal effects","They act as vitamins essential for healthy cell growth"],"answer":0,"explain":"Benzene and polynuclear hydrocarbons with more than two fused rings are toxic and carcinogenic, damaging DNA and causing cancer."}]},{"id":"hc-boss","title":"Duel with the Arene Sovereign","icon":"👑","kind":"boss","story":"At the summit of the Study Atlas, where the alchemist's gate first opened nine chapters ago, the Arene Sovereign awaits -- its crown forged from three fused benzene rings, its voice an echo of every alkane, alkene, alkyne and arene you have mastered. This is the final gate of Class 11 Chemistry.","boss":{"name":"The Arene Sovereign","taunt":"Nine chapters of chemistry lie broken behind you, traveller -- moles, bonds, equilibria, redox, and now the hydrocarbons themselves. I am the delocalised sum of every ring, chain and triple bond you have faced. Answer for alkanes, alkenes, alkynes and arenes all at once, or your journey through Class 11 Chemistry ends unfinished here!"},"teach":[{"title":"Review: bond length and strength trend","body":"As carbon-carbon bonds gain more shared electron pairs, they get shorter and stronger: C-C single bond (154 pm, 348 kJ/mol) > C=C double bond (133 pm, 681 kJ/mol) > C≡C triple bond (120 pm, 823 kJ/mol). Benzene's delocalised ring bonds (139 pm) sit between single and double bond lengths.","visual":"📐"},{"title":"Review: reaction type by hydrocarbon class","body":"Alkanes mainly undergo free-radical substitution (like halogenation) and combustion. Alkenes and alkynes, rich in loosely-held pi electrons, mainly undergo electrophilic addition (as in Markovnikov and anti-Markovnikov HBr addition). Aromatic rings resist addition and instead undergo electrophilic substitution, because substitution preserves the stabilising delocalised pi system that addition would destroy.","visual":"⚔️"},{"title":"Review: isomerism across the unit","body":"Alkanes show chain isomerism and, in ethane, conformational isomerism (staggered vs eclipsed, 12.5 kJ/mol apart). Alkenes add geometrical (cis-trans) isomerism, since rotation about C=C is restricted. Aromatic rings show ortho/meta/para positional isomerism when disubstituted.","visual":"🧩"},{"title":"Review: aromaticity and safety","body":"A ring is aromatic if it is planar, has fully delocalised pi electrons, and holds (4n+2) pi electrons (Huckel's Rule) -- benzene qualifies with 6. This same electron delocalisation that makes benzene so stable also makes polynuclear aromatic hydrocarbons, formed from incomplete combustion, dangerously persistent and carcinogenic.","visual":"👑"}],"questions":[{"kind":"mcq","prompt":"Ranking C-C bond length from longest to shortest across the unit, which order is correct?","options":["Single bond (154 pm) > double bond (133 pm) > triple bond (120 pm)","Triple bond (120 pm) > double bond (133 pm) > single bond (154 pm)","Double bond (133 pm) > single bond (154 pm) > triple bond (120 pm)","All carbon-carbon bonds have the same length"],"answer":0,"explain":"More shared electron pairs pull carbons closer together: single (154 pm) is longest, then double (133 pm), then triple (120 pm) is shortest."},{"kind":"mcq","prompt":"Why does benzene undergo electrophilic substitution rather than the electrophilic addition typical of alkenes and alkynes?","options":["Substitution lets the ring keep its stabilising delocalised pi-electron system, while addition would destroy the aromatic sextet","Benzene has no pi electrons at all, so addition is impossible","Benzene's ring carbons are sp3 hybridised, like alkanes","Electrophiles cannot approach a planar ring system"],"answer":0,"explain":"Because delocalisation of the 6 pi electrons makes benzene unusually stable, reactions that restore this aromatic system (substitution) are strongly favoured over addition."},{"kind":"numeric","prompt":"What is the approximate energy barrier, in kJ/mol, between the staggered and eclipsed conformations of ethane?","answer":12.5,"explain":"This small barrier (about 12.5 kJ/mol) is easily overcome by thermal collisions, so ethane's C-C bond rotation is nearly free."},{"kind":"mcq","prompt":"What is the major product of propene reacting with HBr in the absence of peroxide, and which rule predicts it?","options":["2-Bromopropane, predicted by Markovnikov's rule (Br- attaches to the carbon with fewer H atoms via the more stable carbocation)","1-Bromopropane, predicted by Markovnikov's rule","2-Bromopropane, predicted by the peroxide (Kharash) effect","Propa-1,2-diene, predicted by Huckel's rule"],"answer":0,"explain":"Without peroxide, H+ forms the more stable secondary carbocation, and Br- attacks it to give 2-bromopropane, the Markovnikov product."},{"kind":"mcq","prompt":"According to Huckel's Rule, a planar ring is aromatic if it has how many pi electrons in general form?","options":["(4n + 2) pi electrons, where n = 0, 1, 2, ...","Exactly 4n pi electrons","Any even number of pi electrons","Exactly 2 pi electrons, regardless of ring size"],"answer":0,"explain":"Huckel's Rule requires (4n + 2) delocalised pi electrons in a planar ring; benzene satisfies this with n = 1, giving 6 pi electrons."},{"kind":"numeric","prompt":"How many chain isomers does C6H14 have?","answer":5,"explain":"C6H14 has five chain isomers: n-hexane, 2-methylpentane, 3-methylpentane, 2,3-dimethylbutane and 2,2-dimethylbutane."},{"kind":"mcq","prompt":"What health hazard is associated with polynuclear aromatic hydrocarbons formed from incomplete combustion of tobacco, coal or petroleum?","options":["They are toxic and carcinogenic, damaging DNA","They act as antioxidants that protect DNA","They are inert and pass through the body without effect","They only affect plants, not animals"],"answer":0,"explain":"Polynuclear hydrocarbons with more than two fused benzene rings are toxic and cancer-producing, damaging DNA after entering the body."},{"kind":"numeric","prompt":"What is the C-C bond length in benzene, in picometres (pm), intermediate between a single and double bond?","answer":139,"explain":"Benzene's uniform C-C bond length of 139 pm (between 154 pm single and 133 pm double) is direct evidence for delocalised bonding, not alternating single/double bonds."}]}]}];